(116, 101, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/ZIygVy-ClTw?si=L6umQiSOfh3MIkTY\",\"esdescription\":\"El Templo de Zeus Ol\\u00edmpico, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como el Olimpeion, es un majestuoso templo ubicado en Atenas, Grecia. Aunque su construcci\\u00f3n comenz\\u00f3 en el siglo VI a. C., no se complet\\u00f3 hasta el reinado del emperador Adriano en el siglo II. Durante las \\u00e9pocas helen\\u00edstica y romana, este templo fue el m\\u00e1s grande de toda Grecia.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles sobre el Templo de Zeus Ol\\u00edmpico:\\r\\n- Ubicaci\\u00f3n: Se encuentra a unos 500 metros al sureste de la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas y a unos 700 metros al sur de la Plaza Sintagma (el centro de Atenas).\\r\\n\\r\\n- Historia: Sus cimientos fueron colocados en el sitio de un primer templo por el tirano Pis\\u00edstrato en 515 a. C., pero el trabajo se abandon\\u00f3 cuando su hijo, Hipias, fue derrocado en 510 a. C. Durante los a\\u00f1os de la democracia ateniense, el templo qued\\u00f3 incompleto debido a la creencia de que construirlo a tal escala era un acto de hybris (exceso de orgullo). El trabajo se reanud\\u00f3 en 174 a. C. bajo el patrocinio del rey helen\\u00edstico Ant\\u00edoco IV Ep\\u00edfanes, quien contrat\\u00f3 al arquitecto romano Cosucio para dise\\u00f1ar el templo m\\u00e1s grande del mundo conocido, sin embargo, despu\\u00e9s de la muerte de Ant\\u00edoco en 164 a. C., el trabajo se retras\\u00f3 nuevamente.\\tEn el siglo II, el emperador Adriano, admirador de la cultura griega, retom\\u00f3 el proyecto y finalmente lo complet\\u00f3 en 129 d. C. (algunas fuentes dicen que en 131 d. C.).\\r\\n\\r\\n- Destino posterior: No se sabe cu\\u00e1ndo fue destruido el edificio, pero como muchos edificios grandes de Grecia, es probable que haya ocurrido debido a un terremoto en la Edad Media.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Estructura: El emperador Adriano dedic\\u00f3 el templo al dios Zeus Ol\\u00edmpico y tambi\\u00e9n erigi\\u00f3 una enorme estatua de marfil de Zeus en la cella del templo. \\r\\nEn las \\u00e9pocas helen\\u00edstica y romana fue el mayor templo de Grecia. El templo se construy\\u00f3 con m\\u00e1rmol del monte Pent\\u00e9lico.\\r\\nAunque el majestuoso templo original ya no existe, los restos que a\\u00fan se conservan en pie evocan su colosal tama\\u00f1o y la importancia que tuvo en la antig\\u00fcedad. Gran parte de sus ruinas se utilizaron como materiales de construcci\\u00f3n a lo largo del tiempo, a\\u00fan se pueden ver diecis\\u00e9is columnas sobrevivientes. Una de estas columnas y fragmentos del entablamento se encuentran en el lugar. En el siglo II, el templo influy\\u00f3 en el desarrollo del estilo corintio en Roma.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u00a1Explorar este impresionante monumento hist\\u00f3rico, es un testimonio asombroso de la antigua arquitectura griega, no te pierdas la oportunidad de explorar estas majestuosas columnas corintias que se alzan hacia el cielo en el lugar donde una vez se erigi\\u00f3 el Templo de Zeus Ol\\u00edmpico!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1uKRvrUAIppe48FPLwNUHVEsbTwan9Qgk\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YpU_SZAFbHosNWdaRNVfAE1sn6zw0yYo\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xDBckfFgzeF7w5LrM7ZomNMc94wTFHEc\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_pGnLIlXfBKEQoxVSzUaw0ube7YEgUHv\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/17CiBKTlM4e4C7oqqdZ5JUeMY7D4fynvr\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-u3Bu_gClvIrAzorxR34sBJLi-oakfY9\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 14:28:17', '2024-04-02 11:32:19'),
(117, 38, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1R2f4q7mGPNq9-3sksIr-SHLusiqld4S9\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Temple of Olympian Zeus, also known as the Olimpeion, is a majestic temple located in Athens, Greece. Although its construction began in the 6th century BC, it was not completed until the reign of Emperor Hadrian in the 2nd century. During the Hellenistic and Roman eras, this temple was the largest in all of Greece.\\r\\nHere are some details about the Temple of Olympian Zeus:\\r\\n- Location: It is located about 500 meters southeast of the Acropolis of Athens and about 700 meters south of Syntagma Square (the center of Athens).\\r\\n\\r\\n- History: Its foundations were laid on the site of a first temple by the tyrant Pisistratus in 515 B.C., but work was abandoned when his son, Hipias, was overthrown in 510 B.C. During the years of Athenian democracy, the temple was left incomplete due to the belief that building it on such a scale was an act of hybris (excessive pride). Work resumed in 174 BC under the patronage of the Hellenistic king Antiochus IV Epiphanes, who hired the Roman architect Cosutius to design the largest temple in the known world, however, after Antiochus\' death in 164 BC, work was again delayed. In the second century, the emperor Hadrian, an admirer of Greek culture, took up the project and finally completed it in 129 AD (some sources say 131 AD).\\r\\n\\r\\n- Subsequent Fate: It is not known when the building was destroyed, but like many large buildings in Greece, it is likely to have occurred due to an earthquake in the Middle Ages.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Structure: Emperor Hadrian dedicated the temple to the god Olympian Zeus and also erected a huge ivory statue of Zeus in the temple cella. \\r\\nIn Hellenistic and Roman times it was the largest temple in Greece. The temple was built with marble from Mount Pentelicus.\\r\\nAlthough the original majestic temple no longer exists, the remains still standing evoke its colossal size and the importance it had in antiquity. Much of its ruins were used as building materials over time, sixteen surviving columns can still be seen. One of these columns and fragments of the entablature are found on the site. In the 2nd century, the temple influenced the development of the Corinthian style in Rome.\\r\\n\\r\\nExploring this impressive historical monument, an amazing testament to ancient Greek architecture, don\'t miss the opportunity to explore these majestic Corinthian columns soaring skyward on the site where the Temple of Olympian Zeus once stood!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-u3Bu_gClvIrAzorxR34sBJLi-oakfY9\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1uKRvrUAIppe48FPLwNUHVEsbTwan9Qgk\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YpU_SZAFbHosNWdaRNVfAE1sn6zw0yYo\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xDBckfFgzeF7w5LrM7ZomNMc94wTFHEc\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_pGnLIlXfBKEQoxVSzUaw0ube7YEgUHv\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/17CiBKTlM4e4C7oqqdZ5JUeMY7D4fynvr\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 14:34:19', '2024-03-19 14:34:19'),
(118, 102, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1gXUXqI_AEeHxP2cAfJiyoDuIAN4kBJ0G\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"Panathinaik\\u00f3 Stadium, also known as the Kallim\\u00e1rmaro (Greek \\u039a\\u03b1\\u03bb\\u03bb\\u03bb\\u03b9\\u03bc\\u03ac\\u03c1\\u03bc\\u03b1\\u03c1\\u03bf, i.e. the \\\"beautiful marble\\\"), is an athletics stadium in Athens, Greece. This majestic stadium hosted the first edition of the Modern Olympic Games in Athens 1896.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis historic stadium has a rich history and is remarkable for several reasons:\\r\\n- Ancient origin: In ancient times, it was used to host the athletic part of the Panathenaean Games, in honor of the goddess Athena. During classical times, the stadium had wooden seats.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Marble reconstruction: It was rebuilt in marble by Archon Lycurgus in 329 BC and then enlarged and renovated by Herodes Atticus in 140 AD, reaching a seating capacity of 50,000.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Olympic Revival: The remains of the ancient structure were excavated and restored thanks to funds provided by Evangelis Zappas, who sponsored the Olympic Games held in 1870 and 1875. The stadium was renovated again in 1895 for the 1896 Olympic Games, with additional funding provided by Greek benefactor George Averoff. Architects Anastasios Metaxas and Ernst Ziller contributed to the design.\\r\\n\\r\\n- White marble construction: the Panathinaik\\u00f3 Stadium is the only major stadium in the world built entirely of white marble, quarried from Mount Pentelicus.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Design and location: The stadium was built long before the dimensions of athletics venues were standardized. Previously, it could accommodate some 80,000 spectators in its 50 rows of marble stands. Today, it seats 45,000 spectators. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Location: Its location in the center of Athens, near the National Garden and the Zappeion, and not far from the Parthenon makes it a place of great historical interest. It is also adjacent to other notable sites such as the Olympic Temple of Zeus and Hadrian\'s Gate.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kOHnTEaZ9g6n6CfPnsJGliHxZXO8v3Bp\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1PW385nxVS0yt8acIUKiBZyJ72Ydw6WVR\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-Jz0VSP5gEohrudrxsF-Ud_GIKmtDM68\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Ri7V19Qf8Be32LIwWDb_FHngp8BV-ZIV\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1b0kKOkMX3qyefuQOoqIr6msyMshA0dhZ\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_wMpxDPaCBYHcQ0aQN78lK8RWz5L8TPy\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 15:08:58', '2024-03-19 15:08:58'),
(119, 39, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/fnHcPrp6BLQ?si=gXelmPbfBEFV3T2k\",\"esdescription\":\"El Estadio Panathinaik\\u00f3, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como el Kallim\\u00e1rmaro (en griego \\u039a\\u03b1\\u03bb\\u03bb\\u03b9\\u03bc\\u03ac\\u03c1\\u03bc\\u03b1\\u03c1\\u03bf, es decir, el \\u201cm\\u00e1rmol hermoso\\u201d), es un estadio de atletismo en Atenas, Grecia. Este majestuoso estadio acogi\\u00f3 la primera edici\\u00f3n de los Juegos Ol\\u00edmpicos Modernos en Atenas 1896.\\r\\n\\r\\nEste hist\\u00f3rico estadio tiene una rica historia y es notable por varias razones:\\r\\n- Origen antiguo: En tiempos antiguos, se utilizaba para albergar la parte atl\\u00e9tica de los Juegos de las Panateneas, en honor a la diosa Atenea. Durante la \\u00e9poca cl\\u00e1sica, el estadio ten\\u00eda asientos de madera.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Reconstrucci\\u00f3n en m\\u00e1rmol: Fue reconstruido en m\\u00e1rmol por el Arconte Licurgo en el a\\u00f1o 329 a.C. y luego ampliado y renovado por Herodes \\u00c1tico en el a\\u00f1o 140 d.C., alcanzando una capacidad de 50,000 asientos.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Renacimiento ol\\u00edmpico: Los restos de la antigua estructura fueron excavados y restaurados gracias a los fondos proporcionados por Evangelis Zappas, quien patrocin\\u00f3 los Juegos Ol\\u00edmpicos celebrados en 1870 y 1875. El estadio fue renovado nuevamente en 1895 para los Juegos Ol\\u00edmpicos de 1896, con financiamiento adicional proporcionado por el benefactor griego George Averoff. Los arquitectos Anastasios Metaxas y Ernst Ziller contribuyeron al dise\\u00f1o.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Construcci\\u00f3n en m\\u00e1rmol blanco: El Estadio Panathinaik\\u00f3 es el \\u00fanico estadio importante en el mundo construido enteramente de m\\u00e1rmol blanco, extra\\u00eddo del Monte Pent\\u00e9lico.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Dise\\u00f1o y ubicaci\\u00f3n: El estadio fue construido mucho antes de que las dimensiones de los lugares de atletismo fueran estandarizadas. Anteriormente, pod\\u00eda albergar a unos 80,000 espectadores en sus 50 filas de gradas de m\\u00e1rmol. Actualmente, tiene capacidad para 45,000 espectadores. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Ubicaci\\u00f3n: Su ubicaci\\u00f3n en el centro de Atenas, cerca del Jard\\u00edn Nacional y el Z\\u00e1ppeion, y no lejos del Parten\\u00f3n lo convierte en un lugar de gran inter\\u00e9s hist\\u00f3rico. Adem\\u00e1s, est\\u00e1 adyacente a otros sitios notables como el Templo Ol\\u00edmpico de Zeus y la Puerta de Adriano.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kOHnTEaZ9g6n6CfPnsJGliHxZXO8v3Bp\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1PW385nxVS0yt8acIUKiBZyJ72Ydw6WVR\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-Jz0VSP5gEohrudrxsF-Ud_GIKmtDM68\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Ri7V19Qf8Be32LIwWDb_FHngp8BV-ZIV\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1b0kKOkMX3qyefuQOoqIr6msyMshA0dhZ\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_wMpxDPaCBYHcQ0aQN78lK8RWz5L8TPy\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 15:09:07', '2024-04-02 11:06:39'),
(120, 108, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/JQVKuX6xnec?si=dH8xjJNvERWBoS7M\",\"esdescription\":\"La Estoa de \\u00c1talo, tambi\\u00e9n conocida simplemente como \\u00c1talo, es un p\\u00f3rtico helen\\u00edstico situado en la parte oriental del \\u00c1gora de Atenas. Este majestuoso monumento fue construido por \\u00c1talo II Filadelfio durante su reinado en P\\u00e9rgamo (159 a 138 a. C.). El p\\u00f3rtico fue un regalo a la polis de Atenas como agradecimiento por la educaci\\u00f3n que hab\\u00eda recibido en ella antes de convertirse en rey.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles sobre la Estoa de \\u00c1talo:\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n: se encuentra en el centro de Atenas, en la zona del \\u00c1gora. Puedes llegar a pie desde la Acr\\u00f3polis, as\\u00ed como desde la plaza Monastiraki, est\\u00e1 cerca de otras maravillas arqueol\\u00f3gicas como el Parten\\u00f3n y el P\\u00f3rtico de Atalo.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Construcci\\u00f3n y origen: Fue erigido por \\u00c1talo II Filadelfio, durante su reinado en P\\u00e9rgamo (159 a 138 a. C.). Como agradecimiento por la educaci\\u00f3n que recibi\\u00f3 en Atenas antes de convertirse en rey, \\u00c1talo II regal\\u00f3 este monumento a la polis ateniense.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Arquitectura: Este edificio de grandes dimensiones mide 112,09 metros de largo por 19,40 metros de ancho. Tiene dos niveles: una planta baja d\\u00f3rica y un piso superior j\\u00f3nico, conectados por escaleras en cada extremo. Las paredes est\\u00e1n hechas de piedra caliza del Pireo, con una fachada de m\\u00e1rmol del Pent\\u00e9lico y una cubierta de tejas. En la antig\\u00fcedad, albergaba 21 locales en cada planta, que eran alquilados por el Estado ateniense. Adem\\u00e1s de ser un centro comercial, tambi\\u00e9n serv\\u00eda como lugar de encuentro y discusi\\u00f3n para los atenienses, protegi\\u00e9ndolos del sol en verano y del fr\\u00edo en invierno.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Restauraci\\u00f3n y Uso Actual: La Estoa de \\u00c1talo fue destruida por los h\\u00e9rulos en el a\\u00f1o 267, pero sus restos se incorporaron a la nueva muralla de la ciudad en \\u00e9poca romana tard\\u00eda. Sin embargo, desde su reconstrucci\\u00f3n entre 1953 y 1956 por la Escuela Americana de Estudios Cl\\u00e1sicos de Atenas, alberga el Museo del \\u00c1gora de Atenas. El museo consta de almacenes en el s\\u00f3tano, una zona de exposici\\u00f3n en la planta baja y salas de trabajo en el primer piso. Algunas partes del edificio original se conservan, lo que permite a los visitantes comprobar la validez de la restauraci\\u00f3n.\\r\\n\\r\\nLa Estoa de \\u00c1talo es un testimonio impresionante de la riqueza hist\\u00f3rica y arquitect\\u00f3nica de la antigua Atenas, no dejes de explorar este magn\\u00edfico monumento y sumergirte en su historia y belleza.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1VatHiXvU__cdngvU5AmX_mu_bGIEelvq\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1hnL7Muz35d8hFv2uX8PtdNidjzJYwFD1\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L82n8PC0b8y-mG8INs473mW-lte9r1gl\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1bhxTDu4mrW9MAP68o9EgL7YGm82VvRqo\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YVUcJzaNJ7mFhJyJfULqI5bbzp5SSp2x\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Sw8d7mjXC--dl4kW83z7tf1Zqx5GvR_W\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 18:32:44', '2024-04-02 11:02:28'),
(121, 43, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/18aTXIOoRK-i3Qry35ncKjH9X6BBhmGGG\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Stoa of Attalus, also known simply as Attalus, is a Hellenistic portico located in the eastern part of the Agora of Athens. This majestic monument was built by Attalus II Philadelphus during his reign in Pergamum (159 to 138 BC). The portico was a gift to the polis of Athens as a thank you for the education he had received there before becoming king.\\r\\nHere are some details about the Stoa of Attalus:\\r\\n1. Location: it is located in the center of Athens, in the Agora area. You can reach it on foot from the Acropolis, as well as from Monastiraki Square, it is close to other archaeological wonders such as the Parthenon and the Portico of Attalus.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Construction and origin: It was erected by Attalus II Philadelphus, during his reign in Pergamum (159 to 138 BC). In gratitude for the education he received in Athens before becoming king, Attalus II gave this monument to the Athenian polis.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Architecture: This large building measures 112.09 meters long by 19.40 meters wide. It has two levels: a Doric first floor and an Ionic upper floor, connected by stairs at each end. The walls are made of Piraean limestone, with a Pentelic marble fa\\u00e7ade and a tiled roof. In ancient times, it housed 21 shops on each floor, which were rented by the Athenian state. In addition to being a commercial center, it also served as a meeting and discussion place for the Athenians, protecting them from the sun in summer and the cold in winter.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Restoration and Present Use: The Attalus Stoa was destroyed by the Heruli in 267, but its remains were incorporated into the new city wall in the late Roman period. However, since its reconstruction between 1953 and 1956 by the American School of Classical Studies in Athens, it houses the Museum of the Agora of Athens. The museum consists of storage rooms in the basement, an exhibition area on the first floor and working rooms on the second floor. Some parts of the original building are preserved, allowing visitors to check the validity of the restoration.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Attalus Stoa is an impressive testimony to the historical and architectural richness of ancient Athens, be sure to explore this magnificent monument and immerse yourself in its history and beauty.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1VatHiXvU__cdngvU5AmX_mu_bGIEelvq\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1hnL7Muz35d8hFv2uX8PtdNidjzJYwFD1\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L82n8PC0b8y-mG8INs473mW-lte9r1gl\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1bhxTDu4mrW9MAP68o9EgL7YGm82VvRqo\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YVUcJzaNJ7mFhJyJfULqI5bbzp5SSp2x\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Sw8d7mjXC--dl4kW83z7tf1Zqx5GvR_W\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 18:32:54', '2024-03-19 18:32:54'),
(122, 204, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/qI3YcCbR9rM?si=D_6NyzWSvR0cg9tC\",\"esdescription\":\"Tiatira, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Thy\\u00e1tira o Ci\\u00e1tira (si se pronuncia de acuerdo con la pronunciaci\\u00f3n del espa\\u00f1ol europeo), fue una antigua ciudad en Asia Menor, ubicada en los l\\u00edmites de Lidia y Misia, en lo que hoy es Turqu\\u00eda. Esta antigua ciudad es especialmente famosa por su industria gremial tintorera, como se menciona en los Hechos de los Ap\\u00f3stoles y en inscripciones relacionadas con el gremio de tintoreros.\\r\\nAunque su historia es rica y diversa, aqu\\u00ed hay algunos aspectos destacados:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Origen hist\\u00f3rico: Tiatira fue una ciudad lidia, situada cerca del r\\u00edo Lico, en la parte norte de Lidia, pero tan pr\\u00f3xima a Misia que algunos la consideraban \\\"el extremo de Misia\\\". Durante la \\u00e9poca helen\\u00edstica, fue refundada por Seleuco Nicator con una colonia de macedonios, convirti\\u00e9ndose en un n\\u00facleo helen\\u00edstico en Asia Menor occidental.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Industria gremial: Tiatira era famosa por sus gremios artesanales, y uno de los m\\u00e1s importantes era el de los tintoreros. Estos tintoreros conoc\\u00edan el secreto de la tintura de p\\u00farpura con ra\\u00edz de rubia, en contraste con otros centros productores de p\\u00farpura que utilizaban crust\\u00e1ceos.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Templo y profetisa: La leyenda de Tiatira menciona un templo dedicado a la deidad Sambate, donde una profetisa daba or\\u00e1culos. Algunos analistas del Nuevo Testamento han relacionado esto con las palabras de Juan Evangelista en el Apocalipsis, mencionando a una mujer llamada Jezabel que se dec\\u00eda profetisa.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Batallas y fortuna: Tiatira tuvo suerte en momentos cruciales. En el a\\u00f1o 190 a.C., durante la lucha entre Ant\\u00edoco III el Grande y Roma, la ciudad no sufri\\u00f3 da\\u00f1os debido a que la batalla se libr\\u00f3 en otro lugar. Lo mismo ocurri\\u00f3 en el a\\u00f1o 366, cuando el emperador romano Valente derrot\\u00f3 a un usurpador cerca de la ciudad.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Actualmente: Hoy en d\\u00eda, la antigua Tiatira est\\u00e1 ubicada dentro de la moderna ciudad de Akhisar, aproximadamente a 40 millas al sureste de P\\u00e9rgamo y en el camino hacia Sardis. Aunque la arqueolog\\u00eda ha revelado poco sobre Tiatira en el per\\u00edodo del Nuevo Testamento, su historia sigue siendo fascinante y llena de misterio.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wVfDfFwlOihBq8BaKDygK8fy48nfoQDn\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kmCj81w4tsbrylWAysR3Lm6af9KonNUd\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L6cc84qP4fGsJdwYQ_g0PlJ6dxqE6VgD\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_PWH9hp_I4r-Uely7TVm0SdoB4pnV-Aq\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xPqmWGy7EEQAJEfROD2FgE_3Rydrajw_\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aSyyodYmi7ezeJZ2h1_xGQUxfXM0fZI9\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 20:17:58', '2024-04-02 11:26:25'),
(123, 205, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ROaE8mVlpPYFr4JJEbmFxl8yXHluh0yO\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"Thyatira, also known as Thyatira or Ciatira (if pronounced according to the European English pronunciation), was an ancient city in Asia Minor, located on the borders of Lydia and Mysia, in what is now Turkey. This ancient city is especially famous for its dyeing guild industry, as mentioned in the Acts of the Apostles and in inscriptions related to the dyers\' guild.\\r\\nAlthough its history is rich and diverse, here are some highlights:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. historical origin: Thyatira was a Lydian city, located near the Lycian River in the northern part of Lydia, but so close to Mysia that some considered it \\\"the end of Mysia\\\". During the Hellenistic period, it was refounded by Seleucus Nicator with a colony of Macedonians, becoming a Hellenistic nucleus in western Asia Minor.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Guild industry: Thyatira was famous for its craft guilds, and one of the most important was that of the dyers. These dyers knew the secret of dyeing purple with blonde root, in contrast to other purple-producing centers that used crustaceans.\\r\\n\\r\\nTemple and prophetess: The legend of Thyatira mentions a temple dedicated to the deity Sambate, where a prophetess gave oracles. Some analysts of the New Testament have related this with the words of John the Evangelist in the Apocalypse, mentioning a woman called Jezebel who called herself a prophetess.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Battles and fortune: Thyatira was lucky at crucial moments. In 190 B.C., during the struggle between Antiochus III the Great and Rome, the city was not damaged because the battle was fought elsewhere. The same happened in 366, when the Roman emperor Valens defeated a usurper near the city.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Today: Today, ancient Thyatira is located within the modern city of Akhisar, approximately 40 miles southeast of Pergamon and on the road to Sardis. Although archaeology has revealed little about Thyatira in the New Testament period, its history remains fascinating and full of mystery.\\r\\n\\r\\nToday, the ruins of Thyatira are buried beneath the modern city of Akhisar in Turkey, known for its carpet making industry.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wVfDfFwlOihBq8BaKDygK8fy48nfoQDn\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kmCj81w4tsbrylWAysR3Lm6af9KonNUd\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L6cc84qP4fGsJdwYQ_g0PlJ6dxqE6VgD\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_PWH9hp_I4r-Uely7TVm0SdoB4pnV-Aq\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xPqmWGy7EEQAJEfROD2FgE_3Rydrajw_\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aSyyodYmi7ezeJZ2h1_xGQUxfXM0fZI9\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 20:18:48', '2024-03-19 20:18:48'),
(124, 354, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/LxmBcXyMNrY?si=Slz-16GQYp29KRnP\",\"esdescription\":\"La Mezquita de Santa Sof\\u00eda, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Ayasofya en turco (en griego: \\u1f09\\u03b3\\u03af\\u03b1 \\u03a3\\u03bf\\u03c6\\u03af\\u03b1, en lat\\u00edn: Sancta Sophia), es un sitio cultural e hist\\u00f3rico notable ubicado en Estambul, Turqu\\u00eda. Perm\\u00edteme compartir algunos detalles fascinantes sobre esta ic\\u00f3nica estructura:\\r\\nSu historia es fascinante y ha pasado por diversas transformaciones a lo largo de los siglos:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Construcci\\u00f3n: La Hagia Sophia fue construida por el emperador bizantino Justiniano I entre los a\\u00f1os 532 y 537 d.C. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Dise\\u00f1adores: Fue dise\\u00f1ada por los ge\\u00f3metras griegos Isidoro de Mileto y Antemio de Tralles.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Espacio Interior: Al completarse, se convirti\\u00f3 en el espacio interior m\\u00e1s grande del mundo y fue uno de los primeros en emplear una c\\u00fapula completamente pendiente.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Caracter\\u00edsticas Arquitect\\u00f3nicas: Santa Sof\\u00eda se destaca por su enorme c\\u00fapula, cuatro minaretes, arcos grandiosos y ornamentaci\\u00f3n que incluye m\\u00e1rmoles, mosaicos y caligraf\\u00eda.\\r\\n\\r\\nA lo largo de los siglos, ha servido como lugar de culto, centro religioso, museo y s\\u00edmbolo cultural.\\r\\n1. Bas\\u00edlica Cristiana: Inaugurada en el a\\u00f1o 537, Inicialmente Santa Sof\\u00eda sirvi\\u00f3 como la catedral ortodoxa bizantina de rito oriental de Constantinopla. Durante este per\\u00edodo, fue un importante centro religioso y arquitect\\u00f3nico.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Catedral Cat\\u00f3lica: Entre 1204 y 1261, durante el patriarcado latino de Constantinopla, fue reconvertida en catedral cat\\u00f3lica de rito latino debido a la ocupaci\\u00f3n de la ciudad por los cruzados.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Mezquita Otomana: Tras la Conquista de Constantinopla por el Imperio otomano en 1453, Santa Sof\\u00eda se transform\\u00f3 en una mezquita. Durante casi 500 a\\u00f1os, manteniendo esta funci\\u00f3n hasta 1931.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Museo: En 1935, se inaugur\\u00f3 como museo, permitiendo a visitantes de todo el mundo apreciar su impresionante arquitectura y rica historia.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WCfU_NpQCcjPbcEHOid5PmXVGpYqaPji\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-JQNWArYPpm8z2js-nNF4H9LMVeOgX2D\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Mqlv5Oc1qtQAxumADSqgQnxape_3nmNi\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1RNJxUIAQoQcthwFyI6GKqxEHalhzkAvE\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/11l6WslyuC3WJktlIqKPB8-JphZ6JqChz\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1MtEwrkKyPGafaAHYhGtgFNh0MJuB4QkG\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 21:17:24', '2024-04-01 13:53:09'),
(125, 216, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/tEICh4--Tus?si=6BLQAqmSOYO5rnus\",\"esdescription\":\"The Hagia Sophia Mosque, also known as Ayasofya in Turkish (Greek: \\u1f09\\u03b3\\u03af\\u03b1 \\u03a3\\u03bf\\u03c6\\u03af\\u03b1, Latin: Sancta Sophia), is a remarkable cultural and historical site located in Istanbul, Turkey. Let me share some fascinating details about this iconic structure:\\r\\nIts history is fascinating and has gone through several transformations over the centuries:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Construction: The Hagia Sophia was built by the Byzantine Emperor Justinian I between 532 and 537 AD. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Designers: It was designed by the Greek geometers Isidore of Miletus and Anthemius of Tralles.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Interior Space: Upon completion, it became the largest interior space in the world and was one of the first to employ a fully pitched dome.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Architectural Features: Hagia Sophia is noted for its enormous dome, four minarets, grandiose arches and ornamentation that includes marble, mosaics and calligraphy.\\r\\n\\r\\nOver the centuries, it has served as a place of worship, religious center, museum and cultural symbol.\\r\\nChristian Basilica: Inaugurated in 537, Hagia Sophia initially served as the Eastern Rite Byzantine Orthodox Cathedral of Constantinople. During this period, it was an important religious and architectural center.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Catholic Cathedral: Between 1204 and 1261, during the Latin Patriarchate of Constantinople, it was converted into a Catholic cathedral of Latin rite due to the occupation of the city by the Crusaders.\\r\\n\\r\\nOttoman Mosque: After the conquest of Constantinople by the Ottoman Empire in 1453, Hagia Sophia was transformed into a mosque. For nearly 500 years, maintaining this function until 1931.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Museum: In 1935, it was inaugurated as a museum, allowing visitors from all over the world to appreciate its impressive architecture and rich history. \\r\\n\\r\\n5. New Transformation: On August 1, 2020, Hagia Sophia was again converted into a mosque by the decision of President Recep Tayyip Erdo\\u011fan12.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WCfU_NpQCcjPbcEHOid5PmXVGpYqaPji\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-JQNWArYPpm8z2js-nNF4H9LMVeOgX2D\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Mqlv5Oc1qtQAxumADSqgQnxape_3nmNi\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1RNJxUIAQoQcthwFyI6GKqxEHalhzkAvE\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/11l6WslyuC3WJktlIqKPB8-JphZ6JqChz\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1MtEwrkKyPGafaAHYhGtgFNh0MJuB4QkG\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-19 21:17:43', '2024-04-01 13:50:35'),
(126, 41, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YePPszqHuoiYKh_4xsRbCLtSXPd7oFvp\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Agora of Athens, also known as the Ancient Agora, was the nerve center of political, administrative, commercial and social activity, its religious and cultural focus, and the place where justice was dispensed in the ancient city of Athens. It was a large rectangular open space, flanked by an accumulation of public buildings.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis space displayed a variety of important functions:\\r\\n1. center of government: \\r\\n- In the Agora, Athenians met to discuss laws and make political decisions. Here, oratory and the art of convincing were fundamental.\\r\\n\\r\\n- The philosophy of Socrates and the Platonic dialogues left a lasting impression on our way of thinking, challenging the democracy of the Agora that relied on skilled orators and demagogues.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Until the reforms of Cl\\u00edstenes, the Agora was the meeting point of the Ekkles\\u00eda (Assembly).\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Sacred Precinct:\\r\\n- The Agora housed numerous shrines dedicated to the Olympian gods, such as Hephaestus, Zeus and Apollo.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Judicial Seat:\\r\\n- This was where the courts were located, where trials were held. It was in the Agora that Socrates was condemned to death for corrupting the youth and introducing new gods.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Market:\\r\\n- The vitality of the Agora was boosted by the Via Panathenaica, the main street of Athens, which was sometimes the central stage for processions during the Panathenaean festivals.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Origins:\\r\\n- The Agora area has been continuously occupied throughout the city\'s history.\\r\\n\\r\\n- From the Neolithic, through the Bronze and Iron Ages, to the Archaic Period, the Agora has witnessed settlements, cemeteries and dwellings.\\r\\n\\r\\n- In the Archaic Agora, located northeast of the Acropolis, the political, economic and social life of the Athenians developed.\\r\\n\\r\\nYou must explore the majestic Agora and learn about the root of Western civilization!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1DNs_zsnomM07JRiJkCuGCj3fIUjp3nbY\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1VuzHw7Nmu67JnBUK5z9hIjr3XO3hVX7-\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IAw65fcx-Pads5PyVByDjaMB60g5KFe7\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CAR2o04qDDMPwo7Ec0pn_AUgarQUlkhW\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UJoPUx9OAFASCcPQJW9cKHlfthOKlqcu\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wMbVwSJBrFUBVepwnR_QTGCr57gODT53\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 15:17:29', '2024-03-22 15:17:29'),
(127, 105, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-J3PmrrhV4z78_uqA9JjvMiJLpzMresU\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"El \\u00c1gora de Atenas, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como el \\u00c1gora Antigua, fue el centro neur\\u00e1lgico de la actividad pol\\u00edtica, administrativa, comercial y social, su foco religioso y cultural, y el lugar donde se impart\\u00eda justicia en la antigua ciudad de Atenas. Se trataba de un amplio espacio abierto rectangular, flanqueado por una acumulaci\\u00f3n de edificios p\\u00fablicos.\\r\\n\\r\\nEste espacio desplegaba una variedad de funciones importantes:\\r\\n1. Centro de Gobierno: \\r\\n- En el \\u00c1gora, los atenienses se reun\\u00edan para discutir leyes y tomar decisiones pol\\u00edticas. Aqu\\u00ed, la oratoria y el arte de convencer eran fundamentales.\\r\\n\\r\\n- La filosof\\u00eda de S\\u00f3crates y los di\\u00e1logos plat\\u00f3nicos dejaron una huella duradera en nuestra forma de pensar, cuestionando la democracia del \\u00c1gora que confiaba en h\\u00e1biles oradores y demagogos.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Hasta las reformas de Cl\\u00edstenes, el \\u00c1gora era el punto de encuentro de la Ekkles\\u00eda (Asamblea).\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Recinto Sagrado:\\r\\n- El \\u00c1gora albergaba numerosos santuarios dedicados a los dioses ol\\u00edmpicos, como Hefesto, Zeus y Apolo.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Sede Judicial:\\r\\n- Aqu\\u00ed se encontraban los tribunales, donde se celebraban juicios. Fue en el \\u00c1gora donde se conden\\u00f3 a S\\u00f3crates a muerte por corromper a los j\\u00f3venes e introducir nuevos dioses.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Mercado:\\r\\n- La vitalidad del \\u00c1gora se ve\\u00eda impulsada por la V\\u00eda Panatenaica, la principal calle de Atenas, que en ocasiones era el escenario central de las procesiones durante las fiestas de las Panateneas.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Or\\u00edgenes:\\r\\n- La zona del \\u00c1gora ha estado ocupada de manera continua a lo largo de la historia de la ciudad.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Desde el Neol\\u00edtico, pasando por las Edades del Bronce y del Hierro, hasta la \\u00c9poca Arcaica, el \\u00c1gora ha sido testigo de asentamientos, cementerios y viviendas.\\r\\n\\r\\n- En el \\u00c1gora Arcaica, situado al noreste de la Acr\\u00f3polis, se desarroll\\u00f3 la vida pol\\u00edtica, econ\\u00f3mica y social de los atenienses.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1DNs_zsnomM07JRiJkCuGCj3fIUjp3nbY\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1VuzHw7Nmu67JnBUK5z9hIjr3XO3hVX7-\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IAw65fcx-Pads5PyVByDjaMB60g5KFe7\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CAR2o04qDDMPwo7Ec0pn_AUgarQUlkhW\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UJoPUx9OAFASCcPQJW9cKHlfthOKlqcu\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wMbVwSJBrFUBVepwnR_QTGCr57gODT53\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 15:18:00', '2024-03-22 15:18:00'),
(128, 106, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/ldxqXatck90?si=iQ8EtG8p1AckLNNR\",\"esdescription\":\"El Templo de Hefesto, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como el Hefesti\\u00f3n, este majestuoso templo est\\u00e1 dedicado al dios del fuego, Hefesto, y a su esposa, Atenea donde se les rend\\u00eda culto. Es uno de los tesoros arqueol\\u00f3gicos m\\u00e1s antiguos y mejor conservados de Grecia, debido en parte al hecho de que Atenas no se encuentra en una zona de terremotos frecuentes por otra parte, por el hecho de que el templo fue transformado en iglesia cristiana.\\r\\n\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles sobre este impresionante monumento:\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n: El Templo de Hefesto se encuentra en la cima de la colina llamada Colonos Agoreo, en el coraz\\u00f3n de la antigua Atenas. Aunque no es tan famoso como el Parten\\u00f3n en la Acr\\u00f3polis, su belleza e impacto son innegables.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Arquitectura: Construido en el per\\u00edodo 460-420 a. C., este templo sigue el orden d\\u00f3rico. Sus frisos est\\u00e1n adornados con escenas de la mitolog\\u00eda griega, como la Centauromaquia. Adem\\u00e1s, la estructura del templo est\\u00e1 adornada con una riqueza de ornamentos esculpidos con relieves de bronce que a\\u00fan son visibles en la actualidad. Estos detalles art\\u00edsticos nos transportan a la antigua Atenas y su brillantez cl\\u00e1sica\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Significado: Hefesto era el dios de la metalurgia, mientras que Atenea Ergane era la diosa de la cer\\u00e1mica griega y la artesan\\u00eda. Dada la proximidad de talleres de ceramistas y trabajo de metales, se cree que el templo estaba dedicado a estas divinidades. A veces tambi\\u00e9n se le llama Teseion, debido a la creencia bizantina de que los huesos del legendario h\\u00e9roe griego Teseo estaban enterrados all\\u00ed. Sin embargo, los restos de Teseo fueron sepultados en otro lugar de la Acr\\u00f3polis. \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Historia de la Construcci\\u00f3n: La construcci\\u00f3n del Templo de Hefesto comenz\\u00f3 en el a\\u00f1o 449 a. C., pero no se complet\\u00f3 hasta el 415 a. C. Su dise\\u00f1o refleja la riqueza tradicional de Atenas, con detalles como el uso del orden d\\u00f3rico y las esculturas en metopas y frontones. Aunque fue una iglesia cristiana desde el siglo VII hasta 1834, a lo largo de los siglos, ha servido como templo, iglesia cristiana y monumento hist\\u00f3rico.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Estado de conservaci\\u00f3n: A diferencia de muchos otros edificios antiguos, su legado como un antiguo templo griego perdura hasta hoy, el Templo de Hefesto ha resistido admirablemente el paso del tiempo. En la actualidad, se puede ver casi completamente reconstruido, lo que permite a los visitantes apreciar su esplendor original.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u00a1Este templo es un testimonio impresionante de la habilidad y la visi\\u00f3n arquitect\\u00f3nica de la antigua Grecia, recomendamos encarecidamente explorar el \\u00c1gora Antigua y maravillarte ante la majestuosidad del Templo de Hefesto!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1LCgES0qRP9mhPdPFAoWfIjCLkspNQiZB\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ALsrxPyK7PyTur4Tl8SSTkkiH_mhCV8B\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-MYZbqn32OnzuIlHIockKTY2f04Zc4Tw\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1vNd6ttaDTmUANypsbWK9q7GCXs7DwnRj\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wYcQ5pLyxOadFHKNUTjSVf5StIkbuN4b\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YqNEHKlerMJ5muwOuKvDC5-1ZGGnr04k\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 15:33:07', '2024-04-02 11:34:39'),
(129, 42, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-k_qiYezAuZGfD5BE0sDu63uSOqbkFMS\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Temple of Hephaestus, also known as the Hephaestion, this majestic temple is dedicated to the god of fire, Hephaestus, and his wife, Athena where they were worshipped. It is one of the oldest and best preserved archaeological treasures in Greece, due in part to the fact that Athens is not in a frequent earthquake zone and in part to the fact that the temple was transformed into a Christian church.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere are some details about this impressive monument:\\r\\n1. Location: The Temple of Hephaestus is located on the top of the hill called Colonos Agoreo, in the heart of ancient Athens. Although it is not as famous as the Parthenon on the Acropolis, its beauty and impact are undeniable.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Architecture: Built in the period 460-420 B.C., this temple follows the Doric order. Its friezes are adorned with scenes from Greek mythology, such as the Centauromaquia. In addition, the structure of the temple is adorned with a wealth of ornaments sculpted with bronze reliefs that are still visible today. These artistic details transport us to ancient Athens and its classical brilliance.\\r\\n\\r\\nSignificance: Hephaestus was the god of metallurgy, while Athena Ergane was the goddess of Greek pottery and craftsmanship. Given the proximity of potters\' workshops and metalworking, it is believed that the temple was dedicated to these divinities. It is also sometimes called Theseion, due to the Byzantine belief that the bones of the legendary Greek hero Theseus were buried there. However, the remains of Theseus were buried elsewhere on the Acropolis. \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Construction History: Construction of the Temple of Hephaestus began in 449 B.C., but it was not completed until 415 B.C. Its design reflects the traditional richness of Athens, with details such as the use of the Doric order and sculptures on metopes and pediments. Although it was a Christian church from the 7th century until 1834, over the centuries, it has served as a temple, Christian church and historical monument.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. State of preservation: Unlike many other ancient buildings, its legacy as an ancient Greek temple endures to this day, the Temple of Hephaestus has stood the test of time admirably. Today, it can be seen almost completely reconstructed, allowing visitors to appreciate its original splendor.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis temple is a stunning testament to the skill and architectural vision of ancient Greece, we highly recommend exploring the Ancient Agora and marveling at the majesty of the Temple of Hephaestus!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1LCgES0qRP9mhPdPFAoWfIjCLkspNQiZB\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ALsrxPyK7PyTur4Tl8SSTkkiH_mhCV8B\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-MYZbqn32OnzuIlHIockKTY2f04Zc4Tw\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1vNd6ttaDTmUANypsbWK9q7GCXs7DwnRj\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wYcQ5pLyxOadFHKNUTjSVf5StIkbuN4b\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YqNEHKlerMJ5muwOuKvDC5-1ZGGnr04k\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 15:36:35', '2024-03-22 15:36:35'),
(130, 112, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/We1sBNJyROI?si=rkzWVv3X0C4HMwbw\",\"esdescription\":\"La isla de Egina (en griego antiguo: \\u0391\\u1f34\\u03b3\\u03b9\\u03bd\\u03b1, A\\u00edgina; en griego moderno: \\u0391\\u03af\\u03b3\\u03b9\\u03bd\\u03b1, \\u00c9yina) es una de las islas de Grecia situada en medio del golfo Sar\\u00f3nico, entre las islas de Salamina al norte, Angistri al oeste y Poros al sur. Se encuentra a una decena de kil\\u00f3metros de la costa noreste de la pen\\u00ednsula de Methano, en el Peloponeso, y al sudoeste del Pireo, puerto del cual dista unos 20 km. El gentilicio de los habitantes de esta isla es egineta, es un destino fascinante con una rica historia y una belleza natural impresionante.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Historia: Egina fue una de las primeras ciudades mar\\u00edtimas y comerciantes de la Grecia Antigua: tuvo la primera marina de Grecia y fue la primera ciudad en acu\\u00f1ar moneda. Adem\\u00e1s, fue la primera capital (1828-1829) de Grecia que luchaba por su independencia, y el joven Estado griego hizo acu\\u00f1ar all\\u00ed sus primeras monedas. \\r\\nSeg\\u00fan la tradici\\u00f3n, se considera que su nombre deriva de la ninfa Egina, madre de \\u00c9aco, quien naci\\u00f3 en la isla y fue su rey.\\r\\n\\r\\n-  Geograf\\u00eda: La isla tiene una superficie de alrededor de 90 km\\u00b2 y est\\u00e1 situada a una veintena de kil\\u00f3metros al sudoeste de Atenas.\\r\\nSu forma es triangular, con una longitud de 18,5 km desde el cabo Tourla al noreste hasta el cabo Perdika al sureste.\\r\\nSe divide en tres regiones: un macizo monta\\u00f1oso de marga calc\\u00e1rea al norte, una llanura costera aluvial al oeste (el \\u00fanico lugar f\\u00e9rtil de la isla) y el resto de la isla es de traquita volc\\u00e1nica.\\r\\nLa isla es tambi\\u00e9n conocida por sus pumitas, que fueron una vez una de las principales exportaciones.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Lugares de inter\\u00e9s: Entre los sitios m\\u00e1s hist\\u00f3ricos y pintorescos tenemos el Pueblo de Egina, explora este pintoresco pueblo, donde encontrar\\u00e1s:\\r\\n1. El Templo de Apolo (tambi\\u00e9n conocido como Kolona), que data del siglo VI a.C. y se construy\\u00f3 en un sitio prehist\\u00f3rico.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. La Torre de Markellos, construida como torre de vigilancia contra los otomanos en el siglo XVII o principios del XVIII.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Los Edificios Kapodistrianos, que datan del siglo XIX y fueron parte de la breve \\u00e9poca en que Egina fue la capital de la Grecia moderna tras la independencia.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Monumentos \\u00fanicos como: \\r\\n- Templo de Afaya: La isla es c\\u00e9lebre por su templo de Afaya, y forma un tri\\u00e1ngulo imaginario uno de los tres templos del tri\\u00e1ngulo sagrado formado por el Parten\\u00f3n, el Templo de Poseid\\u00f3n del cabo de Suni\\u00f3n, y el templo de Afaya. Fue durante mucho tiempo una gran rival de Atenas, tanto en la Antig\\u00fcedad como al principio del siglo XIX. Data de entre el 500 y el 490 a.C. \\r\\n- Drakospita: Casas prehist\\u00f3ricas de m\\u00e1s de 3,000 a\\u00f1os de antig\\u00fcedad cerca de Sfentouri.\\r\\n- Ruinas del Templo de Ellanios Zeus: Considerado el templo m\\u00e1s antiguo de Europa, anterior a la Guerra de Troya por tres generaciones.\\r\\n- La costa: Sigue el camino de los artistas y disfruta de las playas y la luz inspiradora de Egina.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Cultura y gastronom\\u00eda: La isla es la principal productora de pistachos de Grecia y la mayor exportadora mundial, prueba los famosos pistachos y mariscos de Egina, productos emblem\\u00e1ticos de la isla. \\r\\n\\r\\n\\u00a1La isla de Egina es un tesoro griego con una rica historia y belleza natural\\u00a1\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1nSrmlr-PFM4ufeBsoPF5D_k3ufGZFlpn\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13GNAQggj13GbDh3ilZ8zx46YFOH0HMIG\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aTd4Js5ATsMe8lE2pz_0nnBcUSFzszMo\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/14qZCRNSOMvLE4amvK09G4J0qMLS0NJDc\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1dyniiFNnfmKYISZLxf2FIz_N4i9JYDyL\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1pgcWsfvOW2TdqC0zr_v9CaRP-D8sYKj0\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 15:50:52', '2024-04-01 14:06:00');
INSERT INTO `sight_media` (`id`, `sight_id`, `media_link`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(131, 46, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/gTCc99goDVE?si=jCB_TmJXmz9C6RYi\",\"esdescription\":\"The island of Aegina (Ancient Greek: \\u0391\\u1f34\\u03b3\\u03b9\\u03bd\\u03b1, A\\u00edgina; Modern Greek: \\u0391\\u03af\\u03b3\\u03b9\\u03bd\\u03b1, \\u00c9yina) is one of the islands of Greece located in the middle of the Saronic Gulf, between the islands of Salamis to the north, Angistri to the west and Poros to the south. It is located about ten kilometers off the northeast coast of the Methano peninsula in the Peloponnese, and southwest of Piraeus, a port from which it is about 20 km away. A fascinating destination with a rich history and breathtaking natural beauty, Aegina is a fascinating destination with a rich history and breathtaking natural beauty.\\r\\n\\r\\n- History: Aegina was one of the first maritime and trading cities of Ancient Greece: it had the first navy of Greece and was the first city to mint coins. It was also the first capital (1828-1829) of Greece struggling for independence, and the young Greek state had its first coins minted there. \\r\\nAccording to tradition, its name is considered to derive from the nymph Aegina, mother of Aeacus, who was born on the island and was its king.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Geography: The island has an area of about 90 km\\u00b2 and is located about twenty kilometers southwest of Athens.\\r\\nIt is triangular in shape, with a length of 18.5 km from Cape Tourla in the northeast to Cape Perdika in the southeast.\\r\\nIt is divided into three regions: a mountainous massif of calcareous marl to the north, an alluvial coastal plain to the west (the only fertile part of the island) and the rest of the island is volcanic trachyte.\\r\\nThe island is also known for its pumice, which was once a major export.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Places of interest: Among the most historical and picturesque sites we have Aegina Village, explore this picturesque village, where you will find:\\r\\n1. The Temple of Apollo (also known as Kolona), which dates back to the 6th century BC and was built on a prehistoric site.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. The Markellos Tower, built as a watchtower against the Ottomans in the 17th or early 18th century.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. The Kapodistrian Buildings, which date from the 19th century and were part of the brief period when Aegina was the capital of modern Greece after independence.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Unique monuments such as: \\r\\n- Temple of Afaya: The island is famous for its temple of Afaya, and forms an imaginary triangle one of the three temples of the sacred triangle formed by the Parthenon, the Temple of Poseidon of Cape Sunion, and the temple of Afaya. It was for a long time a great rival of Athens, both in antiquity and at the beginning of the 19th century. It dates from between 500 and 490 BC. \\r\\n- Drakospita: Prehistoric houses more than 3,000 years old near Sfentouri.\\r\\n- Ruins of the Temple of Ellanios Zeus: Considered the oldest temple in Europe, predating the Trojan War by three generations.\\r\\n- The coast: Follow the path of the artists and enjoy the beaches and the inspiring light of Aegina.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Culture and gastronomy: The island is the main producer of pistachios in Greece and the world\'s largest exporter, try the famous Aegina pistachios and seafood, emblematic products of the island. \\r\\n\\r\\nThe island of Aegina is a Greek treasure with a rich history and natural beauty.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1nSrmlr-PFM4ufeBsoPF5D_k3ufGZFlpn\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13GNAQggj13GbDh3ilZ8zx46YFOH0HMIG\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aTd4Js5ATsMe8lE2pz_0nnBcUSFzszMo\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/14qZCRNSOMvLE4amvK09G4J0qMLS0NJDc\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1dyniiFNnfmKYISZLxf2FIz_N4i9JYDyL\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1pgcWsfvOW2TdqC0zr_v9CaRP-D8sYKj0\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 15:52:51', '2024-04-01 14:02:52'),
(132, 151, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1E08FYKcX12piDWvUFMKpcoFsUa3eZENR\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The ancient city of Ephesus, also known as Ephesos in Greek and Ephes in Turkish, is a historical treasure located on the west coast of Turkey, an ancient city of Asia Minor.\\r\\nThis Hellenistic city, located on the shores of the Aegean Sea, has a fascinating history:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. foundation and Legends: Ephesus was founded around the 10th century BC and was one of the twelve Ionian cities on the shores of the Aegean Sea. Its history is full of myths and legends, such as the one that attributes its foundation to Ionian colonists led by Androclus, son of the Athenian King Codro. These colonists expelled the L\\u00e9leges and Carians who inhabited the region. \\r\\nAnother legend suggests that the Amazons were also involved in its foundation. In addition, the sanctuary of Artemis at Ephesus has its own legend, attributing its foundation to a son of the river-god Castro named Ephesus.\\r\\nOver the centuries, Ephesus was dominated by different civilizations, including Cimmerians, Persians, Romans, Goths and Arabs.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Location: Ephesus is located in the province of Izmir, on the west coast of Turkey.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Architecture and Monuments:\\r\\n- The temple of Artemis at Ephesus is one of the seven wonders of the ancient world. Although only one column survives, it originally had 120 columns 20 meters high.\\r\\n- The Library of Celsus, with its impressive facade, is another outstanding monument in Ephesus.\\r\\n- The ruins of Ephesus include cobblestone streets, amphitheaters, baths, luxury houses and public baths.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Importance and Ruins: Ephesus was an important religious, cultural and commercial center in ancient times. Its ruins, which include the Library of Celsus, the Great Theater, and the Temple of Artemis, are an impressive tourist attraction. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Economy and Tourism: Although in ancient times Ephesus was a vital port city, its harbor was abandoned due to processes of sedimentation and erosion. Today, Ephesus\' economy is based mainly on tourism, which attracts visitors to its remarkable Greco-Roman ruins and Paleo-Christian and Byzantine remains.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Gentilicio: The inhabitants of Ephesus were called Ephesians.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. World Heritage Site: Ephesus has been inscribed by UNESCO as a World Heritage Site since 2015.\\r\\n\\r\\nToday, its ruins attract visitors from all over the world and are a major tourist attraction. In short, Ephesus is a living testimony to the rich history, impressive architecture and cultural influence of this ancient Greek and Roman city It\'s a trip back in time you won\'t want to miss!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1tnb3KAmSz1zshayQSGe62LtB8FY5n2gl\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1olHAj6fTTs_X3JrQj9nwfJByireT8JMh\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WWXDTR_qQtAHC4j9YU_hDqnb56AcYBtq\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lG_vP2oHfk0cMW_I9LLm5d7iCe589-Dj\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1DR7L1lbWYRniH3l5OEWwe2-hVhtBDoYe\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1icDCnFXf5f0nQDR_yaV-IfN5GrpGE6RS\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 16:07:00', '2024-03-22 16:07:00'),
(133, 150, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/896xKMoyyR0?si=IoBV5rRLLdEVDvq1\",\"esdescription\":\"La ciudad antigua de \\u00c9feso, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Ephesos en griego y Efes en turco, es un tesoro hist\\u00f3rico ubicado en la costa oeste de Turqu\\u00eda, una antigua ciudad de Asia Menor.\\r\\nEsta ciudad helen\\u00edstica, situada a orillas del mar Egeo, tiene una historia fascinante:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Fundaci\\u00f3n y Leyendas: \\u00c9feso se fund\\u00f3 alrededor del siglo X a.C. y fue una de las doce ciudades j\\u00f3nicas a orillas del mar Egeo. Su historia est\\u00e1 llena de mitos y leyendas, como la que atribuye su fundaci\\u00f3n a colonos jonios liderados por Androclo, hijo del rey ateniense Codro. Estos colonos expulsaron a los l\\u00e9leges y carios que habitaban la regi\\u00f3n. \\r\\nOtra leyenda sugiere que las Amazonas tambi\\u00e9n estuvieron involucradas en su fundaci\\u00f3n. Adem\\u00e1s, el santuario de Artemisa en \\u00c9feso tiene su propia leyenda, atribuyendo su fundaci\\u00f3n a un hijo del dios-r\\u00edo Ca\\u00edstro llamado \\u00c9feso.\\r\\nA lo largo de los siglos, \\u00c9feso fue dominada por diferentes civilizaciones, incluyendo cimerios, persas, romanos, godos y \\u00e1rabes.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Ubicaci\\u00f3n: \\u00c9feso se encuentra en la provincia de Izmir, en la costa oeste de Turqu\\u00eda.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Arquitectura y Monumentos:\\r\\n- El templo de Artemisa en \\u00c9feso es una de las siete maravillas del mundo antiguo. Aunque solo una columna sobrevive, originalmente ten\\u00eda 120 columnas de 20 metros de altura.\\r\\n- La Biblioteca de Celso, con su impresionante fachada, es otro destacado monumento en \\u00c9feso.\\r\\n- Las ruinas de \\u00c9feso incluyen calles empedradas, anfiteatros, termas, casas de lujo y ba\\u00f1os p\\u00fablicos.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Importancia y Ruinas: \\u00c9feso fue un importante centro religioso, cultural y comercial en la antig\\u00fcedad. Sus ruinas, que incluyen la Biblioteca de Celso, el Gran Teatro, y el Templo de Artemisa, son una atracci\\u00f3n tur\\u00edstica impresionante. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Econom\\u00eda y Turismo: Aunque en la antig\\u00fcedad \\u00c9feso fue una ciudad portuaria vital, su puerto fue abandonado debido a procesos de sedimentaci\\u00f3n y erosi\\u00f3n. Hoy en d\\u00eda, la econom\\u00eda de \\u00c9feso se basa principalmente en el turismo, que atrae a visitantes a sus notables ruinas grecorromanas y restos paleocristianos y bizantinos.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Gentilicio: Los habitantes de \\u00c9feso se llamaban efesios.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Patrimonio de la Humanidad: \\u00c9feso ha sido inscrito por la UNESCO como Patrimonio de la Humanidad desde 2015.\\r\\n\\r\\nActualmente, sus ruinas atraen a visitantes de todo el mundo y constituyen una atracci\\u00f3n tur\\u00edstica importante. En resumen, \\u00c9feso es un testimonio vivo de la rica historia, la arquitectura impresionante y la influencia cultural de esta antigua ciudad griega y romana \\u00a1Es un viaje al pasado que no querr\\u00e1s perderte!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1tnb3KAmSz1zshayQSGe62LtB8FY5n2gl\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1olHAj6fTTs_X3JrQj9nwfJByireT8JMh\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WWXDTR_qQtAHC4j9YU_hDqnb56AcYBtq\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lG_vP2oHfk0cMW_I9LLm5d7iCe589-Dj\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1DR7L1lbWYRniH3l5OEWwe2-hVhtBDoYe\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1icDCnFXf5f0nQDR_yaV-IfN5GrpGE6RS\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 16:07:34', '2024-04-02 10:45:11'),
(134, 173, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/4IOU6DKaA1Y?si=4eEO1aam8MyboTbQ\",\"esdescription\":\"La Casa de la Virgen Mar\\u00eda, conocida como Meryemana en turco, es un lugar de gran significado religioso y espiritual.  \\r\\nAqu\\u00ed est\\u00e1n algunas caracter\\u00edsticas notables de este lugar:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Ubicaci\\u00f3n y Creencias: La Casa de la Virgen Mar\\u00eda se encuentra en un parque natural cerca de la antigua ciudad de \\u00c9feso, a unos siete kil\\u00f3metros de la actual poblaci\\u00f3n de Sel\\u00e7uk, en la provincia de Esmirna, al occidente de Turqu\\u00eda. Seg\\u00fan la tradici\\u00f3n y las creencias de muchas personas, Mar\\u00eda, la madre de Jes\\u00fas, pas\\u00f3 los \\u00faltimos a\\u00f1os de su vida aqu\\u00ed. Sin embargo, al igual que la historia de San Juan, hay incertidumbres sobre esta ubicaci\\u00f3n.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Historia y Dudas: La creencia de que Santa Mar\\u00eda vivi\\u00f3 en \\u00c9feso se basa en d\\u00e9biles indicios. No se menciona este hecho en la Biblia, y la evidencia arqueol\\u00f3gica es limitada. Aunque la carta sinodal del Concilio de \\u00c9feso en el a\\u00f1o 431 hace referencia a la ciudad donde Juan el Te\\u00f3logo y la Virgen Madre de Dios Santa Mar\\u00eda vivieron y est\\u00e1n enterrados, hay mejores pruebas de que su residencia permanente estaba en Jerusal\\u00e9n.\\r\\nEste lugar es considerado sagrado tanto para cristianos como para musulmanes, ya que se dice que fue la \\u00faltima residencia de la Virgen Mar\\u00eda. Seg\\u00fan la tradici\\u00f3n cristiana, Mar\\u00eda lleg\\u00f3 a \\u00c9feso acompa\\u00f1ada por el ap\\u00f3stol Juan despu\\u00e9s de la resurrecci\\u00f3n de Jes\\u00fas, y vivi\\u00f3 all\\u00ed sus \\u00faltimos d\\u00edas. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Estructura: La casa es una edificaci\\u00f3n peque\\u00f1a, con forma de letra \\u2018T\\u2019. Est\\u00e1 compuesta por una habitaci\\u00f3n y una cocina. El interior es simple y carece de decoraciones ostentosas. En su interior, se pueden encontrar algunas im\\u00e1genes de la Virgen Mar\\u00eda y varias velas.\\r\\nEl altar principal est\\u00e1 a cargo de los Padres Lazaristas, y all\\u00ed se celebra la misa todos los d\\u00edas. Uno de los d\\u00edas m\\u00e1s concurridos es el 15 de agosto, que coincide con la Asunci\\u00f3n de la Virgen. Adem\\u00e1s, se cree que el manantial que fluye debajo de la Casa de la Virgen tiene propiedades curativas, y muchas personas aseguran haber experimentado milagros debido a \\u00e9l. Dentro de la casa, se encuentran muletas y bastones dejados por aquellos que fueron sanados por el manantial. Tambi\\u00e9n hay varios lugares donde el agua brota y se puede beber en el mismo sitio.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Restauraci\\u00f3n y Visitas: La casa fue restaurada en la segunda mitad del siglo pasado y se conserva hasta hoy. Varios papas, incluido Benedicto XVI, la han visitado. Aunque modesta, su forma recuerda a una iglesia con c\\u00fapula. Una estatua de Mar\\u00eda est\\u00e1 ubicada frente a la entrada.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, la Casa de la Virgen Mar\\u00eda es un lugar de peregrinaci\\u00f3n y reflexi\\u00f3n, donde los visitantes pueden conectarse con la historia y la espiritualidad asociadas con la madre de Jes\\u00fas.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/14T9ppqVSR_WtWrvxAYde_tlxh6MsTc7j\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1y8OdT9bqRfV098w50EDCJ7w7qUveSXww\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kjYbpNUJxGwUUdlPcLB9peHvkriLDioD\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WByTVizXNmvReE4qpn5SA-B2s9B8MYOn\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WkZD2d2XdzgGUiHX46Fr2fYiBXLM6Xqa\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1dieaZbccgvzkvtLwHxHIJk6aLkR-mbMm\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 16:46:42', '2024-04-01 14:14:09'),
(135, 174, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/S09C7eTeKZg?si=jOMldkAVtDhypGWH\",\"esdescription\":\"The House of the Virgin Mary, known as Meryemana in Turkish, is a place of great religious and spiritual significance.  \\r\\nHere are some notable features of this place:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Location and Beliefs: The House of the Virgin Mary is located in a natural park near the ancient city of Ephesus, about seven kilometers from the present-day town of Sel\\u00e7uk, in the province of Smyrna, western Turkey. According to tradition and the beliefs of many people, Mary, the mother of Jesus, spent the last years of her life here. However, like the story of St. John, there are uncertainties about this location.\\r\\n\\r\\n- History and Doubts: The belief that St. Mary lived in Ephesus is based on weak indications. There is no mention of this fact in the Bible, and archaeological evidence is limited. Although the synodal letter of the Council of Ephesus in 431 refers to the city where John the Theologian and the Virgin Mother of God St. Mary lived and are buried, there is better evidence that their permanent residence was in Jerusalem.\\r\\nThis place is considered sacred to both Christians and Muslims, as it is said to have been the last residence of the Virgin Mary. According to Christian tradition, Mary arrived in Ephesus accompanied by the apostle John after the resurrection of Jesus, and lived there her last days. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Structure: The house is a small building, in the shape of a letter \'T\'. It consists of one room and a kitchen. The interior is simple and lacks ostentatious decorations. Inside, there are some images of the Virgin Mary and several candles.\\r\\nThe main altar is in charge of the Lazarist Fathers, and mass is celebrated there every day. One of the busiest days is August 15, which coincides with the Assumption of the Virgin. In addition, the spring that flows under the House of the Virgin is believed to have healing properties, and many people claim to have experienced miracles because of it. Inside the house, there are crutches and walking sticks left by those who were healed by the spring. There are also several places where water gushes and can be drunk on site.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Restoration and Visits: The house was restored in the second half of the last century and is preserved to this day. Several popes, including Benedict XVI, have visited it. Although modest, its shape is reminiscent of a domed church. A statue of Mary is located in front of the entrance.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, the House of the Virgin Mary is a place of pilgrimage and reflection, where visitors can connect with the history and spirituality associated with the mother of Jesus.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/14T9ppqVSR_WtWrvxAYde_tlxh6MsTc7j\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1y8OdT9bqRfV098w50EDCJ7w7qUveSXww\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kjYbpNUJxGwUUdlPcLB9peHvkriLDioD\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WByTVizXNmvReE4qpn5SA-B2s9B8MYOn\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WkZD2d2XdzgGUiHX46Fr2fYiBXLM6Xqa\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1dieaZbccgvzkvtLwHxHIJk6aLkR-mbMm\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-22 16:47:37', '2024-04-01 14:12:24'),
(136, 341, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/etdFIq1TEtQ?si=UgyewszSvjvjhHyt\",\"esdescription\":\"El Are\\u00f3pago, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como la Colina de Ares, es un monte situado al oeste de la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas. Su nombre proviene de la combinaci\\u00f3n de \\u201cAres\\u201d (el dios de la guerra) y \\u201cp\\u00e1gos\\u201d (que significa \\u201ccolina\\u201d en griego). Geol\\u00f3gicamente, esta colina es un enorme monolito de m\\u00e1rmol gris azulado, veteado de rojo, y mide 115 metros de altura. \\r\\nLeyendas y Significado:\\r\\n- Seg\\u00fan la leyenda, el Are\\u00f3pago recibi\\u00f3 su nombre porque Ares fue juzgado all\\u00ed por los dioses y exonerado de ser ajusticiado por dar muerte a Halirrotio, hijo de Poseid\\u00f3n, quien hab\\u00eda violado a una hija de Ares llamada Alcipe.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Tambi\\u00e9n se dice que en este lugar fue juzgado Orestes por el asesinato de su madre, Clitemnestra.\\r\\n\\r\\nEl Consejo del Are\\u00f3pago:\\r\\n1. Fue sede del Consejo que all\\u00ed se reuni\\u00f3 desde el 480 a. C. hasta el 425 d. C.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. En sus inicios, el Consejo del Are\\u00f3pago estaba compuesto exclusivamente por eup\\u00e1tridas y depend\\u00eda del rey. Con el tiempo, su influencia creci\\u00f3 y, tras las reformas de Sol\\u00f3n, sus miembros eran escogidos entre los arcontes (magistrados).\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Este tribunal ten\\u00eda la funci\\u00f3n de controlar a los magistrados, interpretar las leyes y juzgar a los homicidas. Aunque sus poderes pol\\u00edticos disminuyeron con el tiempo, mantuvieron su relevancia hasta las Guerras M\\u00e9dicas.\\r\\n\\r\\nSan Pablo en el Are\\u00f3pago:\\r\\n- Seg\\u00fan el registro b\\u00edblico en Hechos 17:16-18 y 22, el ap\\u00f3stol Pablo pronunci\\u00f3 un discurso en el Are\\u00f3pago. All\\u00ed, fil\\u00f3sofos epic\\u00fareos y estoicos lo llevaron para que explicara su ense\\u00f1anza \\u201cextranjera\\u201d. Pablo razon\\u00f3 con los habitantes de Atenas, quienes estaban rodeados de \\u00eddolos.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn la actualidad es un enorme monolito de m\\u00e1rmol gris azulado, veteado de rojo. Desde su cima y laderas, se pueden observar cortes en la roca que forman plataformas, que son los \\u00fanicos restos de antiguo lugar de reuniones.\\r\\n\\r\\nEl Are\\u00f3pago es un lugar cargado de historia y significado, y su majestuosidad sigue siendo impresionante hasta el d\\u00eda de hoy.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/15rm6UDg1t42V19jwvhrjZ8QObJkUt2KJ\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1D4We7ecQHfejnQNrn1PuV7uFkeJal-oZ\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qJuUdLWPvhEtWfUmRNRb9wZ1zc-L7EHt\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1olDzN4HZF45hgA1Tlsx8rtJNALZZmrii\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TPKcGy3e4Ri1StjgcftuJ_EWSxHbY34c\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ktjfIy4JZybK-jIHZFHGcRgkfgH08y3n\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 09:45:28', '2024-04-02 10:46:54'),
(137, 37, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13EXL46zDw9RSCFGFEkaTndQ7SwaabRza\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Areopagus, also known as the Hill of Ares, is a hill located west of the Acropolis of Athens. Its name comes from the combination of \\\"Ares\\\" (the god of war) and \\\"p\\u00e1gos\\\" (meaning \\\"hill\\\" in Greek). Geologically, this hill is a huge monolith of bluish-gray marble, veined with red, and is 115 meters high. \\r\\nLegends and Significance:\\r\\n- According to legend, the Areopagus received its name because Ares was judged there by the gods and exonerated from being executed for putting to death Halirrotius, son of Poseidon, who had raped a daughter of Ares named Alcipe.\\r\\n\\r\\n- It is also said that in this place Orestes was judged for the murder of his mother, Clytemnestra.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Council of the Areopagus:\\r\\n1. It was the seat of the Council which met there from 480 B.C. to A.D. 425.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. In its beginnings, the Council of the Areopagus was composed exclusively of eupatrides and depended on the king. Over time, its influence grew and, after Solon\'s reforms, its members were chosen from among the archons (magistrates).\\r\\n\\r\\n3. This court had the function of controlling the magistrates, interpreting the laws and judging murderers. Although its political powers diminished over time, it maintained its relevance until the Medical Wars.\\r\\n\\r\\nSt. Paul in the Areopagus:\\r\\n- According to the biblical record in Acts 17:16-18 and 22, the apostle Paul delivered a speech at the Areopagus. There, Epicurean and Stoic philosophers brought him to explain their \\\"foreign\\\" teaching. Paul reasoned with the inhabitants of Athens, who were surrounded by idols.\\r\\n\\r\\nToday it is a huge monolith of bluish-gray marble, veined with red. From its summit and slopes, one can see cuts in the rock that form platforms, which are the only remains of the ancient meeting place.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Areopagus is a place steeped in history and significance, and its majesty remains impressive to this day.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/15rm6UDg1t42V19jwvhrjZ8QObJkUt2KJ\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1D4We7ecQHfejnQNrn1PuV7uFkeJal-oZ\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qJuUdLWPvhEtWfUmRNRb9wZ1zc-L7EHt\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1olDzN4HZF45hgA1Tlsx8rtJNALZZmrii\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TPKcGy3e4Ri1StjgcftuJ_EWSxHbY34c\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ktjfIy4JZybK-jIHZFHGcRgkfgH08y3n\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 09:45:45', '2024-03-25 09:45:45'),
(138, 364, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/ig3gVSLbavo?si=9z15dMlQkb5ODwWp\",\"esdescription\":\"El Teatro de Dioniso, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como Teatro de Dionisio, fue el mayor teatro de la antigua Grecia y se encuentra en la parte oriental de la vertiente sur de la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas. Este impresionante teatro forma parte de los t\\u00e9menos de Dioniso y es considerado el m\\u00e1s antiguo del mundo. \\r\\nAlgunos detalles sobre el Teatro de Dioniso:\\r\\n1. Dedicado a Dioniso: Como su nombre indica, este teatro estaba dedicado al dios griego Dioniso, quien era el dios de las vi\\u00f1as y del teatro. Inicialmente, se realizaban rituales en su honor alrededor del altar del templo, y los espectadores se sentaban a los lados ya que, la duraci\\u00f3n de las representaciones se alargaba hasta unas seis horas y la entrada era costosa. Con el tiempo, estos rituales evolucionaron hacia las tragedias cl\\u00e1sicas de autores como Esquilo, S\\u00f3focles, Eur\\u00edpides y Arist\\u00f3fanes. \\r\\n\\r\\n2. Dimensiones Monumentales: El Teatro de Dioniso ten\\u00eda 117,9 metros de ancho y 96,09 metros de alto. En su etapa final, ten\\u00eda capacidad para 15,000-17,000 espectadores. El grader\\u00edo o koilon estaba dividido en dos niveles por un pasillo circular. En la parte central de las primeras gradas, hab\\u00eda 67 asientos de m\\u00e1rmol decorado reservados para dignatarios.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Estructura y Escenario: Los coros cantaban y danzaban en el espacio circular central llamado orchesta, acompa\\u00f1ando la acci\\u00f3n dram\\u00e1tica. Los actores actuaban en el legue\\u00edon o proskenion, una plataforma larga y estrecha con un fondo arquitect\\u00f3nico permanente. La sken\\u00e9, o escenario, estaba flanqueada por dos cuerpos salientes llamados parascenias, destinados a la maquinaria del teatro.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Historia y Restauraci\\u00f3n: La construcci\\u00f3n inicial data de la segunda mitad del siglo VI a. C. A finales del siglo V a. C., se sustituyeron las plataformas de madera por gradas de piedra. Alrededor del a\\u00f1o 330 a. C., el Santuario y el Teatro de Dionisio ya estaban completamente configurados. A pesar de las ruinas, este tesoro arquitect\\u00f3nico sigue siendo un testimonio valioso de la antig\\u00fcedad.\\r\\n\\r\\nNo te pierdas la oportunidad de admirar la grandeza y la historia del Teatro de Dioniso.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1gS3-9aMENyQXgkqafzlnOBmnEP-hmFSv\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kF4zZhQarFtHNpfMjDJjvgPpH7PZj5f2\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1V5P4eT2Bde17LCOJHp3qKqc13iatIJ7j\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i9FBezuVUfmyB2N4pbgtao_Cq7JSpjKY\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1uHSe_etgQTGj2C0raofGU2sBkO5GaNM5\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UKsUFt53_dNyxnqdUGOnJNGGB2fragAl\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 11:05:07', '2024-04-02 11:56:40'),
(139, 36, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1LHSJUmjG8wGDFLjI0WDR5s6Yt0ITUOEF\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Theater of Dionysus, also known as the Theater of Dionysus, was the largest theater in ancient Greece and is located on the eastern side of the southern slope of the Acropolis of Athens. This impressive theater is part of the Temple of Dionysus and is considered the oldest theater in the world. \\r\\nSome details about the Theater of Dionysus:\\r\\n1. dedicated to Dionysus: As its name suggests, this theater was dedicated to the Greek god Dionysus, who was the god of the vines and of the theater. Initially, rituals were performed in his honor around the altar of the temple, and the spectators sat on the sides, since the duration of the performances lasted up to six hours and the entrance fee was expensive. Over time, these rituals evolved into classical tragedies by authors such as Aeschylus, Sophocles, Euripides and Aristophanes. \\r\\n\\r\\n2. Monumental Dimensions: The Theater of Dionysus was 117.9 meters wide and 96.09 meters high. In its final stage, it had capacity for 15,000-17,000 spectators. The grandstand or koilon was divided into two levels by a circular corridor. In the central part of the first tiers, there were 67 decorated marble seats reserved for dignitaries.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Structure and Stage: The choirs sang and danced in the central circular space called orchesta, accompanying the dramatic action. The actors performed on the legueion or proskenion, a long, narrow platform with a permanent architectural backdrop. The skene, or stage, was flanked by two projecting bodies called parascenias, intended for the machinery of the theater.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. History and Restoration: The initial construction dates from the second half of the 6th century B.C. At the end of the 5th century B.C., the wooden platforms were replaced by stone tiers. Around 330 B.C., the Sanctuary and the Theater of Dionysus were already fully configured. Despite the ruins, this architectural treasure remains a valuable testimony of antiquity.\\r\\n\\r\\nDon\'t miss the opportunity to admire the grandeur and history of the Theater of Dionysus.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1gS3-9aMENyQXgkqafzlnOBmnEP-hmFSv\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kF4zZhQarFtHNpfMjDJjvgPpH7PZj5f2\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1V5P4eT2Bde17LCOJHp3qKqc13iatIJ7j\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i9FBezuVUfmyB2N4pbgtao_Cq7JSpjKY\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1uHSe_etgQTGj2C0raofGU2sBkO5GaNM5\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UKsUFt53_dNyxnqdUGOnJNGGB2fragAl\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 11:05:22', '2024-03-25 11:05:22'),
(140, 103, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/2E5GxxFTZmg?si=9BFooJQNnRP27-iI\",\"esdescription\":\"Plaka, also known as the Neighborhood of the Gods, is an ancient and picturesque neighborhood in Athens, Greece. Its charm lies in its combination of ancient history and neoclassical architecture. Here are some details about Plaka:\\r\\n1. History and Features:\\r\\n- Plaka is located on the northern and eastern slopes of the Acropolis. It is built on the residential areas of the ancient city of Athens.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Although it is one of the most modern and visited areas of Athens, Plaka still retains its ancient touch. Its narrow streets and beautiful 19th century neoclassical houses give it a special atmosphere.\\r\\n\\r\\n- In Plaka, you will find stores with the best souvenirs to take home and restaurants where you can enjoy the delicious Greek cuisine.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Places of Interest in Plaka:\\r\\n- Church of St. Nicholas Rangavas: This church, founded in the 11th century by the Rangavas family, has a classical design and a bell commemorating the liberation of Greece from the Ottoman Empire in 1821.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Medrese Gate: A former theological school sometimes used as a prison. It is located near the famous Tower of the Winds.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Lantern of Lysikrates, Cathedral of the Annunciation of St. Mary and Church of Agios Eleftherios of Athens are also interesting places to explore.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Tower of the Winds: An ancient water and wind clock that has survived the centuries.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Location and Charm:\\r\\n- Plaka is almost at the eastern foot of the Acropolis, making it perfect for exploring after visiting nearby historic sites.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Its cobblestone streets, flowers on balconies and traditional tavernas create a charming atmosphere. It is a place where history blends with everyday life.\\r\\n\\r\\nDon\'t miss the experience of strolling through the streets of Plaka - it\'s a true historical treasure!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kT2a-izx5iWlXw10fHmSWPjU-SHuzc59\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WZ87b2TS3JGW219EGBhFhru1S3iu6MKN\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ELHhc3vKuljRGaQASk5ZF6pj-_ID2uZ_\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1MCjG2xXUUXdHtWvt7cyiZt7nnnglnY9U\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/11BZ4EWVJwRqgAAFZgGkKfLmLoKjaFFE3\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i7TBKMYEG7ZG52ZaLtgkLJ5o9fTFTr58\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 11:11:44', '2024-04-01 11:29:55'),
(141, 40, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/Q7gnat0AnDQ?si=5BzL-Yz3YHFdW9zt\",\"esdescription\":\"Plaka, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como el Barrio de los Dioses, es un antiguo y pintoresco vecindario en Atenas, Grecia. Su encanto radica en su combinaci\\u00f3n de historia antigua y arquitectura neocl\\u00e1sica. Aqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles sobre Plaka:\\r\\n1. Historia y Caracter\\u00edsticas:\\r\\n- Plaka se encuentra en las laderas norte y este de la Acr\\u00f3polis. Est\\u00e1 construido sobre las \\u00e1reas residenciales de la antigua ciudad de Atenas.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Aunque es una de las zonas m\\u00e1s modernas y visitadas de Atenas, Plaka a\\u00fan conserva su toque antiguo. Sus calles estrechas y las hermosas casas neocl\\u00e1sicas del siglo XIX le dan un ambiente especial.\\r\\n\\r\\n- En Plaka, encontrar\\u00e1s tiendas con los mejores recuerdos para llevar a casa y restaurantes donde podr\\u00e1s disfrutar de la deliciosa gastronom\\u00eda griega.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Lugares de Inter\\u00e9s en Plaka:\\r\\n- Iglesia de San Nicol\\u00e1s Rangav\\u00e1s: Esta iglesia, fundada en el siglo XI por la familia Rangav\\u00e1s, tiene un dise\\u00f1o cl\\u00e1sico y una campana conmemorativa de la liberaci\\u00f3n de Grecia del imperio otomano en 1821.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Puerta de M\\u00e9drese: Una antigua escuela teol\\u00f3gica utilizada en ocasiones como prisi\\u00f3n. Se encuentra cerca de la famosa Torre de los Vientos.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Linterna de Lis\\u00edcrates, Catedral de la Anunciaci\\u00f3n de Santa Mar\\u00eda y Iglesia de Agios Eleftherios de Atenas tambi\\u00e9n son lugares interesantes para explorar.\\r\\n\\r\\n- La Torre de los Vientos: Un antiguo reloj de agua y viento que ha sobrevivido a los siglos.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Ubicaci\\u00f3n y Encanto:\\r\\n- Plaka est\\u00e1 casi al pie oriental de la Acr\\u00f3polis, lo que la hace perfecta para explorar despu\\u00e9s de visitar los sitios hist\\u00f3ricos cercanos.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Sus calles empedradas, las flores en los balcones y las tabernas tradicionales crean una atm\\u00f3sfera encantadora. Es un lugar donde la historia se mezcla con la vida cotidiana.\\r\\n\\r\\nNo te pierdas la experiencia de pasear por las calles de Plaka. \\u00a1Es un verdadero tesoro hist\\u00f3rico!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1kT2a-izx5iWlXw10fHmSWPjU-SHuzc59\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WZ87b2TS3JGW219EGBhFhru1S3iu6MKN\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ELHhc3vKuljRGaQASk5ZF6pj-_ID2uZ_\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1MCjG2xXUUXdHtWvt7cyiZt7nnnglnY9U\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/11BZ4EWVJwRqgAAFZgGkKfLmLoKjaFFE3\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i7TBKMYEG7ZG52ZaLtgkLJ5o9fTFTr58\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 11:12:12', '2024-04-01 11:27:22'),
(142, 162, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1I0bIxUKutTHlNVSFlmYK2j716q7HCEW5\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"Smyrna Agora, also known as \\u0130zmir Agora (Turkish: \\u0130zmir Agoras\\u0131), is an archaeological site of great importance in the ancient Greek city of Smyrna (present-day \\u0130zmir, Turkey). It is an ancient Roman agora located in Smyrna Originally built by the Greeks in the 4th century BC.\\r\\nSome highlights of the Smyrna Agora include:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. History and Reconstruction:\\r\\n- Greek Foundation: The Greeks originally built this agora in the 4th century BC as an important commercial and social center.\\r\\n- Earthquake and Reconstruction: After being devastated by an earthquake in 178 AD, the Roman emperor Marcus Aurelius ordered its reconstruction.\\r\\n- Excavations: Excavations at the site began in 1933.\\r\\n- World Heritage: In 2020, the Agora of Smyrna was listed as a Tentative World Heritage Site as part of \\\"The Historic Port City of \\u0130zmir\\\".\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Recent Discoveries:\\r\\n- During excavations, a 2,000-year-old functional water channel was discovered, highlighting the advanced engineering of the time.\\r\\n- Graffiti was found on the basilica wall, believed to be the world\'s first crossword puzzle.\\r\\n- Evidence of coexistence between Muslims, Christians and Jews was also found, as indicated by candles with the Star of David.\\r\\n- Current excavations focus on Ottoman-era cemeteries.\\r\\nSmyrna\'s Agora is a living testimony to history and cultural diversity, and its importance continues to resonate today.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Architectural features:\\r\\n- Well-Preserved Corinthian Columns: Visitors can admire Corinthian columns that have stood the test of time. These columns were part of the buildings and structures that made up the Agora.\\r\\n- Remains of Stoas: The stoas were arcaded galleries where people gathered for commercial, social and political activities. Although in ruins, the remains of these structures can still be seen in the Agora.\\r\\n- Odeon (Small Theater): The Agora housed a small theater called the Odeon. Although much of it has disappeared, its historical presence is still significant.\\r\\n- Fountains and Temples: The Agora had fountains and temples dedicated to various deities. These places were important for the religious and cultural life of the city.\\r\\n- Intricate Carvings: The advanced craftsmanship and urban planning of ancient Smyrna are reflected in the carvings and architectural details found in the Agora.\\r\\n- Smyrna\'s Agora has undergone extensive conservation efforts, including archaeological excavations and restoration projects, to preserve its historical legacy and make it accessible to the public.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1G7W7Je_LY1mtybizloYuSITjhgfKHeLv\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lp2DULJgWBYPugJXb--W4h79qOtHcQlz\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ExSXgYQLCL_inE1ItUWvUYx9mQOMinMt\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12ySFOEkG_hM8kOeOpryV9yNN7bcc8_GT\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zUstlFQz0J2LLfLKlFEIlL2C_tQ4mI9C\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1d7Bf1Sr5bhakfBsQ-g-LWyGTXUcrZhRF\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 11:28:41', '2024-03-25 11:28:41'),
(143, 163, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/DPzOiAEEYG4?si=0VyfX3LrbHehsb1S\",\"esdescription\":\"El \\u00c1gora de Esmirna, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como \\u00c1gora de \\u0130zmir (en turco: \\u0130zmir Agoras\\u0131), es un sitio arqueol\\u00f3gico de gran importancia en la antigua ciudad griega de Esmirna (actual \\u0130zmir, Turqu\\u00eda). Es un antiguo \\u00e1gora romana ubicado en Esmirna Originalmente construido por los griegos en el siglo IV a.C.\\r\\nAlgunos aspectos destacados del \\u00c1gora de Esmirna incluyen:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Historia y Reconstrucci\\u00f3n:\\r\\n- Fundaci\\u00f3n Griega: Los griegos construyeron originalmente esta \\u00e1gora en el siglo IV a.C. como un importante centro comercial y social.\\r\\n- Terremoto y Reconstrucci\\u00f3n: Despu\\u00e9s de ser devastado por un terremoto en el a\\u00f1o 178 d.C., el emperador romano Marco Aurelio orden\\u00f3 su reconstrucci\\u00f3n.\\r\\n- Excavaciones: Las excavaciones en el sitio comenzaron en 1933.\\r\\n- Patrimonio Mundial: En 2020, el \\u00c1gora de Esmirna fue incluido como Sitio del Patrimonio Mundial Tentativo como parte de \\u201cLa Ciudad Portuaria Hist\\u00f3rica de \\u0130zmir\\u201d.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Descubrimientos Recientes:\\r\\n- Durante las excavaciones, se descubri\\u00f3 un canal de agua funcional de 2,000 a\\u00f1os de antig\\u00fcedad, destacando la ingenier\\u00eda avanzada de la \\u00e9poca.\\r\\n- Se encontraron grafitis en la pared de la bas\\u00edlica, que se cree que es el primer crucigrama del mundo.\\r\\n- Tambi\\u00e9n se hallaron evidencias de coexistencia entre musulmanes, cristianos y jud\\u00edos, como se indica en las velas con la Estrella de David.\\r\\n- Las excavaciones actuales se centran en los cementerios de la era otomana.\\r\\nEl \\u00c1gora de Esmirna es un testimonio vivo de la historia y la diversidad cultural, y su importancia sigue resonando en la actualidad\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Caracter\\u00edsticas arquitect\\u00f3nicas:\\r\\n- Columnas Corintias Bien Conservadas: Los visitantes pueden admirar columnas corintias que han resistido el paso del tiempo. Estas columnas eran parte de los edificios y estructuras que conformaban el \\u00c1gora.\\r\\n- Restos de Estoas: Las estoas eran galer\\u00edas porticadas donde la gente se reun\\u00eda para actividades comerciales, sociales y pol\\u00edticas. Aunque en ruinas, los restos de estas estructuras a\\u00fan se pueden apreciar en el \\u00c1gora.\\r\\n- Ode\\u00f3n (Peque\\u00f1o Teatro): El \\u00c1gora albergaba un peque\\u00f1o teatro llamado Ode\\u00f3n. Aunque gran parte de \\u00e9l ha desaparecido, su presencia hist\\u00f3rica sigue siendo significativa.\\r\\n- Fuentes y Templos: El \\u00c1gora ten\\u00eda fuentes y templos dedicados a diversas deidades. Estos lugares eran importantes para la vida religiosa y cultural de la ciudad.\\r\\n- Tallas Intrincadas: La artesan\\u00eda avanzada y la planificaci\\u00f3n urbana de la antigua Esmirna se reflejan en las tallas y detalles arquitect\\u00f3nicos que se encuentran en el \\u00c1gora.\\r\\n- El \\u00c1gora de Esmirna ha sido objeto de amplios esfuerzos de conservaci\\u00f3n, incluyendo excavaciones arqueol\\u00f3gicas y proyectos de restauraci\\u00f3n, para preservar su legado hist\\u00f3rico y hacerlo accesible al p\\u00fablico\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1G7W7Je_LY1mtybizloYuSITjhgfKHeLv\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lp2DULJgWBYPugJXb--W4h79qOtHcQlz\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ExSXgYQLCL_inE1ItUWvUYx9mQOMinMt\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12ySFOEkG_hM8kOeOpryV9yNN7bcc8_GT\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zUstlFQz0J2LLfLKlFEIlL2C_tQ4mI9C\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1d7Bf1Sr5bhakfBsQ-g-LWyGTXUcrZhRF\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 11:28:53', '2024-04-02 10:43:21'),
(144, 178, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/4zxyc1KvmnI?si=JZTTnwblx2TQcpzq\",\"esdescription\":\"The Basilica of St. John of Ephesus is an imposing monument, full of history and religious significance, which invites travelers and pilgrims to immerse themselves in a world of spirituality and beauty. It is a majestic temple of Byzantine style.  \\r\\nSome outstanding features of the Basilica of St. John of Ephesus are:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Location: It is located in the ancient city of Ephesus in Turkey.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. History and Construction:\\r\\n- It was built in 548 AD by order of Emperor Justinian to honor the Apostle John. The basilica was erected at the foot of the ancient acropolis of Ephesus and suffered damages due to wars, conflicts and earthquakes.\\r\\n- Its original construction included arches and a majestic structure.\\r\\n- St. John of Ephesus is part of the program of renovation of Christian monuments in the East carried out by Emperor Justinian in the 6th century.\\r\\n- The basilica was intended to honor the Apostle John, who according to tradition died in Ephesus.\\r\\n- Excavations began in the 19th century.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Byzantine Style: The basilica follows the Byzantine architectural style. \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Present day:\\r\\n- Although only ruins remain, the Basilica of St. John of Ephesus remains an architectural and historical treasure that leaves a deep impression on those who have the privilege of knowing it.\\r\\n- Today its ruins, history and religious significance are preserved. Its legacy continues to amaze those who study the history and architecture of ancient Greece, as well as lingering in the memory of travelers and pilgrims.\\r\\n\\r\\nVisiting the Basilica of St. John is an unforgettable experience that leaves a deep impression on those who have the privilege of knowing it.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1S__Uhxjm03SUmOBIGDYjrG4euwvDR6eb\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Wz28oDoWSXRB64pGXAldhQ4nxLvee4Wr\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/10qvLoAxmYPZfmrBbFrQsWoe9Osn2h-3D\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FPTqln7e6yhitW31I58CtZxXW5mNvi6K\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aghbMwtGvpAAT530mFM5DRMhy6TGQTIX\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1m87GC3dOqSjp95H3CKT34LT3Uu9NGryU\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 19:39:30', '2024-04-01 15:22:49'),
(145, 177, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/Hn91k8WFbSE?si=f0XE4AucmVW93OPQ\",\"esdescription\":\"La Bas\\u00edlica de San Juan de \\u00c9feso es un monumento imponente, cargado de historia y significado religioso, que invita a viajeros y peregrinos a sumergirse en un mundo de espiritualidad y belleza. Es un majestuoso templo de estilo bizantino.  \\r\\nAlgunas caracter\\u00edsticas destacadas de la Bas\\u00edlica de San Juan de \\u00c9feso son:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n: Esta situada en la antigua ciudad de \\u00c9feso en Turqu\\u00eda.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Historia y Construcci\\u00f3n:\\r\\n- Fue construida en el a\\u00f1o 548 d.C. por orden del emperador Justiniano para honrar al ap\\u00f3stol Juan. La bas\\u00edlica fue erigida al pie de la antigua acr\\u00f3polis de \\u00c9feso y sufri\\u00f3 da\\u00f1os debido a guerras, conflictos y terremotos.\\r\\n- Su construcci\\u00f3n original inclu\\u00eda arcos y una majestuosa estructura.\\r\\n- San Juan de \\u00c9feso forma parte del programa de renovaci\\u00f3n de monumentos cristianos de Oriente que llev\\u00f3 a cabo el emperador Justiniano en el siglo VI.\\r\\n- El cometido de la bas\\u00edlica era honrar al ap\\u00f3stol Juan, quien seg\\u00fan la tradici\\u00f3n muri\\u00f3 en \\u00c9feso.\\r\\n- Las excavaciones comenzaron en el siglo XIX.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Estilo Bizantino: La bas\\u00edlica sigue el estilo arquitect\\u00f3nico bizantino. \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Actualidad:\\r\\n- Aunque solo quedan ruinas, la Bas\\u00edlica de San Juan de \\u00c9feso sigue siendo un tesoro arquitect\\u00f3nico e hist\\u00f3rico que deja una profunda impresi\\u00f3n en quienes tienen el privilegio de conocerla.\\r\\n- Hoy en d\\u00eda se conservan sus ruinas, su historia y significado religioso. Su legado sigue asombrando a quienes estudian la historia y la arquitectura de la antigua Grecia, adem\\u00e1s, perduran en la memoria de los viajeros y peregrinos.\\r\\n\\r\\nVisitar la Bas\\u00edlica de San Juan es una experiencia inolvidable que deja una profunda impresi\\u00f3n en quienes tienen el privilegio de conocerla.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1S__Uhxjm03SUmOBIGDYjrG4euwvDR6eb\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Wz28oDoWSXRB64pGXAldhQ4nxLvee4Wr\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/10qvLoAxmYPZfmrBbFrQsWoe9Osn2h-3D\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FPTqln7e6yhitW31I58CtZxXW5mNvi6K\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aghbMwtGvpAAT530mFM5DRMhy6TGQTIX\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1m87GC3dOqSjp95H3CKT34LT3Uu9NGryU\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 19:39:46', '2024-04-01 15:24:31');
INSERT INTO `sight_media` (`id`, `sight_id`, `media_link`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(146, 183, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/-A_Bdn4_rl8?si=qHLOIAOa6-Qj9NTi\",\"esdescription\":\"Lambi beach, also known as Lampi beach, is the most popular beach in the town of Kos, on the Greek island of the same name. This beautiful beach combines light sand and pebbles, and offers a variety of amenities and options for visitors.\\r\\nHere are more details about Lambi Beach:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Location and Amenities:\\r\\n- Lambi Beach is located just steps away from the picturesque harbor of Kos.\\r\\n- Initially, the beach stretches along a road with numerous restaurants, bars and beach clubs.\\r\\n- Here you will find a wide range of sun loungers and umbrellas, some of which are even free of charge.\\r\\n- After about a kilometer, the road parallel to the shore leads away from the beach, and the area becomes quieter, offering views of the Turkish mainland a few kilometers away.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Soil: The soil of this beach is composed of light sand and pebbles.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Environment and Entertainment Options:\\r\\n- Although not officially in the Lambi district, it is considered part of Lambi Beach.\\r\\n- The beach bars and restaurants on the beach cater to the cultural and gastronomic preferences of tourists.\\r\\n- If you are looking for a perfect beach atmosphere with options such as English breakfast, German beer or Dutch orange loungers, Lambi beach is ideal.\\r\\n- During the day, you can enjoy the friendly atmosphere and the view, in the evening, you can enjoy the bars and taverns on the street are popular places for a meal or a cocktail before heading to the lively street of bars in the harbor. In addition, they regularly host parties and live events in the evenings.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Southern part of the beach: On the southern part of the beach, near the port, is the area known as Akti Zouroudi.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Northern Part of the Beach: On the northern part of the beach, the actual Lambi Beach, you will find more elegant and chic beach clubs, where DJs play music during the day.\\r\\n\\r\\nLambi Beach is a versatile and central location, perfect for relaxing, enjoying the sun and exploring, offering an unforgettable experience on the beautiful Mediterranean island of Kos.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1fuPmdQGlTlS3_L2-0mOkmzMzIBgCqoUk\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16blQj0UlaMB7Ko5ZPwWeIatsgGjxVls7\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1r-DaQDS3WXGXZXWWx5lLZP_rKHWKs73Q\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1txezCl0e1hoy7AEmZ5_jcBCm097E1WmS\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Ahq3S3AZcHqubKRX_Vt_ygp3O5Kypwa8\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YR9un3U5W7JL4XO8tV-k3qPw8725VG-F\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 21:15:49', '2024-04-01 11:19:19'),
(147, 362, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/lFpnkFWF4XI?si=9-PvQyLx6k6CNtJW\",\"esdescription\":\"La playa de Lambi, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como playa de Lampi, es la playa m\\u00e1s popular en la ciudad de Kos, en la isla griega del mismo nombre. Esta hermosa playa combina arena ligera y guijarros, y ofrece una variedad de comodidades y opciones para los visitantes.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes m\\u00e1s detalles sobre la Playa de Lambi:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n y Comodidades:\\r\\n- La playa de Lambi se encuentra a solo unos pasos del pintoresco puerto de Kos.\\r\\n- Inicialmente, la playa se extiende a lo largo de una carretera con numerosos restaurantes, bares y clubes de playa.\\r\\n- Aqu\\u00ed encontrar\\u00e1s una amplia gama de tumbonas y sombrillas, algunas de las cuales incluso son gratuitas.\\r\\n- Despu\\u00e9s de aproximadamente un kil\\u00f3metro, el camino paralelo a la orilla se aleja de la playa, y la zona se vuelve m\\u00e1s tranquila, ofreciendo vistas al continente turco a pocos kil\\u00f3metros de distancia.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Suelo: El suelo de esta playa est\\u00e1 compuesto por arena ligera y guijarros.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Ambiente y Opciones de Entretenimiento:\\r\\n- Aunque no est\\u00e1 oficialmente en el distrito de Lambi, se considera parte de la playa de Lambi.\\r\\n- Los chiringuitos y restaurantes en la playa se adaptan a las preferencias culturales y gastron\\u00f3micas de los turistas.\\r\\n- Si buscas un ambiente de playa perfecto con opciones como el desayuno ingl\\u00e9s, cerveza alemana o tumbonas de color naranja holand\\u00e9s, la playa de Lambi es ideal.\\r\\n- Durante el d\\u00eda, puedes disfrutar de un ambiente amigable y la vista, por la tarde, puedes disfrutar de los bares y tabernas de la calle son lugares populares para una comida o un c\\u00f3ctel antes de dirigirte a la animada calle de bares en el puerto. Adem\\u00e1s, regularmente por las tardes tienen fiestas y eventos en vivo.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Parte Sur de la Playa: En la parte sur de la playa, cerca del puerto, se encuentra la zona conocida como Akti Zouroudi.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Parte Norte de la Playa: En la parte norte de la playa, la actual Playa de Lambi, encontrar\\u00e1s clubes de playa m\\u00e1s elegantes y chic, donde los DJs tocan m\\u00fasica durante el d\\u00eda.\\r\\n\\r\\nLa Playa de Lambi es un lugar vers\\u00e1til y c\\u00e9ntrico, perfecto para relajarse, disfrutar del sol y explorar, ofrece una experiencia inolvidable en la hermosa isla mediterr\\u00e1nea de Kos\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1fuPmdQGlTlS3_L2-0mOkmzMzIBgCqoUk\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16blQj0UlaMB7Ko5ZPwWeIatsgGjxVls7\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1r-DaQDS3WXGXZXWWx5lLZP_rKHWKs73Q\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1txezCl0e1hoy7AEmZ5_jcBCm097E1WmS\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Ahq3S3AZcHqubKRX_Vt_ygp3O5Kypwa8\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YR9un3U5W7JL4XO8tV-k3qPw8725VG-F\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 21:16:18', '2024-04-01 11:22:26'),
(148, 189, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/drWUfrzp8_o?si=e5REshusor3TdEmO\",\"esdescription\":\"El Monasterio de San Juan el Te\\u00f3logo (en griego: \\u039c\\u03bf\\u03bd\\u03ae \\u0391\\u03b3\\u03af\\u03bf\\u03c5 \\u0399\\u03c9\\u03ac\\u03bd\\u03bd\\u03bf\\u03c5 \\u03c4\\u03bf\\u03c5 \\u0398\\u03b5\\u03bf\\u03bb\\u03cc\\u03b3\\u03bf\\u03c5, Mon\\u00ed Ay\\u00edou Io\\u00e1nnou tou Theol\\u00f3gou) es un monasterio ortodoxo situado en la isla de Patmos, Grecia. Este monasterio, que depende directamente del Patriarcado ecum\\u00e9nico de Constantinopla.\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n y Dise\\u00f1o: El monasterio se encuentra en el barrio de Panochori, cerca de la plaza Mazaraki. Su dise\\u00f1o sigue la l\\u00ednea est\\u00e9tica del Rom\\u00e1nico Espa\\u00f1ol, caracterizado por formas pesadas y arcos pronunciados.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Historia y Convento Fortificado: La historia del monasterio se remonta al a\\u00f1o 1088, cuando fue fundado por Christodoulos. El emperador Alejo I Comneno le hab\\u00eda donado la isla de Patmos. Sin embargo, debido a una rebeli\\u00f3n de los monjes, Christodoulos huy\\u00f3 y se refugi\\u00f3 en Eubea, donde falleci\\u00f3 en 1093. \\r\\nA lo largo de los a\\u00f1os, el monasterio se convirti\\u00f3 en el epicentro y origen de la riqueza de la isla, benefici\\u00e1ndose de numerosos privilegios. Adem\\u00e1s, alberg\\u00f3 una de las bibliotecas m\\u00e1s prestigiosas de su tiempo. En 1132, el monasterio recibi\\u00f3 el estatuto imperial, y en 1157, Leontios defendi\\u00f3 sus privilegios ante el emperador Manuel I Comneno. Aunque en la actualidad cuenta con alrededor de 25 monjes, su patrimonio de tierras sigue siendo impresionante, ya que posee propiedades en otras islas.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Arquitectura: El Monasterio de San Juan el Te\\u00f3logo presenta una arquitectura impresionante y distintiva. Aqu\\u00ed est\\u00e1n algunos aspectos notables:\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Importancia: Fue nombrado Patrimonio de la Humanidad de la Unesco en 1999.\\r\\n\\r\\nEl Monasterio de San Juan el Te\\u00f3logo es un lugar de gran importancia hist\\u00f3rica y espiritual, y su ubicaci\\u00f3n en la hermosa isla de Patmos lo hace a\\u00fan m\\u00e1s especial. Su belleza y relevancia cultural lo convierten en un destino imperdible para los visitantes de Patmos.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1vH4XDgPTf5Rgy3vypHd1lwNFYRWVC_Y0\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_v3zo3sZqu4ZMy7zP7D_3J9LPHZVLgw1\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_dDFSTqUkDEZ8CaWlk3UvpYgWliGBzrq\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/19I9jgyjiZg1z4EDlLd72Olngg0KIDbPd\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1QV2PAOIsbPjH-MN97yEzXvSrKpvEEm-f\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ANiZcW4LdwwnyQCAOUX5XCcFF6r23-Ww\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 21:39:58', '2024-04-01 10:31:39'),
(149, 188, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1rHZlNMCFPvwpTLHEBjMLp94HMrnC-9tc\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Monastery of St. John the Theologian (Greek: \\u039c\\u03bf\\u03bd\\u03ae \\u0391\\u03b3\\u03af\\u03bf\\u03c5 \\u0399\\u03c9\\u03ac\\u03bd\\u03bd\\u03bd\\u03bf\\u03c5 \\u03c4\\u03bf\\u03c5 \\u0398\\u03b5\\u03bf\\u03bb\\u03cc\\u03b3\\u03bf\\u03c5, Mon\\u00ed Ay\\u00edou Io\\u00e1nnou tou Theol\\u00f3gou) is an Orthodox monastery located on the island of Patmos, Greece. This monastery, which is directly under the Ecumenical Patriarchate of Constantinople.\\r\\n\\r\\nLocation and Design: The monastery is located in the neighborhood of Panochori, near Mazaraki Square. Its design follows the aesthetic line of the Spanish Romanesque, characterized by heavy forms and pronounced arches.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. History and Fortified Convent: The history of the monastery dates back to 1088, when it was founded by Christodoulos. Emperor Alexius I Comnenus had donated the island of Patmos to him. However, due to a rebellion of the monks, Christodoulos fled and took refuge in Euboea, where he died in 1093. \\r\\nOver the years, the monastery became the epicenter and source of the island\'s wealth, benefiting from numerous privileges. It also housed one of the most prestigious libraries of its time. In 1132, the monastery received imperial status, and in 1157, Leontios defended its privileges before Emperor Manuel I Comnenus. Although it currently has about 25 monks, its land holdings remain impressive, as it owns properties on other islands.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Architecture: The Monastery of St. John the Theologian features impressive and distinctive architecture. Here are some notable features:\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Importance: It was named a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1999.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Monastery of St. John the Theologian is a place of great historical and spiritual importance, and its location on the beautiful island of Patmos makes it even more special. Its beauty and cultural relevance make it a must-see destination for visitors to Patmos.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1vH4XDgPTf5Rgy3vypHd1lwNFYRWVC_Y0\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_v3zo3sZqu4ZMy7zP7D_3J9LPHZVLgw1\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_dDFSTqUkDEZ8CaWlk3UvpYgWliGBzrq\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/19I9jgyjiZg1z4EDlLd72Olngg0KIDbPd\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1QV2PAOIsbPjH-MN97yEzXvSrKpvEEm-f\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ANiZcW4LdwwnyQCAOUX5XCcFF6r23-Ww\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-25 22:20:45', '2024-03-25 22:20:45'),
(150, 175, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/peboi13v39g?si=sGJYTZ2e9k2qcwra\",\"esdescription\":\"El Templo de Artemisa, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como Artemision o como el Templo de Diana para los romanos, fue un majestuoso edificio dedicado al culto de la diosa Artemisa en la antigua ciudad de \\u00c9feso. Este templo es considerado la Maravilla m\\u00e1s impresionante del mundo antiguo.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed est\\u00e1n algunos datos fascinantes sobre este colosal monumento:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n: Se ubicada en la costa de Asia Menor en la localidad turca de Sel\\u00e7uk, en la antig\\u00fcedad estaba ubicado en los dominios de \\u00c9feso (actual Turqu\\u00eda). \\r\\n\\r\\n2. Historia y Legado:\\r\\n- La ciudad de \\u00c9feso, fundada por griegos jonios en el siglo X a.C., erigi\\u00f3 sucesivamente tres templos en honor a \\u00c1rtemisa.\\r\\n- El templo monumental se convirti\\u00f3 en la joya de la ciudad y atrajo a visitantes de todo el mundo antiguo.\\r\\n- Desafortunadamente, hoy en d\\u00eda apenas quedan restos materiales de este colosal monumento, pero su fama perdura en la historia y la mitolog\\u00eda.\\r\\n- Este templo se incluy\\u00f3 entre las Siete Maravillas debido a la majestuosidad de sus formas arquitect\\u00f3nicas y sus imponentes dimensiones. De hecho, fue el templo m\\u00e1s grande jam\\u00e1s construido por los griegos, incluso superando al propio Parten\\u00f3n de Atenas.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Construcci\\u00f3n y Dimensiones:\\r\\n- El rey lidio Creso inici\\u00f3 la construcci\\u00f3n del templo en el siglo VI a.C., y su edificaci\\u00f3n se prolong\\u00f3 durante aproximadamente 120 a\\u00f1os.\\r\\n- El templo ten\\u00eda 127 columnas j\\u00f3nicas que alcanzaban una altura de 60 metros y se alzaba a una altura de 115 metros.\\r\\n- Su tama\\u00f1o era considerable: med\\u00eda 380 pies de largo y 180 pies de ancho.\\r\\n- Ant\\u00edpatro de Sid\\u00f3n, un poeta del siglo II a.C., escribi\\u00f3 sobre el templo: \\u201cCuando vi la mansi\\u00f3n de \\u00c1rtemis alz\\u00e1ndose hasta las nubes, aqu\\u00e9llas palidecieron y me dije: \\u2018Mira, aparte del Olimpo, el Sol no ha contemplado nada parecido\\u2019\\u201d.\\r\\n- Pausanias, un viajero griego del siglo II d.C., tambi\\u00e9n qued\\u00f3 extasiado y elogi\\u00f3 el templo por su magnitud, esplendor y la fama de la ciudad de \\u00c9feso.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Actualidad: Aunque hoy en d\\u00eda quedan muy escasos restos materiales de aquel monumento, su legado sigue asombrando a quienes estudian la historia y la arquitectura de la antigua Grecia, en gran parte, su leyenda sigue inspirando admiraci\\u00f3n y asombro.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zLEnuNdh6DES5ii6WMN2TYSwnD-Kk56E\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FyB5-QqC0e1deShEmnI_ol8jxMXTgCZ4\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lP4FsppvFRRfukbCzRVLr-ICE139Qn4k\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Dp_BCnrT43oVtBKm-RL-JYDi2pZjCZ6p\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Qd3PbQ9vbdlfPhdTkvDv1126di-uXRLZ\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16CGEs-ARq7G2WgLV-cPT5O_T3xxph3fl\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 09:29:36', '2024-04-02 11:38:22'),
(151, 176, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12bZU0YndXML3sAEfXU3-Hi52HAfwpcqY\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Temple of Artemis, also known as Artemision or as the Temple of Diana to the Romans, was a majestic building dedicated to the worship of the goddess Artemis in the ancient city of Ephesus. This temple is considered the most impressive Wonder of the ancient world.\\r\\nHere are some fascinating facts about this colossal monument:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Location: it is located on the coast of Asia Minor in the Turkish town of Sel\\u00e7uk, in ancient times it was located in the domains of Ephesus (present-day Turkey). \\r\\n\\r\\n2. History and Legacy:\\r\\n- The city of Ephesus, founded by Ionian Greeks in the 10th century BC, successively erected three temples in honor of Artemis.\\r\\n- The monumental temple became the jewel of the city and attracted visitors from all over the ancient world.\\r\\n- Unfortunately, today hardly any material remains of this colossal monument remain, but its fame endures in history and mythology.\\r\\n- This temple was included among the Seven Wonders because of the majesty of its architectural forms and its imposing dimensions. In fact, it was the largest temple ever built by the Greeks, even surpassing the Parthenon of Athens itself.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Construction and Dimensions:\\r\\n- The Lydian king Croesus initiated the construction of the temple in the 6th century BC, and its construction lasted for approximately 120 years.\\r\\n- The temple had 127 Ionic columns that reached a height of 60 meters and rose to a height of 115 meters.\\r\\n- Its size was considerable: it was 380 feet long and 180 feet wide.\\r\\n- Antipater of Sidon, a 2nd century B.C. poet, wrote of the temple: \\\"When I saw the mansion of Artemis rising up to the clouds, they paled and I said to myself: \'Look, apart from Olympus, the Sun has never beheld anything like it.\\r\\n- Pausanias, a Greek traveler of the second century A.D., was also ecstatic and praised the temple for its magnitude, splendor and the fame of the city of Ephesus.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Present: Although today very little remains of that monument, its legacy continues to amaze those who study the history and architecture of ancient Greece, in large part, its legend continues to inspire admiration and awe.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zLEnuNdh6DES5ii6WMN2TYSwnD-Kk56E\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FyB5-QqC0e1deShEmnI_ol8jxMXTgCZ4\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lP4FsppvFRRfukbCzRVLr-ICE139Qn4k\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Dp_BCnrT43oVtBKm-RL-JYDi2pZjCZ6p\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Qd3PbQ9vbdlfPhdTkvDv1126di-uXRLZ\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16CGEs-ARq7G2WgLV-cPT5O_T3xxph3fl\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 09:30:07', '2024-03-26 09:30:07'),
(152, 309, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/0gqElleFIyw?si=OusL5FwnpK7ExLoO\",\"esdescription\":\"La Isla de Patmos, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como P\\u00e1tmos, es una peque\\u00f1a joya en el mar Egeo. Es un lugar lleno de historia, belleza y espiritualidad, donde la presencia del ap\\u00f3stol Juan sigue resonando a trav\\u00e9s de los siglos. Es un destino fascinante que combina arte, historia y espiritualidad.\\r\\n Aqu\\u00ed algunos detalles sobre esta fascinante isla griega:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Geograf\\u00eda:\\r\\n- Patmos se encuentra en el archipi\\u00e9lago del Dodecaneso, en Grecia.\\r\\n- Tiene una poblaci\\u00f3n de aproximadamente 2500 personas y una superficie de 34,6 km\\u00b2.\\r\\n- Las principales poblaciones de Patmos son Skala, que es tambi\\u00e9n su \\u00fanico puerto, y Chora, ubicada en el punto m\\u00e1s alto de la isla, el monte Profitis Ilias (Profeta El\\u00edas), a 269 metros sobre el nivel del mar. En este lugar se asienta el monasterio de San Juan.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Historia y Significado:\\r\\n- Patmos debe su renombre a la menci\\u00f3n que aparece en el Apocalipsis de Juan. Seg\\u00fan la introducci\\u00f3n de este libro, el autor fue desterrado a Patmos, donde tuvo su encuentro con Dios en la llamada Gruta del Apocalipsis, en esta cueva se cree que San Juan Evangelista escribi\\u00f3 el Apocalipsis. En dicho lugar se puede observar la grieta en el techo, donde se dice que Jes\\u00fas se le apareci\\u00f3. \\r\\n- Las tradiciones tempranas del cristianismo identificaban a ese personaje con San Juan Evangelista. Por ello, la isla de Patmos se ha convertido en un importante destino de peregrinaci\\u00f3n cristiana.\\r\\n- Los visitantes pueden ver la cueva en la que, seg\\u00fan la tradici\\u00f3n, San Juan tuvo sus visiones. Las iglesias y comunidades religiosas de Patmos pertenecen a la tradici\\u00f3n ortodoxa oriental.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Aqu\\u00ed algunos de las principales atracciones que puedes visitar en Patmos:\\r\\n- Monasterio de San Juan el Te\\u00f3logo: Este monasteriose encuentra en Chora, un hermoso pueblo medieval con casas blancas. \\r\\nChora (Ciudad Patrimonio UNESCO): La capital de Patmos, Chora, es un laberinto de callejones estrechos y casas encaladas, esta llena de tiendas y tabernas tradicionales. \\r\\n- Playas de Patmos: Psili Ammos (Galardonada como la mejor playa de Europa, esta playa es un para\\u00edso vacacional),  Agriolivado (Aguas cristalinas y una de las temperaturas m\\u00e1s fr\\u00edas de Grecia) y Grikos (Otra hermosa playa para relajarte).\\r\\n- Castillo de Chora: Ofrece vistas panor\\u00e1micas impresionantes de la isla.\\r\\n- Monasterio de la Anunciaci\\u00f3n: Otro monasterio importante en Patmos.\\r\\n- Iglesia de Panagia Skala: Ubicada en el puerto principal y el pueblo de Skala.\\r\\n- Museo del Folklore de Patmos: Sum\\u00e9rgete en la cultura local.\\r\\n- Plaza de Chora: Disfruta de la atm\\u00f3sfera relajada y las vistas.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Mg5Lvy3myYVVfwiAnrMpwlX9TQvlsAM4\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qv2r-rVGojCu-eBetrr_CjM8mLThh-pv\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1RQa2u1q6I0scXdCh0R42VnvDR8Yu-PiR\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1sCafFM0XIXA1jAsSERD3nWRnYOpoYwOS\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UOhjWzgpY_beL1xn1Bt461OlLYwbx0Fb\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qdK2y90_Gcwb3rgAm-f91kbH8oqvrq8j\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 09:45:12', '2024-04-01 11:37:38'),
(153, 310, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/lTJkKDKb4ro?si=rKGZ-Sh0W-buOE5l\",\"esdescription\":\"The island of Patmos, also known as Patmos, is a small jewel in the Aegean Sea. It is a place full of history, beauty and spirituality, where the presence of the Apostle John continues to echo through the centuries. It is a fascinating destination that combines art, history and spirituality.\\r\\n Here are some details about this fascinating Greek island:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Geography:\\r\\n- Patmos is located in the Dodecanese archipelago in Greece.\\r\\n- It has a population of approximately 2500 people and an area of 34.6 km\\u00b2.\\r\\n- The main towns of Patmos are Skala, which is also its only port, and Chora, located on the highest point of the island, Mount Profitis Ilias (Prophet Elias), 269 meters above sea level. The monastery of St. John is located here.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. History and Significance:\\r\\n- Patmos owes its renown to the mention that appears in the Apocalypse of John. According to the introduction of this book, the author was exiled to Patmos, where he had his encounter with God in the so-called Grotto of the Apocalypse, in this cave it is believed that St. John the Evangelist wrote the Apocalypse. In this place you can see the crack in the ceiling, where it is said that Jesus appeared to him. \\r\\n- Early Christian traditions identified this character with St. John the Evangelist. For this reason, the island of Patmos has become an important Christian pilgrimage destination.\\r\\n- Visitors can see the cave where, according to tradition, St. John had his visions. The churches and religious communities of Patmos belong to the Eastern Orthodox tradition.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Here are some of the main attractions you can visit in Patmos:\\r\\n- Monastery of St. John the Theologian: This monastery is located in Chora, a beautiful medieval village with white houses. \\r\\nChora (UNESCO Heritage City): The capital of Patmos, Chora, is a maze of narrow alleys and whitewashed houses, full of traditional stores and tavernas. \\r\\n- Beaches of Patmos: Psili Ammos (Awarded as the best beach in Europe, this beach is a holiday paradise), Agriolivado (Crystal clear waters and one of the coolest temperatures in Greece) and Grikos (Another beautiful beach to relax).\\r\\n- Chora Castle: It offers breathtaking panoramic views of the island.\\r\\n- Monastery of the Annunciation: Another important monastery on Patmos.\\r\\n- Church of Panagia Skala: Located in the main port and village of Skala.\\r\\n- Folklore Museum of Patmos: Immerse yourself in the local culture.\\r\\n- Chora Square: Enjoy the relaxed atmosphere and views.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Mg5Lvy3myYVVfwiAnrMpwlX9TQvlsAM4\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qv2r-rVGojCu-eBetrr_CjM8mLThh-pv\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1RQa2u1q6I0scXdCh0R42VnvDR8Yu-PiR\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1sCafFM0XIXA1jAsSERD3nWRnYOpoYwOS\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UOhjWzgpY_beL1xn1Bt461OlLYwbx0Fb\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qdK2y90_Gcwb3rgAm-f91kbH8oqvrq8j\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 09:50:26', '2024-04-01 11:35:09'),
(154, 186, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/FGpTwRMmRHE?si=lAtyZC7wSUAKMWwh\",\"esdescription\":\"The Grotto of the Apocalypse is a site of great historical and religious significance.\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Location: The site of the revelatory visions, known as the Grotto of the Apocalypse, is located halfway along the road linking the port to Chora (between the villages of Chora and Skala), which is located on top of the island\'s mountain.\\r\\n\\r\\nSignificance: This grotto is believed to mark the place where John received his visions, based on which he wrote the Apocalypse.\\r\\nThe mosaic above the entrance to the grotto depicts John of Patmos (on the right) and his disciple Prochorus, who transcribed John\'s visions.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Design: Although modest in size, the grotto has a deep religious significance. Some curious details still preserved in the grotto include:\\r\\n\\r\\n- A rock where St. John presumably rested his head.\\r\\n- Another rock that served as a desk.\\r\\n- Cracks in the stone vault through which, according to tradition, the divine voice reached him.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Significance: In 1999, UNESCO declared the grotto a World Heritage Site, along with the monastery of St. John the Theologian and the historic center of Patmos. \\r\\n\\r\\nThis sacred site is a reminder of spirituality and history, and offers visitors the opportunity to connect with religious roots and apocalyptic narrative.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1QV2PAOIsbPjH-MN97yEzXvSrKpvEEm-f\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Hs2Q9TSO23M9f8S2hYgWRpYhzw_xOWLa\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1BQ9m_MUT9xiAyTzm0pZWgzBt4UrYXf9P\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UNLBLWxpA95JyxCqe_Es-Z8z6OeuDW66\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YITvmP_qts9ctPiMN6l7Ry7eDq7YTvzT\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ANiZcW4LdwwnyQCAOUX5XCcFF6r23-Ww\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 10:39:57', '2024-04-01 15:42:55'),
(155, 187, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/7Qq8PtEnWto?si=FQK6RBpTL2f9hMyl\",\"esdescription\":\"La Gruta del Apocalipsis es un sitio de gran significado hist\\u00f3rico y religioso.\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n: El lugar de las visiones reveladoras, conocido como la Gruta del Apocalipsis, est\\u00e1 situado a medio camino de la carretera que une el puerto con la Chora (entre los pueblos de Chora y Skala), que se encuentra en la cima de la monta\\u00f1a de la isla.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Significado: En esta gruta se cree que marca el lugar donde Juan recibi\\u00f3 sus visiones, con base en las cuales escribi\\u00f3 el Apocalipsis.\\r\\nEl mosaico sobre la entrada de la gruta representa a Juan de Patmos (a la derecha) y a su disc\\u00edpulo Procoro, quien transcribe las visiones de Juan.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Dise\\u00f1o: Aunque su tama\\u00f1o es modesto, la gruta tiene un significado religioso profundo. Algunos detalles curiosos que a\\u00fan se conservan en la gruta incluyen:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Una roca donde presuntamente San Juan apoyaba la cabeza.\\r\\n- Otra roca que le serv\\u00eda de escritorio.\\r\\n- Hendiduras en la b\\u00f3veda p\\u00e9trea por las que, seg\\u00fan la tradici\\u00f3n, le lleg\\u00f3 la voz divina.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Importancia: En 1999, la UNESCO declar\\u00f3 la gruta como Patrimonio de la Humanidad, junto con el monasterio de San Juan el Te\\u00f3logo y el centro hist\\u00f3rico de Patmos. \\r\\n\\r\\nEste sitio sagrado es un recordatorio de la espiritualidad y la historia, y ofrece a los visitantes la oportunidad de conectarse con las ra\\u00edces religiosas y la narrativa apocal\\u00edptica.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1QV2PAOIsbPjH-MN97yEzXvSrKpvEEm-f\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Hs2Q9TSO23M9f8S2hYgWRpYhzw_xOWLa\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1BQ9m_MUT9xiAyTzm0pZWgzBt4UrYXf9P\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1UNLBLWxpA95JyxCqe_Es-Z8z6OeuDW66\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YITvmP_qts9ctPiMN6l7Ry7eDq7YTvzT\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ANiZcW4LdwwnyQCAOUX5XCcFF6r23-Ww\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 10:40:12', '2024-04-01 15:44:51'),
(156, 191, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/OorOi2CvV_I?si=eNSJs_E9Io9FdJX_\",\"esdescription\":\"Chora, also known as Hora or Mykonos Town, is one of the most beautiful cities of the Cyclades islands in Greece. This capital of Mykonos is located in the eastern part of the island and differs from other Greek capitals, such as Santorini, as it is not built on a hill, but along a plain. The beauty of Chora lies in its coastal architecture: cubic buildings with flat roofs, cobblestone streets, two-story whitewashed houses with colorfully painted wooden balconies and bougainvillea adorning the narrow streets. In addition, multiple churches and chapels typical of the Cyclades, with blue or red domes, complete the charm of this place. It is a place that combines vitality and tranquility in a charming way.\\r\\nSome highlights to visit in Chora are:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. \\\"Little Venice\\\" neighborhood: this picturesque seaside neighborhood features colorful two and three-story houses, wooden balconies overlooking the sea and reminiscent of Venice. These houses, built over the sea, used to house subway pantries where pirates kept their booty. Today, \\\"Little Venice\\\" is a favorite spot for tourists, with taverns, bars and terraces.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Port of Mykonos Town (Chora): The fishing port of Mykonos Town is a nice place to stroll and enjoy the sea view. Here you will find a beautiful orthodox church with typical Cycladic architecture. Also, from the port, you can take caiques (small boats) that will take you to the beaches of Mykonos.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Whitewashed alleys and Photogenic Windmills: Walking around Chora, you will be immersed in a labyrinth of whitewashed alleys. The iconic windmills, typical of the Cyclades islands, rise on the hill above the town. These picturesque corners are perfect for capturing memorable photographs. In addition, the aroma of freshly baked bread emanating from the bakeries and cozy bars serving cocktails at sunset will entice you irresistibly.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Church of Panagia Gremniotisas: Located at the highest point of Chora, this beautiful church offers a spectacular view, especially at sunset. Its name means \\\"Virgin Mary of the Cliff\\\" and it is considered the protector of the island. The blue dome and tall bell tower distinguish it, and a nearby palm tree makes it visible from any point in Chora.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Archaeological Museum: Visiting the archaeological museum is an excellent way to appreciate the rich history of Ios. In a neoclassical mansion, you will find ancient pieces from the Cyclades, Hellenistic and Roman sculptures, as well as relics from the Middle and Late Bronze Age.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Odysseus Elytis Theater: This theater, named after the famous Greek poet Odysseus Elytis, is located behind the windmills above Chora. Seating 1,100 people, it is a venue for concerts, plays and other cultural events. It also offers a breathtaking view of the sea.\\r\\n\\r\\nExploring Chora will allow you to immerse yourself in the spirituality and history of this beautiful Greek island. It is a place that combines the authenticity of the Cyclades with a vibrant nightlife and serene beauty; exploring its narrow streets and discovering its treasures is an unforgettable experience!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CM_gyFp5duMq3VJLmrgXPK70w-Q1y3m6\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L3HOl_66bH1BTQTctFRgWA8bgzH8fEwt\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1AQvqr0q-MBTXgWmY6jd76uBo9-zpVKK3\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lBa3CC8iZrXVGwgMDMZyjh2XqpgnZeLW\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1MHNf2GVTwX9j0juBm2s35B_96RbGa4rI\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13Z9VjYrInpyZdvDEEPbMBNlntHEhwR0p\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 10:48:35', '2024-04-01 14:08:34'),
(157, 192, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/t9mHouFbZ9A?si=_IFKWoXUti7gRTXH\",\"esdescription\":\"Chora, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Hora o Mykonos Town, es una de las ciudades m\\u00e1s hermosas de las islas C\\u00edcladas en Grecia. Esta capital de Mykonos se encuentra en la parte este de la isla y se diferencia de otras capitales griegas, como la de Santorini, ya que no est\\u00e1 construida sobre una colina, sino a lo largo de una llanura. La belleza de Chora radica en su arquitectura costera: edificios c\\u00fabicos con techos planos, calles empedradas, casas encaladas de dos plantas con balcones de madera pintados de colores y buganvillas que adornan las callejuelas. Adem\\u00e1s, m\\u00faltiples iglesias y capillas t\\u00edpicas de las C\\u00edcladas, con c\\u00fapulas azules o rojas, completan el encanto de este lugar. Es un lugar que combina la vitalidad y la tranquilidad de manera encantadora.\\r\\nAlgunos puntos destacados para visitar en Chora son:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Barrio de la \\u201cPeque\\u00f1a Venecia\\u201d: Este pintoresco barrio junto al mar cuenta con casas de colores de dos y tres pisos, balcones de madera que miran al mar y reminiscencias de Venecia. Estas casas, construidas sobre el mar, sol\\u00edan albergar despensas subterr\\u00e1neas donde los piratas guardaban su bot\\u00edn. Hoy en d\\u00eda, la \\u201cPeque\\u00f1a Venecia\\u201d es un lugar favorito para los turistas, con tabernas, bares y terrazas.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Puerto de Mykonos Town (Chora): El puerto pesquero de Mykonos Town es un lugar agradable para pasear y disfrutar de la vista al mar. Aqu\\u00ed encontrar\\u00e1s una bonita iglesia ortodoxa con la t\\u00edpica arquitectura de las C\\u00edcladas. Adem\\u00e1s, desde el puerto, puedes tomar caiques (peque\\u00f1as embarcaciones) que te llevar\\u00e1n a las playas de Mykonos.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Callejuelas Encaladas y Molinos de Viento Fotog\\u00e9nicos: Al pasear por Chora, te sumergir\\u00e1s en un laberinto de callejuelas encaladas. Los ic\\u00f3nicos molinos de viento, t\\u00edpicos de las islas C\\u00edcladas, se alzan en la colina sobre la ciudad. Estos rincones pintorescos son perfectos para capturar fotograf\\u00edas memorables. Adem\\u00e1s, el aroma del pan reci\\u00e9n horneado que emana de las panader\\u00edas y los acogedores bares que sirven c\\u00f3cteles al atardecer te atraer\\u00e1n irresistiblemente.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Iglesia de Panagia Gremniotisas: Ubicada en el punto m\\u00e1s alto de Chora, esta hermosa iglesia ofrece una vista espectacular, especialmente al atardecer. Su nombre significa \\u201cVirgen Mar\\u00eda del Acantilado\\u201d y es considerada la protectora de la isla. La c\\u00fapula azul y el alto campanario la distinguen, y una palmera cercana la hace visible desde cualquier punto de Chora.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Museo Arqueol\\u00f3gico: Visitar el museo arqueol\\u00f3gico es una excelente manera de apreciar la rica historia de Ios. En una mansi\\u00f3n neocl\\u00e1sica, encontrar\\u00e1s piezas antiguas de las C\\u00edcladas, esculturas helen\\u00edsticas y romanas, as\\u00ed como reliquias de la Edad del Bronce medio y final.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Teatro Odysseus Elytis: Este teatro, nombrado en honor al famoso poeta griego Odysseus Elytis, se encuentra detr\\u00e1s de los molinos de viento sobre Chora. Con capacidad para 1,100 personas, es un lugar donde se organizan conciertos, obras de teatro y otros eventos culturales. Adem\\u00e1s, ofrece una vista impresionante del mar.\\r\\n\\r\\nExplorar Chora te permitir\\u00e1 sumergirte en la espiritualidad y la historia de esta hermosa isla griega. Es un lugar que combina la autenticidad de las C\\u00edcladas con una vibrante vida nocturna y una belleza serena. \\u00a1Explorar sus callejuelas y descubrir sus tesoros es una experiencia inolvidable!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CM_gyFp5duMq3VJLmrgXPK70w-Q1y3m6\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L3HOl_66bH1BTQTctFRgWA8bgzH8fEwt\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1AQvqr0q-MBTXgWmY6jd76uBo9-zpVKK3\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lBa3CC8iZrXVGwgMDMZyjh2XqpgnZeLW\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1MHNf2GVTwX9j0juBm2s35B_96RbGa4rI\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13Z9VjYrInpyZdvDEEPbMBNlntHEhwR0p\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 10:48:51', '2024-04-01 14:10:17'),
(158, 194, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/L43chCKgHMQ?si=bl1HFIc_j3TI05rY\",\"esdescription\":\"Laodicea, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Laodicea ad Lycum, fue una antigua ciudad del Imperio Sel\\u00e9ucida. Fue fundada entre 261 a.C. y 245 a.C. por el rey Ant\\u00edoco II Teos y bautizada as\\u00ed en honor de su esposa, Laodicea. La ciudad estaba situada a unos 6 kil\\u00f3metros al norte de la actual Denizli (Turqu\\u00eda), cerca del pueblo de Eskihisar. \\r\\nHe aqu\\u00ed algunos detalles fascinantes sobre Laodicea:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Prosperidad comercial: Laodicea prosper\\u00f3 como ciudad comercial pr\\u00f3spera. Ocupaba una posici\\u00f3n estrat\\u00e9gica en la intersecci\\u00f3n de dos importantes rutas comerciales y era famosa por sus tejidos de lana y algod\\u00f3n de alta calidad. La ciudad era un centro neur\\u00e1lgico para el comercio y la banca, y vend\\u00eda conocidos ung\\u00fcentos para los ojos y prendas de moda confeccionadas con excelente lana negra.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Evoluci\\u00f3n hist\\u00f3rica: Originalmente llamada Di\\u00f3polis (\\\"Ciudad de Zeus\\\"), m\\u00e1s tarde pas\\u00f3 a llamarse Roas. Ant\\u00edoco III el Grande traslad\\u00f3 a unas dos mil familias jud\\u00edas de Babilonia a Laodicea, lo que aument\\u00f3 considerablemente la importancia de la ciudad. En 188 a.C., pas\\u00f3 a estar bajo el dominio del reino de P\\u00e9rgamo, y en 133 a.C., cay\\u00f3 bajo el dominio romano.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Centro comercial: Laodicea se convirti\\u00f3 en una de las ciudades comerciales m\\u00e1s importantes de Asia Menor a finales de la Rep\\u00fablica Romana y principios de la \\u00e9poca imperial. Realizaba importantes transacciones monetarias y comerciaba con lana negra. La ciudad ostentaba el t\\u00edtulo de \\\"ciudad libre\\\" de Roma y era la cabeza de un conventus formado por otras 24 ciudades. Cabe destacar que Cicer\\u00f3n presidi\\u00f3 all\\u00ed causas judiciales hacia el a\\u00f1o 50 a.C.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Resistencia y reconstrucci\\u00f3n: A pesar de los frecuentes terremotos, Laodicea resisti\\u00f3. En 60\\/61 d.C., durante el reinado de Ner\\u00f3n, un devastador terremoto destruy\\u00f3 por completo la ciudad. Sorprendentemente, los habitantes rechazaron la ayuda imperial y la reconstruyeron ellos mismos.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Florecimiento cultural: La riqueza de Laodicea foment\\u00f3 el aprecio por el arte griego, evidente en sus ruinas. La ciudad contribuy\\u00f3 al progreso cient\\u00edfico y literario, con fil\\u00f3sofos notables como Ant\\u00edoco y Teodas (sucesores de Enesidemo) y una pr\\u00f3spera escuela de medicina. Tambi\\u00e9n fue cuna del sofista Polem\\u00f3n de Laodicea (ca. 90-144 d.C.).\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, la importancia hist\\u00f3rica de Laodicea, sus proezas comerciales y sus logros culturales la convierten en un cap\\u00edtulo cautivador de la historia de la antigua Anatolia.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1cOfRARYig_LCr2o7xiNwgVqM1BMB67gU\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1pJ6mu6IFVReXMxjrCOAJvHu013ELh01z\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1woqsSDCW98GPAa28yuretuzHppY9_LiM\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1etJuOBCEgy_SiP-ui4XjQKgJAY8Rmr1g\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ZHdu5jegMQGWqzQUJe_QJaqtaIhkE8kU\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1EpKDWxq_c44v6C1BhZwKguHtevpkvhim\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 11:42:08', '2024-04-01 14:39:17'),
(159, 198, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/WHKH3MmVuXA?si=scdbbhJphly67ytd\",\"esdescription\":\"Laodicea, also known as Laodicea ad Lycum, was an ancient city of the Seleucid Empire. It was founded between 261 BC and 245 BC by King Antiochus II Theos and named after his wife, Laodicea. The city was located about 6 kilometers north of present-day Denizli (Turkey), near the village of Eskihisar. \\r\\nHere are some fascinating details about Laodicea:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. commercial prosperity: Laodicea thrived as a prosperous commercial city. It occupied a strategic position at the intersection of two important trade routes and was famous for its high quality wool and cotton fabrics. The city was a hub for trade and banking, and sold well-known eye ointments and fashionable garments made from excellent black wool.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Historical evolution: Originally called Diopolis (\\\"City of Zeus\\\"), it was later renamed Roas. Antiochus III the Great moved about two thousand Jewish families from Babylon to Laodicea, which greatly increased the importance of the city. In 188 B.C., it came under the dominion of the kingdom of Pergamum, and in 133 B.C., it fell under Roman rule.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Commercial center: Laodicea became one of the most important commercial cities in Asia Minor at the end of the Roman Republic and the beginning of the imperial period. It carried out important monetary transactions and traded in black wool. The city held the title of \\\"free city\\\" of Rome and was the head of a conventus consisting of 24 other cities. It is worth noting that Cicero presided over court cases there around 50 BC.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Resistance and reconstruction: In spite of frequent earthquakes, Laodicea resisted. In 60\\/61 A.D., during the reign of Nero, a devastating earthquake completely destroyed the city. Surprisingly, the inhabitants refused imperial help and rebuilt it themselves.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Cultural flourishing: Laodicea\'s wealth fostered an appreciation for Greek art, evident in its ruins. The city contributed to scientific and literary progress, with notable philosophers such as Antiochus and Theodas (successors of Enesidemus) and a thriving medical school. It was also the birthplace of the sophist Polemus of Laodicea (ca. 90-144 AD).\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, Laodicea\'s historical importance, commercial prowess and cultural achievements make it a captivating chapter in the history of ancient Anatolia.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1cOfRARYig_LCr2o7xiNwgVqM1BMB67gU\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1pJ6mu6IFVReXMxjrCOAJvHu013ELh01z\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1woqsSDCW98GPAa28yuretuzHppY9_LiM\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1etJuOBCEgy_SiP-ui4XjQKgJAY8Rmr1g\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ZHdu5jegMQGWqzQUJe_QJaqtaIhkE8kU\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1EpKDWxq_c44v6C1BhZwKguHtevpkvhim\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 11:42:32', '2024-04-01 14:42:26'),
(160, 365, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/FtjtIJ1RPZ0?si=LRPTOCNj6ftH62RK6\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"El Templo de Atenea Nik\\u00e9, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como Atenea Victoriosa o como Templo de Atenea Nique, Nik\\u00e9 es una de las atracciones m\\u00e1s valiosas de Atenas y de Grecia en general. Conmemora la victoria de los griegos sobre los persas en la c\\u00e9lebre batalla de Salamina (480 a. C.). Este templo se encuentra en la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas, Grecia, y fue construido en el a\\u00f1o 421 a. C., tras la paz con los persas en 449 a. C. Aunque las obras se retrasaron debido a la negativa de Pericles, finalmente se llev\\u00f3 a cabo su construcci\\u00f3n.\\r\\nAlgunos detalles sobre el Templo de Atenea Nik\\u00e9:\\r\\n1. Dise\\u00f1o arquitect\\u00f3nico: Dise\\u00f1ado por el arquitecto Cal\\u00edcrates (es decir, el arquitecto que particip\\u00f3 tambi\\u00e9n en la construcci\\u00f3n del Parten\\u00f3n, s\\u00edmbolo del pa\\u00eds) es una estructura j\\u00f3nica tetr\\u00e1stila (con cuatro columnas) con un p\\u00f3rtico de columnatas en las fachadas delantera y trasera (anfipr\\u00f3stilo). Las columnas a lo largo de los frentes este y oeste eran monol\\u00edticas.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Ubicaci\\u00f3n estrat\\u00e9gica: Se encuentra en un basti\\u00f3n (torre\\u00f3n) de los Propileos, dominando la subida a la Acr\\u00f3polis.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Xoanon de Atenea Nik\\u00e9: En su interior, albergaba un xoanon, una imagen de Atenea personificada como Nik\\u00e9, la diosa alada s\\u00edmbolo de las victorias navales. Sus alas fueron cortadas (\\u00e1ptera significa sin alas) para que nunca pudiera abandonar la ciudad.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Restauraci\\u00f3n y reconstrucci\\u00f3n: Atenas cay\\u00f3 en manos del Imperio Otomano en 1456. El templo permaneci\\u00f3 intacto hasta 1686, cuando fue demolido por los otomanos. Sin embargo, en 1834, tras la independencia de Grecia, se reconstruy\\u00f3. En 1998, el templo fue \\u00edntegramente desmantelado para reemplazar el piso de concreto y su friso se traslad\\u00f3 al nuevo Museo de la Acr\\u00f3polis.\\r\\n\\r\\nEl Templo de Atenea Nik\\u00e9, aunque ha perdido parte de su entablamento y cubierta, lamentablemente, el templo ha sido parcialmente da\\u00f1ado a lo largo de los siglos.\\r\\nSin embargo, se trata sin duda de uno de los lugares de inter\\u00e9s m\\u00e1s importantes de la ciudad de Atenas y por esta raz\\u00f3n merece ser visitado, sigue siendo un testimonio visual de la grandeza de la antigua Grecia.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1M7xBr03ybKTL7Ig3Zd1xWtMylcCzb9hj\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Qij3kCcB8gQ_K7k_IK4Mquewzq69ACfq\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1gJh1dxeWMhx_gdjbI7BFm5zrLRxk-RXY\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Z-lSyjfmxheHggV5CMRk0u6o9ytroovj\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1h24cJQ2AjW6alV2-9l9avVWwep8priid\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wcvvvHBsWpYjj4jQmgt7DcZ1k-AxCx7z\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 14:26:04', '2024-04-02 10:37:15');
INSERT INTO `sight_media` (`id`, `sight_id`, `media_link`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(161, 33, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1pUQ2ppYK-PQKKKyilzldBXovAeh888f9\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Temple of Athena Nike, also known as Athena Victorious or as Temple of Athena Nique, Nike is one of the most valuable attractions of Athens and Greece in general. It commemorates the victory of the Greeks over the Persians in the famous battle of Salamis (480 BC). This temple is located on the Acropolis of Athens, Greece, and was built in 421 B.C., after the peace with the Persians in 449 B.C. Although the works were delayed due to the refusal of Pericles, its construction was finally carried out.\\r\\nSome details about the Temple of Athena Nike:\\r\\n1. Architectural design: designed by the architect Callicrates (i.e. the architect who was also involved in the construction of the Parthenon, symbol of the country) it is a tetrastyle Ionic structure (with four columns) with a colonnaded portico on the front and rear facades (amphiprostyle). The columns along the east and west fronts were monolithic.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Strategic location: It is located on a bastion (keep) of the Propylaea, dominating the ascent to the Acropolis.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Xoanon of Athena Nike: Inside, it housed a xoanon, an image of Athena personified as Nike, the winged goddess symbol of naval victories. Her wings were clipped (aptera means wingless) so that she could never leave the city.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Restoration and reconstruction: Athens fell into the hands of the Ottoman Empire in 1456. The temple remained intact until 1686, when it was demolished by the Ottomans. However, in 1834, after the independence of Greece, it was rebuilt. In 1998, the temple was completely dismantled to replace the concrete floor and its frieze was moved to the new Acropolis Museum.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Temple of Athena Nike, although it has lost part of its entablature and roof, unfortunately, the temple has been partially damaged over the centuries.\\r\\nHowever, it is undoubtedly one of the most important sights of the city of Athens and for this reason it deserves to be visited, it remains a visual testimony to the grandeur of ancient Greece.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1M7xBr03ybKTL7Ig3Zd1xWtMylcCzb9hj\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Qij3kCcB8gQ_K7k_IK4Mquewzq69ACfq\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1gJh1dxeWMhx_gdjbI7BFm5zrLRxk-RXY\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Z-lSyjfmxheHggV5CMRk0u6o9ytroovj\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1h24cJQ2AjW6alV2-9l9avVWwep8priid\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wcvvvHBsWpYjj4jQmgt7DcZ1k-AxCx7z\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 14:26:17', '2024-03-26 14:26:17'),
(162, 100, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/7-2Gcd9SE9Q?si=xQ4aIEOiSJKrgTT6\",\"esdescription\":\"El Parten\\u00f3n es un antiguo templo griego dedicado a la diosa Atenea, ubicado en la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas. Su construcci\\u00f3n comenz\\u00f3 en el a\\u00f1o 447 a.C. durante la Edad de Oro de Atenas y se complet\\u00f3 en el a\\u00f1o 438 a.C. El templo fue dise\\u00f1ado por los arquitectos Ictinos y Cal\\u00edcrates, mientras que el escultor Fidias supervis\\u00f3 los elementos art\\u00edsticos.\\r\\n\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes un resumen de la historia del Parten\\u00f3n:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Prop\\u00f3sito y Dedicaci\\u00f3n:\\r\\n\\r\\nEl Parten\\u00f3n fue construido como un templo dedicado a la diosa Atenea Parthenos, considerada la patrona de la ciudad de Atenas.\\r\\nEl nombre \\\"Parten\\u00f3n\\\" proviene de la palabra griega \\\"parthenos\\\", que significa virgen o doncella, reflejando la dedicaci\\u00f3n del templo a la diosa virgen Atenea.\\r\\n2. Caracter\\u00edsticas Arquitect\\u00f3nicas:\\r\\n\\r\\nEl Parten\\u00f3n es un templo d\\u00f3rico con ocho columnas en las fachadas y diecisiete columnas a lo largo de los lados.\\r\\nPresenta una cella (c\\u00e1mara interior) con una estatua de culto de Atenea, rodeada por un peristilo de columnas.\\r\\nEl exterior del templo est\\u00e1 adornado con intrincados frisos, metopas y frontones, que representan diversas escenas y eventos mitol\\u00f3gicos.\\r\\n3. Construcci\\u00f3n y Materiales:\\r\\n\\r\\nLa construcci\\u00f3n fue supervisada por el estadista Pericles como parte del ambicioso programa de construcci\\u00f3n en la Acr\\u00f3polis.\\r\\nEl templo se construy\\u00f3 utilizando m\\u00e1rmol pent\\u00e9lico extra\\u00eddo cerca de Atenas.\\r\\n4. Fases de Uso y Alteraciones:\\r\\n\\r\\nEl Parten\\u00f3n sirvi\\u00f3 como templo durante siglos, pero sufri\\u00f3 cambios basados en diferentes influencias culturales y religiosas.\\r\\nEn el siglo V d.C., fue convertido en una iglesia cristiana dedicada a la Virgen Mar\\u00eda (Parthena Maria).\\r\\nDurante el dominio otomano en el siglo XVII, se convirti\\u00f3 en una mezquita.\\r\\n5. Da\\u00f1o y Destrucci\\u00f3n:\\r\\n\\r\\nA lo largo de los siglos, el Parten\\u00f3n sufri\\u00f3 da\\u00f1os debido a guerras, terremotos y alteraciones.\\r\\nEn el siglo XIX, Lord Elgin retir\\u00f3 muchas esculturas del Parten\\u00f3n y las traslad\\u00f3 al Museo Brit\\u00e1nico de Londres, acci\\u00f3n que sigue siendo motivo de controversia.\\r\\n6. Restauraci\\u00f3n y Preservaci\\u00f3n:\\r\\n\\r\\nSe han realizado esfuerzos para preservar y restaurar el Parten\\u00f3n, incluyendo intentos de reconstrucci\\u00f3n y el uso de materiales modernos para reemplazar elementos da\\u00f1ados.\\r\\nEl Parten\\u00f3n, junto con otras estructuras en la Acr\\u00f3polis, ha sido objeto de proyectos continuos de restauraci\\u00f3n y conservaci\\u00f3n.\\r\\nA pesar de su historia tumultuosa, el Parten\\u00f3n se erige como un s\\u00edmbolo de la civilizaci\\u00f3n griega antigua y sigue siendo un hito cultural e hist\\u00f3rico significativo. Hoy en d\\u00eda, atrae a millones de visitantes de todo el mundo que se maravillan de su brillantez arquitect\\u00f3nica y su importancia hist\\u00f3rica.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1w9qxiDZvaTx7IGNL2eXu3ZnsJBcyo3dF\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13fi16Or7DNJxmad5JJdZ3rrzxiMj2UZM\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1iqJE7etQeA329bntoz_qXVp3mOvMk_QM\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1S37PXlNAEHu-PDc84vTSS9DUP9Rhq8OG\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12BLh-dZZ4cr8s0jHwUxhs9mrwPmO7Cma\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1OklXDgvHuythOvsp2ljjbBgNRVTeGWlj\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 14:41:27', '2024-04-01 22:55:01'),
(163, 30, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i8HY7rFqll5_okDXBQF_p3JdL93wKjFA\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Parthenon is an ancient Greek temple dedicated to the goddess Athena, located on the Acropolis of Athens. Its construction began in 447 BC during the Golden Age of Athens and was completed in 438 BC. The temple was designed by the architects Ictinos and Callicrates, while the sculptor Phidias supervised the artistic elements.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is a summary of the history of the Parthenon:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Purpose and Dedication:\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Parthenon was built as a temple dedicated to the goddess Athena Parthenos, considered the patron saint of the city of Athens.\\r\\nThe name \\\"Parthenon\\\" comes from the Greek word \\\"parthenos\\\", meaning virgin or maiden, reflecting the dedication of the temple to the virgin goddess Athena.\\r\\n2. Architectural Features:\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Parthenon is a Doric temple with eight columns on the facades and seventeen columns along the sides.\\r\\nIt features a cella (inner chamber) with a cult statue of Athena, surrounded by a peristyle of columns.\\r\\nThe exterior of the temple is adorned with intricate friezes, metopes and pediments, depicting various mythological scenes and events.\\r\\n3. Construction and Materials:\\r\\n\\r\\nConstruction was supervised by the statesman Pericles as part of the ambitious building program on the Acropolis.\\r\\nThe temple was built using Pentelic marble quarried near Athens.\\r\\n4. Phases of Use and Alterations:\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Parthenon served as a temple for centuries, but underwent changes based on different cultural and religious influences.\\r\\nIn the 5th century AD, it was converted into a Christian church dedicated to the Virgin Mary (Parthena Maria).\\r\\nDuring Ottoman rule in the 17th century, it became a mosque.\\r\\n5. Damage and Destruction:\\r\\n\\r\\nOver the centuries, the Parthenon suffered damage due to wars, earthquakes and alterations.\\r\\nIn the 19th century, Lord Elgin removed many sculptures from the Parthenon and moved them to the British Museum in London, an action that remains controversial.\\r\\n6. Restoration and Preservation:\\r\\n\\r\\nEfforts have been made to preserve and restore the Parthenon, including attempts at reconstruction and the use of modern materials to replace damaged elements.\\r\\nThe Parthenon, along with other structures on the Acropolis, has been the subject of ongoing restoration and conservation projects.\\r\\nDespite its tumultuous history, the Parthenon stands as a symbol of ancient Greek civilization and remains a significant cultural and historical landmark. Today, it attracts millions of visitors from around the world who marvel at its architectural brilliance and historical importance.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1w9qxiDZvaTx7IGNL2eXu3ZnsJBcyo3dF\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13fi16Or7DNJxmad5JJdZ3rrzxiMj2UZM\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1iqJE7etQeA329bntoz_qXVp3mOvMk_QM\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1S37PXlNAEHu-PDc84vTSS9DUP9Rhq8OG\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12BLh-dZZ4cr8s0jHwUxhs9mrwPmO7Cma\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1OklXDgvHuythOvsp2ljjbBgNRVTeGWlj\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 14:42:11', '2024-03-26 14:42:11'),
(164, 366, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/ikpyDHGnMEc?si=zTtvHxuKAB0peKHw\",\"esdescription\":\"El Ode\\u00f3n de Herodes \\u00c1tico es un monumento impresionante situado en la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas. Fue erigido en el a\\u00f1o 161, era un edificio para audiciones musicales, construido por la gran fortuna del c\\u00f3nsul romano Herodes \\u00c1tico, quien lo mand\\u00f3 construir en memoria de su esposa, Apia Ania Regila, fallecida el a\\u00f1o anterior. \\r\\nAlgunos detalles sobre el Ode\\u00f3n de Herodes \\u00c1tico:\\r\\n- Dise\\u00f1o Irregular: Este edificio se encuentra junto al Teatro de Dioniso, en el extremo oeste de la pendiente sur de la Acr\\u00f3polis, est\\u00e1 ubicado junto al Teatro de Dioniso. Su planta es similar a la de un teatro romano, pero con la diferencia de que el Ode\\u00f3n estaba cubierto. Ten\\u00eda un aforo en sus gradas para cinco mil espectadores, en una ciudad con una poblaci\\u00f3n de aproximadamente 290,000 habitantes.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Escenario y Construcci\\u00f3n: Su construcci\\u00f3n dur\\u00f3 aproximadamente diez a\\u00f1os. El escenario estaba hecho de m\\u00e1rmol blanco y cipol\\u00edn (un tipo de m\\u00e1rmol) y ten\\u00eda una longitud de 35 metros, es similar a la de un teatro romano, con la diferencia de que el ode\\u00f3n estaba cubierto. La construcci\\u00f3n fue extremadamente costosa, con testimonios antiguos que mencionan la madera de cedro utilizada en el techo. Sorprendentemente, el techo de la c\\u00e1vea, con 38 metros de radio, no ten\\u00eda soportes internos, lo que constituye un alarde constructivo incluso en la actualidad. Pero en capacidad era peque\\u00f1o, ten\\u00eda un aforo en sus gradas para cinco mil espectadores.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Estado Actual: El Ode\\u00f3n fue destruido en el a\\u00f1o 267 debido a la invasi\\u00f3n de los H\\u00e9rulos y, a diferencia de otros edificios da\\u00f1ados, nunca fue reconstruido. Sin embargo, en 1952-1953, se llev\\u00f3 a cabo una restauraci\\u00f3n del monumento. Desde 1957, el Ode\\u00f3n ha albergado diversos espect\\u00e1culos art\\u00edsticos, como conciertos, teatro, \\u00f3peras y danza. El Festival de Atenas se celebra all\\u00ed cada a\\u00f1o de mayo a septiembre, forjando su reputaci\\u00f3n internacional.\\r\\n\\r\\nEste tesoro arquitect\\u00f3nico es un testimonio de la rica historia y la habilidad de la antigua Roma. Si alguna vez tienes la oportunidad de visitar la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas, aseg\\u00farate de admirar la belleza y la significancia del Ode\\u00f3n de Herodes \\u00c1tico.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1vwF1rGAEe_ReWAbekMmZc6x8Y-yvH6Vz\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WyKoyrWvptgJg_wmqtuuTOOvNpGuycf0\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1s0Iy_dAFlfgVB4abLfFn1KpgeVJEdejB\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L1ZcF152q3vY--zQIqfXwcf20QTLbqMB\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1cgRqVPcw9ZdtiL48sRmKiNsd2z4AJWnS\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ldHSPvo868g_Vx9NU5lFQmEVTbh4Beft\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 14:54:06', '2024-04-02 12:11:40'),
(165, 35, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ZI7nXcmzZAE0gcHvwHWbjHZ_5RAY-RJC\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Odeon of Herodes Atticus is an impressive monument located on the Acropolis of Athens. It was erected in 161, it was a building for musical auditions, built by the great fortune of the Roman consul Herodes Atticus, who had it built in memory of his wife, Apia Ania Regila, who died the previous year. \\r\\nSome details about the Odeon of Herodes Atticus:\\r\\n- Irregular Design: This building is located next to the Theater of Dionysus, at the western end of the southern slope of the Acropolis, it is located next to the Theater of Dionysus. Its plan is similar to that of a Roman theater, but with the difference that the Odeon was covered. It had a seating capacity for five thousand spectators in a city with a population of approximately 290,000 inhabitants.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Stage and Construction: Its construction lasted approximately ten years. The stage was made of white marble and cipolin (a type of marble) and had a length of 35 meters, similar to that of a Roman theater, with the difference that the odeon was covered. The construction was extremely expensive, with ancient testimonies mentioning the cedar wood used in the roof. Surprisingly, the roof of the cavea, with a radius of 38 meters, had no internal supports, which is a constructive boast even today. But its capacity was small, it had a seating capacity for five thousand spectators.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Current State: The Odeon was destroyed in 267 due to the invasion of the Heruli and, unlike other damaged buildings, it was never rebuilt. However, in 1952-1953, a restoration of the monument was carried out. Since 1957, the Odeon has hosted various artistic performances, including concerts, theater, operas and dance. The Athens Festival is held there every year from May to September, forging its international reputation.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis architectural treasure is a testament to the rich history and skill of ancient Rome. If you ever have the opportunity to visit the Acropolis of Athens, be sure to admire the beauty and significance of the Odeon of Herodes Atticus.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1vwF1rGAEe_ReWAbekMmZc6x8Y-yvH6Vz\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WyKoyrWvptgJg_wmqtuuTOOvNpGuycf0\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1s0Iy_dAFlfgVB4abLfFn1KpgeVJEdejB\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1L1ZcF152q3vY--zQIqfXwcf20QTLbqMB\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1cgRqVPcw9ZdtiL48sRmKiNsd2z4AJWnS\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ldHSPvo868g_Vx9NU5lFQmEVTbh4Beft\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 14:54:32', '2024-03-26 14:54:32'),
(166, 367, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/pyrX527Snn8?si=oeQJm6M_ujRqkQAj\",\"esdescription\":\"La Calle de los Curetes en \\u00c9feso, Turqu\\u00eda, es una v\\u00eda empedrada que se extiende alrededor de la cual se encuentran la mayor\\u00eda de las ruinas, ruinas y monumentos que cuentan la historia de la civilizaci\\u00f3n cl\\u00e1sica. Es una de las calles principales de esta antigua ciudad. Este tramo de calle es como un museo al aire libre, con restos de viviendas, templos, columnas y un suelo que a\\u00fan conserva los mosaicos que alguna vez lo adornaron.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Historia y cultura: La Calle de los Curetes es como un museo al aire libre, donde cada piedra cuenta una historia. Puedes explorar y maravillarte ante la grandeza de la antigua \\u00c9feso mientras caminas por esta calle hist\\u00f3rica.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Curetes: Los curetes eran sacerdotes y guardianes de la diosa Artemisa, y esta calle lleva su nombre. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Dise\\u00f1o: La calle est\\u00e1 hecha mayormente de m\\u00e1rmol y se encuentra encima del sistema de alcantarillas, est\\u00e1 rebosantes de mosaicos y galer\\u00edas de columnas. Hay dos pilares de m\\u00e1rmol que se encuentran en lados opuestos de los caminos, estos pilares forman la Puerta de H\\u00e9rcules, decorada con fascinantes relieves que representan al famoso hombre fuerte, estos pilares dan la entrada a la calle Curetes, la calle conecta toda la ciudad de \\u00c9feso guiaban a los visitantes hacia las tiendas y hogares en la ciudad.\\r\\n\\r\\nTodo lo que queda de su ubicaci\\u00f3n son los pilares que han permanecido a trav\\u00e9s del tiempo.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Excavaciones: Aunque solo se ha desenterrado el 20% de lo que alguna vez fue esta gran metr\\u00f3poli, su esplendor como capital de la Asia Menor romana es innegable.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Mosaicos: El suelo de la Calle de los Curetes a\\u00fan conserva los mosaicos que alguna vez adornaron esta v\\u00eda. Estos intrincados dise\\u00f1os agregan un toque de belleza y color al entorno.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Tiendas y talleres antiguos: A medida que caminas por la calle, ver\\u00e1s los restos de antiguas tiendas y talleres. Imagina a los comerciantes y artesanos que una vez ocuparon estos espacios, vendiendo sus productos y creando obras de arte.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u00c9feso, con su rica historia y magn\\u00edficos restos arqueol\\u00f3gicos, es un lugar que nos permite imaginar la grandeza del mundo cl\\u00e1sico. No te pierdas la experiencia de caminar por la Calle de los Curetes y admirar los mosaicos y las huellas del pasado que a\\u00fan perduran en sus piedras.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12lJ7Z_8OjeZEpDOLaPrHDHCyq9j_HhED\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1GeOa6Kc9DHZHQNTfAkW1K9eJfha6918a\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1j-VKbuCr1XSXirm9H5BeOPE5uo9B4L0K\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/18T8TNgvpAqI4737WbD6MguLQMFC-NlIX\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-oZ9aZRQZsBQyKV2RzayUtfF2Bc1Lf6T\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/19x467Mu5PE-tNoKOATmF9svp1A8DYLkK\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 15:37:42', '2024-04-01 15:51:48'),
(167, 368, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/-OznNn_gey0?si=HvlzOz1q-9jQfIDJ\",\"esdescription\":\"The Street of the Curetes in Ephesus, Turkey, is a cobblestone road that extends around which are most of the ruins, ruins and monuments that tell the story of classical civilization. It is one of the main streets of this ancient city. This stretch of street is like an open-air museum, with the remains of houses, temples, columns and a floor that still preserves the mosaics that once adorned it.\\r\\n\\r\\n- History and culture: The Calle de los Curetes is like an open-air museum, where every stone tells a story. You can explore and marvel at the grandeur of ancient Ephesus as you walk down this historic street.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Curetes: The Curetes were priests and guardians of the goddess Artemis, and this street is named after her. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Layout: The street is made mostly of marble and sits above the sewer system, it is overflowing with mosaics and columned galleries. There are two marble pillars that stand on opposite sides of the roads, these pillars form the Gate of Hercules, decorated with fascinating reliefs depicting the famous strongman, these pillars give the entrance to Curetes Street, the street connects the entire city of Ephesus and guided visitors to the stores and homes in the city.\\r\\n\\r\\nAll that remains of their location are the pillars that have remained through time.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Excavations: Although only 20% of what was once this great metropolis has been unearthed, its splendor as the capital of Roman Asia Minor is undeniable.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Mosaics: The floor of the Calle de los Curetes still preserves the mosaics that once adorned this thoroughfare. These intricate designs add a touch of beauty and color to the surroundings.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Old stores and workshops: As you walk down the street, you will see the remains of old stores and workshops. Imagine the merchants and artisans who once occupied these spaces, selling their wares and creating works of art.\\r\\n\\r\\nEphesus, with its rich history and magnificent archaeological remains, is a place that allows us to imagine the grandeur of the classical world. Don\'t miss the experience of walking along the Street of Curetes and admiring the mosaics and traces of the past that still remain in its stones.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12lJ7Z_8OjeZEpDOLaPrHDHCyq9j_HhED\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1GeOa6Kc9DHZHQNTfAkW1K9eJfha6918a\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1j-VKbuCr1XSXirm9H5BeOPE5uo9B4L0K\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/18T8TNgvpAqI4737WbD6MguLQMFC-NlIX\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-oZ9aZRQZsBQyKV2RzayUtfF2Bc1Lf6T\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/19x467Mu5PE-tNoKOATmF9svp1A8DYLkK\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 15:37:58', '2024-04-01 15:48:51'),
(168, 369, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/3aF-JfuZGHE?si=hJSmcyFqY2FSy8lY\",\"esdescription\":\"La calle del puerto de \\u00c9feso es un lugar lleno de historia y encanto, era una v\\u00eda vital en la antigua ciudad de \\u00c9feso, situada junto al Mar Egeo. Aunque en la actualidad no se puede ver el mar desde all\\u00ed, en la Antig\\u00fcedad, esta calle conduc\\u00eda al puerto y terminaba justo al lado del Gran Teatro de \\u00c9feso. Imagina a Cleopatra y Marco Antonio caminando por esta misma calle, quedando impresionados al encontrarse de frente con el majestuoso teatro.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles interesantes:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Gran Ciudad Portuaria: Durante la \\u00e9poca romana, el puerto de \\u00c9feso gener\\u00f3 una gran ciudad con m\\u00e1s de 250,000 habitantes. Desde este puerto, tres carreteras principales se extend\\u00edan hacia diferentes direcciones: una al sur hacia el valle del Meandro, otra al este hacia Babilonia a trav\\u00e9s de Laodicea y una tercera al norte a trav\\u00e9s de Esmirna.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Importancia Comercial: \\u00c9feso fue uno de los principales centros comerciales del mundo romano. Su puerto era conocido como \\u201cla puerta por la que Occidente visitaba Oriente\\u201d. Estrab\\u00f3n, un historiador antiguo, lo describi\\u00f3 como el enlace m\\u00e1s importante entre Oriente y Occidente, conectando Italia y Grecia con Asia Menor.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Tiendas y comercios: Aunque las tiendas y galer\\u00edas comerciales que sol\\u00edan alinearse a lo largo de la Calle del Puerto apenas conservan sus cimientos, imaginar el bullicio y la actividad comercial de anta\\u00f1o nos transporta a una \\u00e9poca llena de historia y vida.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Monumentos y Calle Principal: La calle del puerto comenzaba en el teatro y terminaba en el propio puerto. A lo largo de sus 530 metros, estaba iluminada por antorchas y bordeada por comercios y pasos peatonales. A lo largo de la calle se alzaban comercios y pasos peatonales.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Declive y Abandono: Aunque en su apogeo, el puerto estaba activo y pr\\u00f3spero, con el tiempo, la sedimentaci\\u00f3n causada por el r\\u00edo Caystros (K\\u00fc\\u00e7\\u00fck Menderes) lo cubri\\u00f3 con tierra y polvo. La llanura aluvial del r\\u00edo Cayster se desplaz\\u00f3 hacia el mar, lo que provoc\\u00f3 la decadencia de \\u00c9feso. Las epidemias de malaria y el abandono gradual de la ciudad llevaron a la creaci\\u00f3n de un nuevo puerto llamado \\u201cScala Nuova\\u201d (nuevo puerto) en lugar de revitalizar \\u00c9feso.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Actualidad: Hoy en d\\u00eda, la calle del puerto es la entrada principal a las ruinas de \\u00c9feso, y sigue siendo un lugar que evoca la grandeza y la vida de la antigua ciudad. Al caminar por esta calle, podr\\u00edas ver algunos de los lugares ic\\u00f3nicos de \\u00c9feso, como la Fuente de Trajano, donde las personas sin suministro de agua en sus casas pod\\u00edan beber agua.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, la calle del puerto de \\u00c9feso fue un punto crucial en la historia comercial y cultural de la antigua ciudad, aunque hoy en d\\u00eda las ruinas est\\u00e1n alejadas de la costa y el puerto est\\u00e1 cubierto de tierra y olvido, es un testimonio tangible de la rica historia y cultura de esta antigua ciudad y te transporta a trav\\u00e9s de los siglos mientras exploras sus ruinas.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lBWkMl2dkB8Oy9PG53JYSkFwqRgR7yaN\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1rYdnD0lbRxrM0brmKtuu8Pl2tgXn4L7m\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1gQ0Xrt_V7-bfmiHnz1IC5mE_AtNw02rT\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zga2MPs1Knc4dLk1GDvywOKpsroRxhP6\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ax1-CWKA-inUdxdCqlC6jNs1CGZ6nPB2\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/19k-rzCkW6fLN7xkJGDK8j3OoRdBWPomn\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 15:57:12', '2024-04-02 11:19:24'),
(169, 370, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zwzMnoDYO4j0mlJj4qybih2XifsRYpk8\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"Ephesus Harbor Street is a place full of history and charm, it was a vital thoroughfare in the ancient city of Ephesus, located by the Aegean Sea. Although today you can\'t see the sea from there, in ancient times, this street led to the harbor and ended right next to the Great Theater of Ephesus. Imagine Cleopatra and Mark Antony walking down this very street, being impressed as they came face to face with the majestic theater.\\r\\nHere are some interesting details:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Great Port City: During Roman times, the port of Ephesus spawned a large city with more than 250,000 inhabitants. From this port, three main roads extended in different directions: one south to the Meander Valley, one east to Babylon via Laodicea and a third north through Smyrna.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Commercial Importance: Ephesus was one of the main commercial centers of the Roman world. Its port was known as \\\"the gateway through which the West visited the East\\\". Strabo, an ancient historian, described it as the most important link between East and West, connecting Italy and Greece with Asia Minor.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Stores and businesses: Although the stores and commercial galleries that used to line Port Street barely retain their foundations, imagining the bustle and commercial activity of yesteryear takes us back to a time full of history and life.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Monuments and Main Street: Port Street began at the theater and ended at the port itself. Along its 530 meters, it was illuminated by torches and lined with stores and crosswalks. Stores and crosswalks lined the street.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Decline and Abandonment: Although in its heyday, the port was active and prosperous, over time, siltation caused by the Caystros River (K\\u00fc\\u00e7\\u00fck Menderes) covered it with dirt and dust. The alluvial plain of the Cayster River shifted towards the sea, which led to the decline of Ephesus. Malaria epidemics and the gradual abandonment of the city led to the creation of a new port called \\\"Scala Nuova\\\" (new port) instead of revitalizing Ephesus.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Present day: Today, the harbor street is the main entrance to the ruins of Ephesus, and remains a place that evokes the grandeur and life of the ancient city. As you walk along this street, you might see some of Ephesus\' iconic sites, such as Trajan\'s Fountain, where people without water supply in their homes could drink water.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, Ephesus Harbor Street was a crucial point in the commercial and cultural history of the ancient city, although today the ruins are far from the coast and the harbor is covered with dirt and oblivion, it is a tangible testimony to the rich history and culture of this ancient city and transports you through the centuries as you explore its ruins.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1lBWkMl2dkB8Oy9PG53JYSkFwqRgR7yaN\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1rYdnD0lbRxrM0brmKtuu8Pl2tgXn4L7m\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1gQ0Xrt_V7-bfmiHnz1IC5mE_AtNw02rT\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zga2MPs1Knc4dLk1GDvywOKpsroRxhP6\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ax1-CWKA-inUdxdCqlC6jNs1CGZ6nPB2\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/19k-rzCkW6fLN7xkJGDK8j3OoRdBWPomn\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 15:57:37', '2024-03-26 15:57:37'),
(170, 32, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1p4CLVIKlVGfy4-UIKO7ETK_DDbVyK48C\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Propylaea, also known as the Propylaia, are the only western access to the Acropolis of Athens. They were built between 437 and 431 BC as part of Pericles\' monumental program and replaced the ancient Propylaia of the time of Pisistratus. These impressive architectural monuments combine Doric and Ionic elements in a monumental structure.\\r\\nThe columns, friezes and pediments of the Propylaea reflect the splendor and sophistication of classical Greek art, making them a masterpiece of ancient architecture.\\r\\nSome details about the Propylaea:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Architectural design: the architect Mnesicles projected a monumental majestic entrance with columns to an enclosed enclosure, they had a wall or also called the porticoes as if they were facades of Doric temples.\\r\\nThe outer portico has a platform on each side with three Ionic columns that supported a blue marble roof with stars.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Strategic location: The Propylaea are located on the western slope of the sacred rock and constitute the entrance to the interior of the fortification of the Acropolis. Their construction was a challenge due to the difficult topographical conditions.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Original purpose: They were to replace the simple propylon built under Pisistratus and to crown the steep road leading to the top of the upper city on its southern side.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Unfinished: Although begun in 437 BC, work on the Propylaea was interrupted in 432 BC, a year before the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Propylaea, with its impressive design and its crucial role in the history of the Acropolis, remains a visual testimony to the grandeur of ancient Greece.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1OCca3gfxYoopiJ4y0ilmqmQoTZ3_Wjwr\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1vWi8VmGFSuy8Zd-uSdfiakyaoQ676mxc\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1A-2uIXUEhgOKFh3OCqQE_v0b_TWDgv0G\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1EbcjDr6mCN88UphzsV6kQ-mjOXe_BfXG\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1JGzUm1LZ-lstGe7uVihm07tpZ7_ESYXq\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/129C0ZypdlfoJp3JwuJ77xuI6okkZNAje\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 16:34:01', '2024-03-26 16:34:01'),
(171, 34, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16JQsA7MRT2uQE-DmwqBsaSX9er3KOV3H\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Erechtheion, also known as the Erechtheion (Greek \\u1f18\\u03c1\\u03ad\\u03c7\\u03b8\\u03b5\\u03b9\\u03bf\\u03bd), is a Greek temple located on the north side of the Acropolis of Athens, opposite the Temple of Athena Nike. It was created in honor of the gods Athena Polias and the god of the seas Poseidon, as well as the mythical king of the city Erechtheus. This magnificent temple, of Ionic order, is one of the most beautiful Greek architectural monuments and is made of Pentelic marble. Its construction took place between 421 BC and 406 BC.\\r\\nHere are some details about the Erechtheion:\\r\\n- Irregular Design: Due to the slope of the land, the Erechtheion has an irregular plan and consists of three porticoes. One of them, on the south side, is the famous tribune of the Caryatids, which indicates the tomb of the mythical king Cecrope. It is one of the most beautiful Greek architectural monuments. It is made of Pentelic marble.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Multiple Sanctuaries: The temple is not dedicated to a single divinity, but consists of several sanctuaries. Three of these sanctuaries are dedicated to Athena, Poseidon and Zeus. The other two sanctuaries are supposed to be dedicated to Cecrope and Erechtheus, two mythical kings of Attica.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Legends and Relics: In this place, according to legend, the dispute between Athena and Poseidon took place. You can see the mark of Poseidon\'s trident on a rock. In addition, the Erechtheion housed sacred relics, such as the Palladium (a statue of Athena fallen from the sky), the tombs of C\\u00e9crope and Erechtheus, a chapel dedicated to Pandrous (daughter of C\\u00e9crope), a saltwater well and the sacred olive tree, which Athena supposedly gave to the people after her dispute with Poseidon.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis temple is an impressive testimony to the rich history and architectural skill of ancient Greece. If you ever have the opportunity to visit the Acropolis of Athens, be sure to admire the beauty and significance of the Erechtheion.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1d_24vsuY_iagSJYggLukP1uC3djgOX_5\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1JR1Ix1-taW48QH67xcdyzTI3XWNEW8Jd\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/181ZJI0sheaPfo1poBr3zZTuK-1U7Eg1Y\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1RuvgJsFva3n-3t56aWdCgAz2s13KgJXh\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1DH-OKNFF4ebqH-1APLSEtN2rFPe1Hpiv\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aLw6zXWhe_58tponJUtmxfQHF7zISlPM\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 16:53:04', '2024-03-26 16:53:04'),
(172, 371, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/4sg_Dh3VZwQ?si=5UYqtzMwx0uQum5t\",\"esdescription\":\"El Erectei\\u00f3n, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como Erecteion (en griego \\u1f18\\u03c1\\u03ad\\u03c7\\u03b8\\u03b5\\u03b9\\u03bf\\u03bd), es un templo griego situado en el lado norte de la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas, en frente al Templo de Atenea Nik\\u00e9. Fue creado en honor a los dioses Atenea Polias y el dios de los mares Poseid\\u00f3n, as\\u00ed como al rey m\\u00edtico de la ciudad Erecteo. Este magn\\u00edfico templo, de orden j\\u00f3nico, es uno de los m\\u00e1s bellos monumentos arquitect\\u00f3nicos griegos y est\\u00e1 hecho de m\\u00e1rmol pent\\u00e9lico. Su construcci\\u00f3n tuvo lugar entre los a\\u00f1os 421 a. C. y 406 a. C.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed hay algunos detalles sobre el Erectei\\u00f3n:\\r\\n- Dise\\u00f1o Irregular: Debido al declive del terreno, el Erectei\\u00f3n tiene una planta irregular y consta de tres p\\u00f3rticos. Uno de ellos, en la cara sur, es la famosa tribuna de las Cari\\u00e1tides, que indica la tumba del m\\u00edtico rey C\\u00e9crope. Es uno de los m\\u00e1s bellos monumentos arquitect\\u00f3nicos griegos. Est\\u00e1 hecho de m\\u00e1rmol pent\\u00e9lico.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Santuarios M\\u00faltiples: El templo no est\\u00e1 dedicado a una \\u00fanica divinidad, sino que est\\u00e1 constituido por varios santuarios. Tres de estos santuarios est\\u00e1n dedicados a Atenea, Poseid\\u00f3n y Zeus. Se supone que los otros dos santuarios est\\u00e1n dedicados a C\\u00e9crope y Erecteo, dos reyes m\\u00edticos del \\u00c1tica.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Leyendas y Reliquias: En este lugar, seg\\u00fan la leyenda, tuvo lugar la disputa entre Atenea y Poseid\\u00f3n. Se puede ver la marca del tridente de Poseid\\u00f3n sobre una roca. Adem\\u00e1s, el Erectei\\u00f3n albergaba reliquias sagradas, como el Paladio (una estatua de Atenea ca\\u00edda del cielo), las tumbas de C\\u00e9crope y Erecteo, una capilla dedicada a P\\u00e1ndroso (hija de C\\u00e9crope), un pozo de agua salada y el olivo sagrado, que Atenea supuestamente dio a la poblaci\\u00f3n tras su disputa con Poseid\\u00f3n.\\r\\n\\r\\nEste templo es un testimonio impresionante de la rica historia y la habilidad arquitect\\u00f3nica de la antigua Grecia. Si alguna vez tienes la oportunidad de visitar la Acr\\u00f3polis de Atenas, aseg\\u00farate de admirar la belleza y la significancia del Erectei\\u00f3n.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1d_24vsuY_iagSJYggLukP1uC3djgOX_5\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1JR1Ix1-taW48QH67xcdyzTI3XWNEW8Jd\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/181ZJI0sheaPfo1poBr3zZTuK-1U7Eg1Y\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1RuvgJsFva3n-3t56aWdCgAz2s13KgJXh\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1DH-OKNFF4ebqH-1APLSEtN2rFPe1Hpiv\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aLw6zXWhe_58tponJUtmxfQHF7zISlPM\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 16:53:44', '2024-04-02 11:08:32'),
(173, 374, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/52Enxuj30rA?si=klq7gH8T3AqxgTky\",\"esdescription\":\"La isla de Hidra (en griego: \\u038e\\u03b4\\u03c1\\u03b1) es una peque\\u00f1a isla griega ubicada en el golfo Sar\\u00f3nico, en el mar Egeo, al sur de Atenas, conocida por su belleza natural y su rica historia. Tiene una ciudad principal, conocida simplemente como Puerto Hidra. Consiste en un puerto de forma de media luna, alrededor del cual est\\u00e1n la playa y los establecimientos comerciales todo lo cual satisface a los turistas y sus habitantes.\\r\\n Se encuentra frente a la pen\\u00ednsula de la Arg\\u00f3lida, entre los golfos de Sar\\u00f3nico y Arg\\u00f3lico. Hidra est\\u00e1 separada del Peloponeso por el estrecho del golfo de Hidra. \\r\\n\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles sobre esta encantadora isla:\\r\\n- Geograf\\u00eda: Hidra tiene una extensi\\u00f3n de 64,44 km\\u00b2 y un per\\u00edmetro costero de 55 km. Es una isla monta\\u00f1osa con menos vegetaci\\u00f3n que otras islas del Argosar\\u00f3nico. Su punto m\\u00e1s alto es el Monte Eros o Vigla, que culmina a 593 metros. Excepto por tres ensenadas, la costa de la isla es rocosa e inh\\u00f3spita, mientras que el interior es monta\\u00f1oso y poco f\\u00e9rtil. Las colinas grises a menudo terminan en acantilados junto al mar.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Transporte y Vida en la Isla: Los veh\\u00edculos no est\\u00e1n permitidos en Hidra, lo que hace que el transporte p\\u00fablico se limite al burro y los taxis acu\\u00e1ticos. La zona habitada es compacta, y la mayor\\u00eda de las personas se desplazan a pie. La ciudad principal, conocida simplemente como Puerto Hidra, cuenta con un puerto en forma de media luna rodeado de playas y establecimientos comerciales. Empinadas calles de piedra conducen desde el puerto, y la mayor\\u00eda de las residencias locales est\\u00e1n ubicadas en estas calles. Hidra fue una de las grandes potencias navales del Mediterr\\u00e1neo en la \\u00e9poca moderna, y sus armadores y navieros desempe\\u00f1aron un papel crucial en la Guerra de independencia de Grecia.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Historia y Encanto: Durante la Antig\\u00fcedad, la isla era conocida como Hidrea (\\u03a5\\u03b4\\u03c1\\u03b5\\u03b1), en referencia a los manantiales. Hoy en d\\u00eda, Hidra es un lugar cosmopolita, ideal para vacaciones y descanso. Los atenienses constituyen una parte importante de los visitantes. Los barcos llegan regularmente desde El Pireo y la cercana costa del Peloponeso. La belleza pintoresca de Hidra, su arquitectura neocl\\u00e1sica y su historia mar\\u00edtima la convierten en un destino encantador para explorar y ver algunos lugares de inter\\u00e9s como: \\r\\n\\r\\n1. Archivos Hist\\u00f3ricos del Museo de Hidra: Explora este museo para sumergirte en la historia de la isla y descubrir artefactos y documentos hist\\u00f3ricos.\\r\\n2. Museo Eclesi\\u00e1stico y Bizantino de Hidra: Este museo alberga una colecci\\u00f3n de arte religioso y objetos bizantinos, perfecto para los amantes de la cultura y la historia.\\r\\n3. Monasterio de la Asunci\\u00f3n de la Virgen Mar\\u00eda: Un lugar tranquilo rodeado de vegetaci\\u00f3n, ideal para reflexionar y apreciar la arquitectura religiosa.\\r\\n4. Mansi\\u00f3n hist\\u00f3rica de Lazaros Kountouriotis: Visita esta mansi\\u00f3n para conocer m\\u00e1s sobre la vida y la historia de la familia Kountouriotis.\\r\\n5. Mansi\\u00f3n de George Kountouriotis: Otra impresionante mansi\\u00f3n hist\\u00f3rica que vale la pena explorar.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u00a1La isla de Hidra es un tesoro griego que merece la pena descubrir!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1HAqs3bATkRIULAgZfbbn_AQc2joE6FRt\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TfyOrdeYCGuN2HzKbJI_D4KiZADJSk1k\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zL2noS7c0bDvJt3Ix7MyJZlMqlFPDszZ\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1X6a-kN3Ge0qd17xEMkSHCBkD0Cr2Ebim\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IFrnUbFLwbN0PUxYeeoE-ojiLz_CeD7B\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/11Ut0ghAqfRJsYAtm0m25t8g7kDRKobiG\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 20:43:38', '2024-04-02 11:22:22'),
(174, 375, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qK4Cq7GquGjYEtO_ELnCBnHPFKLYNo2V\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The island of Hydra (Greek: \\u038e\\u03b4\\u03c1\\u03b1) is a small Greek island located in the Saronic Gulf, in the Aegean Sea, south of Athens, known for its natural beauty and rich history. It has one main town, known simply as Port Hydra. It consists of a crescent-shaped harbor, around which are the beach and commercial establishments all of which satisfy tourists and its inhabitants.\\r\\n It is located off the Argolid peninsula, between the Saronic and Argolic gulfs. Hydra is separated from the Peloponnese by the strait of the Gulf of Hydra. \\r\\n\\r\\nHere are some details about this charming island:\\r\\n- Geography: Hydra has an area of 64.44 km\\u00b2 and a coastal perimeter of 55 km. It is a mountainous island with less vegetation than other Argosaronic islands. Its highest point is Mount Eros or Vigla, which peaks at 593 meters. Except for three inlets, the island\'s coast is rocky and inhospitable, while the interior is mountainous and unfertile. The gray hills often end in cliffs by the sea.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Transportation and Island Life: Vehicles are not allowed on Hydra, making public transportation limited to donkey and water cabs. The inhabited area is compact, and most people get around on foot. The main town, known simply as Port Hydra, has a crescent-shaped harbor surrounded by beaches and commercial establishments. Steep stone streets lead from the harbor, and most of the local residences are located on these streets. Hydra was one of the great naval powers of the Mediterranean in modern times, and its shipowners and shipbuilders played a crucial role in the Greek War of Independence.\\r\\n\\r\\n- History and Charm: During Antiquity, the island was known as Hydrea (\\u03a5\\u03b4\\u03c1\\u03b5\\u03b1), in reference to the springs. Today, Hydra is a cosmopolitan place, ideal for vacations and relaxation. Athenians constitute an important part of the visitors. Boats arrive regularly from Piraeus and the nearby Peloponnese coast. Hydra\'s picturesque beauty, neoclassical architecture and maritime history make it a charming destination to explore and see some sights such as: \\r\\n\\r\\nHistorical Archives of Hydra Museum: explore this museum to immerse yourself in the island\'s history and discover historical artifacts and documents 2.\\r\\n2. Ecclesiastical and Byzantine Museum of Hydra: This museum houses a collection of religious art and Byzantine artifacts, perfect for lovers of culture and history.\\r\\nMonastery of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary: A quiet place surrounded by greenery, ideal for reflection and appreciation of religious architecture.\\r\\n4. Historic mansion of Lazaros Kountouriotis: Visit this mansion to learn more about the life and history of the Kountouriotis family.\\r\\n5. George Kountouriotis Mansion: Another impressive historical mansion worth exploring.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1HAqs3bATkRIULAgZfbbn_AQc2joE6FRt\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TfyOrdeYCGuN2HzKbJI_D4KiZADJSk1k\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1zL2noS7c0bDvJt3Ix7MyJZlMqlFPDszZ\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1X6a-kN3Ge0qd17xEMkSHCBkD0Cr2Ebim\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IFrnUbFLwbN0PUxYeeoE-ojiLz_CeD7B\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/11Ut0ghAqfRJsYAtm0m25t8g7kDRKobiG\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 20:44:08', '2024-03-26 20:44:08'),
(175, 358, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1NmnFwXTiLZMhShlCdHOcLJu_xrWau_LL\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The island of Poros (Greek: \\u03a0\\u03cc\\u03c1\\u03bf\\u03c2) is a small Greek double island located in the waters of the Aegean Sea, in the southern part of the Saronic Gulf, at a distance of 48 km south of Piraeus and separated by a 200 m sea channel from the Peloponnese. It has an area of 31 km\\u00b2 and a population of 4,000 inhabitants. The ancient name of Poros was Pogon.\\r\\nPoros consists of two islands:\\r\\n1. Sphairia, the southern part, which has a volcanic origin.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Kalaureia, the northern part.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe island contains a rich vegetation. Much of the north, east and west has very dense vegetation, and pine forests in the south and center. Geographically, the island is very mountainous, with the highest peak being the 358-meter Monte Vigla in the central-western part. It also has numerous coves into which small seasonal streams flow.\\r\\nThe history of Poros dates back to the Bronze Age, and tombs dating back to the Mycenaean period have been found on the island. Poros was the seat of an Amphictyony in the Archaic period, a league of cities composed of Prasias, Athens, Aegina, Epidaurus, Ermioni, Nauplia and Orcamene.  During the occupation of the Ottoman Empire, Poros remained independent and helped the neighboring islands to initiate the Greek war of independence. Today, the city of Poros has beautiful neoclassical buildings built in the form of an amphitheater on the side of a mountain. \\r\\nIn this city you will find different places of interest and archaeological sites nearby such as: \\r\\nThe town of Poros itself: explore this picturesque town, where you will find:\\r\\nThe Archaeological Museum, which houses historical artifacts and treasures of the island.\\r\\nThe municipal library, an interesting place for culture lovers.\\r\\nThe clock tower, one of the symbols of Poros, offering panoramic views from its elevated location, erected in 1927.\\r\\nRussian Shipyard: Visit this former naval shipyard, which has a historical connection with the Russian fleet and the Greek War of Independence.\\r\\nMonastery of Zoodochos Pigi: This monastery, surrounded by greenery, is a quiet place to reflect and enjoy religious architecture.\\r\\nTemple of Poseidon: Although not as famous as the Parthenon, the ancient city of Calauria (\\u039a\\u03b1\\u03bb\\u03b1\\u03c5\\u03c1\\u03b5\\u03af\\u03b1) had a temple dedicated to Poseidon, the ruins of which are still accessible on a nearby hill. This temple dedicated to the god of the sea is a testimony to ancient Greek culture.\\r\\n\\r\\nBeaches:\\r\\n1. Love Bay: A picturesque beach with crystal clear waters and a breathtaking view.\\r\\n2. Askeli Beach: Another option to relax by the sea.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1teo8Rh73G_okc3ASnRyQN3Z30F7GUkfd\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ehZuhltSw5n93rZhlpi7qLTJdqewqsTT\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wDSed_TNz0SRoiMEU6dZ8xS_beHWFPDN\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1camLEMVakFKHRKrpxsptvdGu5HJypVEG\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ORLGyNCJS0cknJsZ4cX4oPoYOe1dGnd7\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1p30QESrb83E88--QwjVS1ohJaY6Nnd34\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 21:43:32', '2024-03-26 21:43:32');
INSERT INTO `sight_media` (`id`, `sight_id`, `media_link`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(176, 350, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/n-gDQzJQWhY?si=LpSMExyYNFzL8Qv0\",\"esdescription\":\"La isla de Poros (en griego, \\u03a0\\u03cc\\u03c1\\u03bf\\u03c2) es una peque\\u00f1a isla doble griega localizada en aguas del mar Egeo, en la zona sur del golfo Sar\\u00f3nico, a una distancia de 48 km al sur del Pireo y separada por un canal mar\\u00edtimo de 200 m del Peloponeso. Su superficie es de 31 km\\u00b2 y tiene una poblaci\\u00f3n de 4.000 habitantes. El nombre antiguo de Poros era Pogon.\\r\\nPoros est\\u00e1 formada por dos islas:\\r\\n1. Sphairia, la parte sur, que tiene un origen volc\\u00e1nico.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Kalaureia, la parte norte.\\r\\n\\r\\nLa isla contiene una rica vegetaci\\u00f3n. Gran parte del norte, este y oeste tiene una vegetaci\\u00f3n muy tupida, y bosques de pinos en el sur y el centro. Geogr\\u00e1ficamente, la isla es muy monta\\u00f1osa, con el pico m\\u00e1s alto siendo el Monte Vigla de 358 metros en la parte centro-oeste. Adem\\u00e1s, posee numerosas calas a las que desembocan peque\\u00f1os riachuelos estacionales.\\r\\nLa historia de Poros se remonta a la Edad del Bronce, y tumbas datadas en el periodo mic\\u00e9nico han sido encontradas en la isla. Poros fue sede de una Anfiction\\u00eda en el periodo arcaico, una liga de ciudades compuesta por Prasias, Atenas, Egina, Epidauro, Ermioni, Nauplia y Orc\\u00f3meno.  Durante la ocupaci\\u00f3n del Imperio otomano, Poros permaneci\\u00f3 independiente y ayud\\u00f3 a las islas vecinas a iniciar la guerra de independencia de Grecia. Hoy en d\\u00eda, la ciudad de Poros cuenta con bonitos edificios neocl\\u00e1sicos construidos en forma de anfiteatro en la ladera de una monta\\u00f1a. \\r\\nEn esta ciudad se encuentran diferentes lugares de inter\\u00e9s y sitios arqueol\\u00f3gicos cercanos como: \\r\\nEl propio pueblo de Poros: Explora este pintoresco pueblo, donde encontrar\\u00e1s:\\r\\nEl Museo Arqueol\\u00f3gico, que alberga artefactos hist\\u00f3ricos y tesoros de la isla.\\r\\nLa biblioteca municipal, un lugar interesante para los amantes de la cultura.\\r\\nLa torre del reloj, uno de los s\\u00edmbolos de Poros, que ofrece vistas panor\\u00e1micas desde su ubicaci\\u00f3n elevada, erigida en 1927.\\r\\nAstillero Ruso: Visita este antiguo astillero naval, que tiene una conexi\\u00f3n hist\\u00f3rica con la flota rusa y la Guerra de Independencia griega.\\r\\nMonasterio de Zoodochos Pigi: Este monasterio, rodeado de vegetaci\\u00f3n, es un lugar tranquilo para reflexionar y disfrutar de la arquitectura religiosa.\\r\\nTemplo de Poseid\\u00f3n: Aunque no es tan famoso como el Parten\\u00f3n, la antigua ciudad de Calauria (\\u039a\\u03b1\\u03bb\\u03b1\\u03c5\\u03c1\\u03b5\\u03af\\u03b1) ten\\u00eda un templo dedicado a Poseid\\u00f3n, cuyas ruinas a\\u00fan son accesibles en una colina cercana. Este templo dedicado al dios del mar es un testimonio de la antigua cultura griega.\\r\\n\\r\\nPlayas:\\r\\n1. Love Bay: Una playa pintoresca con aguas cristalinas y una vista impresionante.\\r\\n2. Askeli Beach: Otra opci\\u00f3n para relajarte junto al mar.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1teo8Rh73G_okc3ASnRyQN3Z30F7GUkfd\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ehZuhltSw5n93rZhlpi7qLTJdqewqsTT\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1wDSed_TNz0SRoiMEU6dZ8xS_beHWFPDN\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1camLEMVakFKHRKrpxsptvdGu5HJypVEG\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ORLGyNCJS0cknJsZ4cX4oPoYOe1dGnd7\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1p30QESrb83E88--QwjVS1ohJaY6Nnd34\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 21:43:44', '2024-04-02 12:05:22'),
(177, 294, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/XWH121-4mXA?si=Utb-HbnihxyKZBeL\",\"esdescription\":\"Hier\\u00e1polis, ciudad sagrada, es una antigua ciudad helen\\u00edstica, fue una antigua ciudad situada en la regi\\u00f3n de Frigia en lo que hoy es Turqu\\u00eda. \\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles interesantes sobre Hier\\u00e1polis:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Historia: La ciudad fue fundada alrededor del a\\u00f1o 180 a.C. por Eumenes II, rey de P\\u00e9rgamo. \\r\\nA lo largo de su historia, Hier\\u00e1polis experiment\\u00f3 transformaciones significativas: desde su car\\u00e1cter helen\\u00edstico original hasta convertirse en una urbe t\\u00edpicamente romana. Durante los siglos II y III, se convirti\\u00f3 en un importante centro de descanso veraniego para los nobles de todo el Imperio Romano, quienes acud\\u00edan atra\\u00eddos por las aguas termales.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Ubicaci\\u00f3n y Geolog\\u00eda: Se encuentra ubicada en la actual Pamukkale, provincia de Denizli, Turqu\\u00eda, en el suroeste de Turqu\\u00eda.\\r\\n- La ciudad estaba construida en una meseta de travertino, una piedra caliza depositada por aguas termales ricas en minerales.\\r\\n- La formaci\\u00f3n de terrazas blancas y piscinas naturales en Hier\\u00e1polis es un fen\\u00f3meno geol\\u00f3gico \\u00fanico.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Aguas Termales y Propiedades Curativas:\\r\\n- Las aguas termales de Hier\\u00e1polis eran famosas en la antig\\u00fcedad por sus propiedades curativas.\\r\\n- Los romanos construyeron ba\\u00f1os termales, piscinas y templos dedicados a la salud y la relajaci\\u00f3n.\\r\\n- La leyenda cuenta que Cleopatra visit\\u00f3 las aguas termales de Hier\\u00e1polis para mantener su belleza y salud.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Importancia: Hoy yace en ruinas. Pero por sus valiosos restos arqueol\\u00f3gicos, la UNESCO la declar\\u00f3 Patrimonio de la Humanidad en 1998.\\r\\n\\r\\nAlgunos de los notables sitios arqueol\\u00f3gicos en Hier\\u00e1polis incluyen:\\r\\nEl Templo de Apolo, Plutonio de Hier\\u00e1polis (Era una gruta descrita por Estrab\\u00f3n, a la cual los peregrinos llegaban desde distintas partes y pagaban a los sacerdotes para que realizaran sacrificios en nombre de Plut\\u00f3n. En excavaciones recientes, se encontr\\u00f3 una estatua de Cerbero de 1,5 metros de altura, confirmando que esta gruta era el Plutonio), Fuente monumental (Nympheum), y el Teatro. \\r\\n\\r\\nHier\\u00e1polis sigue siendo un testimonio fascinante de la antig\\u00fcedad y su legado perdura en las ruinas que hoy podemos explorar.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1GTxLcNNAKocQAcwmXi7FOfXTRMcwDPGC\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1g4YwBjtlWHcKpRaoLUPJD_mpxx_bKom4\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1sPp7EuFGgUxK7_IB1s_ahcojfIftOX-K\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ybnGKwTWZU84YyAmix_eDhs6PkS1zNmD\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1rUfPfXxaNKvV9TCb1UiwgAgtizHPD5Ur\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TBQnFwH5r4DAkrQ-lp5aC2TcJcIsYXPb\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 23:00:53', '2024-04-01 15:37:18'),
(178, 295, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/zPJrKI6AYrc?si=1QAkJJRKDcPmwcCw\",\"esdescription\":\"Hierapolis, sacred city, is an ancient Hellenistic city, was an ancient city located in the region of Phrygia in what is now Turkey. \\r\\nHere are some interesting details about Hierapolis:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. History:The city was founded around 180 BC by Eumenes II, king of Pergamum. \\r\\nThroughout its history, Hierapolis underwent significant transformations: from its original Hellenistic character to become a typically Roman city. During the 2nd and 3rd centuries, it became an important summer resort for nobles from all over the Roman Empire, who were attracted by the thermal waters.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Location and Geology: It is located in the current Pamukkale, province of Denizli, Turkey, in the southwest of Turkey.\\r\\n- The city was built on a plateau of travertine, a limestone deposited by mineral-rich hot springs.\\r\\n- The formation of white terraces and natural pools in Hierapolis is a unique geological phenomenon.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Hot Springs and Healing Properties:\\r\\n- The hot springs of Hierapolis were famous in ancient times for their healing properties.\\r\\n- The Romans built thermal baths, pools and temples dedicated to health and relaxation.\\r\\n- Legend has it that Cleopatra visited the hot springs of Hierapolis to maintain her beauty and health.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Importance: Today it lies in ruins. But for its valuable archaeological remains, UNESCO declared it a World Heritage Site in 1998.\\r\\n\\r\\nSome of the notable archaeological sites in Hierapolis include:\\r\\nThe Temple of Apollo, Plutonius of Hierapolis (It was a grotto described by Strabo, to which pilgrims came from different parts and paid priests to perform sacrifices in the name of Pluto. In recent excavations, a statue of Cerberus 1.5 meters high was found, confirming that this grotto was the Plutonian), Monumental Fountain (Nympheum), and the Theater. \\r\\n\\r\\nHierapolis remains a fascinating testimony of antiquity and its legacy lives on in the ruins that we can explore today.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1GTxLcNNAKocQAcwmXi7FOfXTRMcwDPGC\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1g4YwBjtlWHcKpRaoLUPJD_mpxx_bKom4\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1sPp7EuFGgUxK7_IB1s_ahcojfIftOX-K\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ybnGKwTWZU84YyAmix_eDhs6PkS1zNmD\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TBQnFwH5r4DAkrQ-lp5aC2TcJcIsYXPb\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1rUfPfXxaNKvV9TCb1UiwgAgtizHPD5Ur\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-26 23:11:47', '2024-04-01 15:35:55'),
(179, 240, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/alMyNw39xqE?si=OA3Pf8U0PQt9rPFm\",\"esdescription\":\"La Biblioteca de Celso, en \\u00c9feso, Asia Menor (actualmente parte de Sel\\u00e7uk, Turqu\\u00eda), fue un impresionante edificio del Imperio Romano construida en honor al senador, c\\u00f3nsul y proc\\u00f3nsul romano, su nombre proviene de Tiberio Julio Celso Polemeano, antiguo gobernador romano de la ciudad, en el siglo II.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed est\\u00e1n algunas caracter\\u00edsticas notables de la Biblioteca de Celso:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Ubicaci\\u00f3n Actual: La Biblioteca de Celso se encuentra en la ciudad moderna de Sel\\u00e7uk, en Turqu\\u00eda. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Construcci\\u00f3n: Su construcci\\u00f3n se complet\\u00f3 aproximadamente entre los a\\u00f1os 117 y 135 d.C. por el hijo de Celso, Tiberio Julio \\u00c1quila Polemeano, quien tambi\\u00e9n fue c\\u00f3nsul en el a\\u00f1o 110 d.C.12.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Dise\\u00f1o: Su arquitectura y valor la hacen destacar, con una fuerte influencia de la arquitectura y el arte griegos.\\r\\nEl edificio constaba de tres pisos y estaba adornado con relieves, columnas, nichos y estatuas. Su fachada frontal orientada hacia la audiencia es un ejemplo impresionante de arquitectura romana p\\u00fablica y sirvi\\u00f3 como modelo para otras bibliotecas en diferentes partes del Imperio Romano.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Importancia y Funci\\u00f3n: La biblioteca fue dise\\u00f1ada para almacenar 12,000 rollos de papiro y manuscritos. Es considerada la tercera gran biblioteca de la antig\\u00fcedad cl\\u00e1sica, superada solo por las bibliotecas de Alejandr\\u00eda y P\\u00e9rgamo, la Biblioteca de Celso es un magn\\u00edfico ejemplo de edificio civil y religioso. Tambi\\u00e9n serv\\u00eda como monumento funerario para Celso. Es interesante destacar que era inusual que alguien fuera enterrado dentro de una biblioteca o incluso dentro de los l\\u00edmites de una ciudad. Sin embargo, Celso est\\u00e1 enterrado en una cripta debajo de la biblioteca, en un sarc\\u00f3fago de m\\u00e1rmol decorado.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Destrucci\\u00f3n y Reconstrucci\\u00f3n: Aunque el interior de la biblioteca y sus colecciones fueron destruidos, la fachada sobrevivi\\u00f3. Despu\\u00e9s de m\\u00e1s de 900 a\\u00f1os en ruinas, entre 1970 y 1978, el arque\\u00f3logo alem\\u00e1n Volker Michael Strocka dirigi\\u00f3 una campa\\u00f1a de reconstrucci\\u00f3n. La restauraci\\u00f3n se considera bastante fiel al edificio hist\\u00f3rico. La fachada muestra c\\u00f3mo las bibliotecas p\\u00fablicas no solo se constru\\u00edan en Roma, sino en todo el Imperio Romano.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Actualidad: Aunque gran parte del edificio original se ha perdido, hoy en d\\u00eda solo quedan ruinas. Imagina c\\u00f3mo debi\\u00f3 ser este lugar en su apogeo, lleno de conocimiento y cultura, donde los antiguos romanos acud\\u00edan en busca de sabidur\\u00eda y literatura.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, la Biblioteca de Celso es un fascinante recordatorio de la importancia de la cultura, el conocimiento y la memoria en la antigua Roma.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1dyJDanAeqPtDuZ35aGwdurSm_oP2XFcJ\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1sgQEYfP3Y2ZWhbL-iK63D85cis5MyeYJ\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1eZZU43NDrtG9ULencnkLkPy4J6c8d-CR\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YIgNCS7D_SS2eqoc6kA7hqRS-B2iXZ0u\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1y-cw9P4Lab6hU7_of3q-8fChzO1_qT0W\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1hglI0W1IBwwqEybhxVCKzyJUyGow7jPb\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 10:58:50', '2024-04-01 12:16:38'),
(180, 241, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/yYllMwSj6fM?si=hi49d663WD54OLVt\",\"esdescription\":\"The Library of Celsus, in Ephesus, Asia Minor (now part of Sel\\u00e7uk, Turkey), was an impressive Roman Empire building built in honor of the Roman senator, consul and proconsul, named after Tiberius Julius Celsus Polemianus, former Roman governor of the city, in the 2nd century.\\r\\nHere are some notable features of the Library of Celsus:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Current Location: The Library of Celsus is located in the modern city of Sel\\u00e7uk, Turkey. \\r\\n\\r\\n- Construction: Its construction was completed approximately between 117 and 135 A.D. by Celsus\' son, Tiberius Julius Aquila Polemeanus, who was also consul in 110 A.D.12.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Design: Its architecture and value make it stand out, with a strong influence of Greek architecture and art.\\r\\nThe building consisted of three floors and was adorned with reliefs, columns, niches and statues. Its front facade facing the audience is an impressive example of Roman public architecture and served as a model for other libraries in different parts of the Roman Empire.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Importance and Function: The library was designed to store 12,000 papyrus scrolls and manuscripts. Considered the third great library of classical antiquity, surpassed only by the libraries of Alexandria and Pergamum, the Library of Celsus is a magnificent example of a civil and religious building. It also served as a funerary monument for Celsus. It is interesting to note that it was unusual for someone to be buried inside a library or even within city limits. However, Celsus is buried in a crypt beneath the library, in a decorated marble sarcophagus.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Destruction and Reconstruction: Although the interior of the library and its collections were destroyed, the facade survived. After more than 900 years in ruins, between 1970 and 1978, German archaeologist Volker Michael Strocka led a reconstruction campaign. The restoration is considered quite faithful to the historic building. The facade shows how public libraries were not only built in Rome, but throughout the Roman Empire.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Today: Although much of the original building has been lost, today only ruins remain. Imagine what this place must have been like in its heyday, full of knowledge and culture, where ancient Romans flocked in search of wisdom and literature.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, the Library of Celsus is a fascinating reminder of the importance of culture, knowledge and memory in ancient Rome.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1dyJDanAeqPtDuZ35aGwdurSm_oP2XFcJ\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1sgQEYfP3Y2ZWhbL-iK63D85cis5MyeYJ\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1eZZU43NDrtG9ULencnkLkPy4J6c8d-CR\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YIgNCS7D_SS2eqoc6kA7hqRS-B2iXZ0u\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1y-cw9P4Lab6hU7_of3q-8fChzO1_qT0W\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1hglI0W1IBwwqEybhxVCKzyJUyGow7jPb\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 10:59:04', '2024-04-01 14:49:12'),
(181, 376, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1z2UmcR07SHPHB2Vo14RfezGOyZwl4HoA\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Odeon of Laodicea also known as the Odeon of Laodicea ad Lycum, is one of the most impressive notable monuments that was part of the ruins of the ancient city of Laodicea, located in present-day Turkey.\\r\\nHere are some details about this remarkable building:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Location and Present Condition:At the present archaeological site of Laodicea, the ruins of the Odeon can be seen, along with other elements such as the stadium, the amphitheater, the cistern, the aqueduct, the thermal baths and the Nymphaeum.\\r\\n\\r\\nShape and Structure: The Odeon was a small, enclosed theater, with a semicircular or horseshoe shape, typical of small theaters used for musical performances, recitals, poetry, philosophical debates, public meetings and other cultural activities. It also served as a social gathering space.\\r\\nUnlike large amphitheaters, the Odeon had a roof, which allowed its use even in adverse weather conditions.\\r\\nIts architectural design is impressive, with tiered tiers and a raised platform for performers. Its construction reflects the richness and cultural flourishing of Laodicea in antiquity.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Capacity: Although smaller than a theater, the Odeon of Laodicea had a considerable capacity. It could seat about 1,500 to 2,000 people.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Strategic Location: The Odeon was close to the center of the city, which facilitated access for citizens. In addition, its proximity to other important buildings, such as the stadium and the amphitheater, made it a focal point in urban life.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Current State: At the present archaeological site, the ruins of the Odeon can be observed along with other elements, such as the stadium, the amphitheater, the cistern and the aqueduct. These ruins provide a fascinating insight into the history and culture of Laodicea.\\r\\n\\r\\nExploring these ancient ruins will transport you to a bygone era filled with art, music and intellectual debate! Their presence at the archaeological site continues to attract curious visitors!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12xWSHQaIy1oa3UGMGzy_3ciTSRg_qA8Q\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1SN_Vy7732yPQLdG0SNqbQw1dPcKThG0X\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1OdGBdP26M8-b_cjWikCNxi0r8twUyviG\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1NSLk-wYb9fDKh37WanNGICMatGIofav5\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1H7bTaazAqBGvghsiKpS-FG45UMBFwpzM\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xOIYyTH1Kk_7vwv7UXz4nYFbb99pClaR\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 11:23:35', '2024-03-27 11:23:35'),
(182, 377, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1GtwQTM4Hpx1RaGlPa2CfQo1X-vAUV11U\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"El Ode\\u00f3n de Laodicea tambi\\u00e9n conocido como Ode\\u00f3n de Laodicea ad Lycum, es uno de los notables monumentos m\\u00e1s impresionantes que formaba parte de las ruinas de la antigua ciudad de Laodicea, ubicada en la actual Turqu\\u00eda.\\r\\nAqu\\u00ed tienes algunos detalles sobre este notable edificio:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n y Estado Actual: En el sitio arqueol\\u00f3gico actual de Laodicea, se pueden observar las ruinas del Ode\\u00f3n, junto con otros elementos como el estadio, el anfiteatro, la cisterna, el acueducto, los ba\\u00f1os termales y el Ninfeo.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Forma y Estructura: El Ode\\u00f3n era un teatro peque\\u00f1o y cerrado, tiene una forma semicircular o de herradura, t\\u00edpica de los teatros peque\\u00f1os utilizados para actuaciones musicales, recitales, poes\\u00eda, debates filos\\u00f3ficos, reuniones p\\u00fablicas y otras actividades culturales. Tambi\\u00e9n serv\\u00eda como un espacio de encuentro social.\\r\\nA diferencia de los grandes anfiteatros, el Ode\\u00f3n ten\\u00eda un techo, lo que permit\\u00eda su uso incluso en condiciones clim\\u00e1ticas adversas.\\r\\nSu dise\\u00f1o arquitect\\u00f3nico es impresionante, con gradas escalonadas y una plataforma elevada para los artistas. Su construcci\\u00f3n refleja la riqueza y el florecimiento cultural de Laodicea en la antig\\u00fcedad.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Capacidad: Aunque m\\u00e1s peque\\u00f1o que un teatro, el Ode\\u00f3n de Laodicea ten\\u00eda una capacidad considerable. Pod\\u00eda albergar a alrededor de 1,500 a 2,000 personas.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Ubicaci\\u00f3n Estrat\\u00e9gica: El Ode\\u00f3n estaba cerca del centro de la ciudad, lo que facilitaba el acceso para los ciudadanos. Adem\\u00e1s, su proximidad a otros edificios importantes, como el estadio y el anfiteatro, lo convert\\u00eda en un punto focal en la vida urbana.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Estado Actual: En el sitio arqueol\\u00f3gico actual, se pueden observar las ruinas del Ode\\u00f3n junto con otros elementos, como el estadio, el anfiteatro, la cisterna y el acueducto. Estas ruinas nos brindan una visi\\u00f3n fascinante de la historia y la cultura de Laodicea.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u00a1Explorar estas antiguas ruinas te transportar\\u00e1 a una \\u00e9poca pasada llena de arte, m\\u00fasica y debate intelectual! \\u00a1Su presencia en el sitio arqueol\\u00f3gico sigue atrayendo a visitantes curiosos!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12xWSHQaIy1oa3UGMGzy_3ciTSRg_qA8Q\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1SN_Vy7732yPQLdG0SNqbQw1dPcKThG0X\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1OdGBdP26M8-b_cjWikCNxi0r8twUyviG\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1NSLk-wYb9fDKh37WanNGICMatGIofav5\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1H7bTaazAqBGvghsiKpS-FG45UMBFwpzM\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xOIYyTH1Kk_7vwv7UXz4nYFbb99pClaR\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 11:24:00', '2024-03-27 11:24:00'),
(183, 378, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/zgNhQg_adZY?si=yASkhX1QeU7_s7qM\",\"esdescription\":\"El gimnasio de Laodicea era una estructura notable en la antigua ciudad de Laodicea, que se encuentra ubicada hoy en la actual Turqu\\u00eda. \\r\\nAqu\\u00ed hay algunos detalles interesantes sobre este lugar:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Estadio y Gimnasio: Laodicea deslumbra con su estadio, que es el m\\u00e1s grande de Anatolia y el segundo m\\u00e1s grande del mundo antiguo. Adem\\u00e1s, su gimnasio tambi\\u00e9n es impresionante. Los antiguos gimnasios no solo eran lugares para hacer ejercicio f\\u00edsico, sino tambi\\u00e9n centros culturales y sociales.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Forma y Estructura: El gimnasio ten\\u00eda una estructura impresionante, con amplias \\u00e1reas para ejercicios f\\u00edsicos y actividades atl\\u00e9ticas. Contaba con espacios para correr, luchar, practicar lanzamiento de disco y otras disciplinas.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Estadio Anexo: Junto al gimnasio, se encontraba un estadio de gran tama\\u00f1o. Este estadio era utilizado para competencias deportivas y eventos atl\\u00e9ticos. Laodicea se enorgullec\\u00eda de su estadio, que era uno de los m\\u00e1s grandes de Anatolia.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, el gimnasio de Laodicea era un testimonio de la opulencia de la ciudad y su enfoque en la salud f\\u00edsica y el bienestar. Sin embargo, la advertencia espiritual a la iglesia en Laodicea nos recuerda que la verdadera riqueza est\\u00e1 en un compromiso apasionado con Dios. Es un recordatorio tangible de su pasado pr\\u00f3spero y su importancia en la historia antigua.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1d64LTKQvBfYcRF2VGczrgLFdYrO2NZeq\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IOvMJTMmN1ew0pF7iOfPstArjloXLndf\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1yQybl4xlJ9fEJLJnteyml5jrLSmOELwy\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TPI2RDSM_KUX_zoapZwCOAzlJk9Qj2mi\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IRfezct2qfv6jlnrMKyKTiuajg406Yja\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YdGbA1J619fXjbRgUDk3IfUSXKwnHLRt\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 11:41:57', '2024-04-02 11:00:50'),
(184, 379, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1MmucJ05vQ_vLCQVIQ3jQ5ZYc1QlWH_wh\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The gymnasium of Laodicea was a notable structure in the ancient city of Laodicea, which is located today in modern-day Turkey. \\r\\nHere are some interesting details about this place:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Stadium and Gymnasium: Laodicea dazzles with its stadium, which is the largest in Anatolia and the second largest in the ancient world. In addition, its gymnasium is also impressive. Ancient gymnasiums were not only places for physical exercise, but also cultural and social centers.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Form and Structure: The gymnasium had an impressive structure, with large areas for physical exercise and athletic activities. It had spaces for running, wrestling, discus throwing and other disciplines.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Annex Stadium: Next to the gymnasium, there was a large stadium. This stadium was used for sports competitions and athletic events. Laodicea was proud of its stadium, which was one of the largest in Anatolia.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, Laodicea\'s gymnasium was a testament to the city\'s opulence and its focus on physical health and wellness. However, the spiritual admonition to the church in Laodicea reminds us that true wealth is in a passionate commitment to God. It is a tangible reminder of its prosperous past and its importance in ancient history.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1d64LTKQvBfYcRF2VGczrgLFdYrO2NZeq\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IOvMJTMmN1ew0pF7iOfPstArjloXLndf\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1yQybl4xlJ9fEJLJnteyml5jrLSmOELwy\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1TPI2RDSM_KUX_zoapZwCOAzlJk9Qj2mi\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IRfezct2qfv6jlnrMKyKTiuajg406Yja\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YdGbA1J619fXjbRgUDk3IfUSXKwnHLRt\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 11:42:14', '2024-03-27 11:42:14'),
(185, 381, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/s2l3mr8kEFQ?si=XCBlzQ-wjXsXYjR1\",\"esdescription\":\"The Domitian Gate, also known as the Domitian Gate, is an ancient construction located in Hierapolis.\\r\\nLet me tell you more about it:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. History:The city of Hierapolis was established around 180 BC by Eumenes II, king of Pergamon, as a tribute to the Roman emperor after whom it is named, Domitian. However, it collapsed after an earthquake during the reign of Tiberius in 17 AD. Subsequently, it was rebuilt and underwent significant transformations in the second and third centuries, becoming an important summer resort for the nobles of the Roman Empire, attracted by its thermal waters.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Location and Design: The Gate is located next to the Colonnaded Way in Hierapolis, this impressive structure is composed of three arches, flanked by two cylindrical towers. The Dominician Gate served as the grand entrance to the city along the route coming from Sardis.\\r\\nThis gate was built by Julius Frontinus, proconsul of Asia Minor, during the reign of Domitian in 82-83 AD. The north and south gates are connected by a road surrounded by columns, on the sides of which are important buildings. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Actuality: Today, due to its archaeological importance, it is in ruins, but it was declared a World Heritage Site by UNESCO. \\r\\n\\r\\nThe Domitian Gate is a fascinating testimony of the history and architecture of ancient Hierapolis, do not miss the opportunity to admire this ancient gate and immerse yourself in the history that surrounds it!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1eC9vt7xCsSou--mVBYCoc8fAuHiYlQ6B\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1e3Qo5XqrsqV2BO6wva2dgjEb1uAmJiK0\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12K7w_ZdeOD04gJKI5JTfN6rnXazj1nSr\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CtjDdHzVFtIgSGNb3y2-HFl1uoIERvUI\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1NLcRbhGJhxnAC2qmQFKCYwRDJ6_FyJHY\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1b-CFuUmbdB1g-_jCTr_wsUWkMimW9Bbo\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 11:59:19', '2024-04-01 10:51:29'),
(186, 380, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/hxv-wIOklcA?si=CewRSvhsANk8PzP4\",\"esdescription\":\"La Puerta de Domiciano, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Puerta de Dominiano, es una antigua construcci\\u00f3n ubicada en Hier\\u00e1polis.\\r\\nPerm\\u00edteme contarte m\\u00e1s sobre ella:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Historia: La ciudad de Hier\\u00e1polis fue establecida alrededor del 180 a.C. por Eumenes II, rey de P\\u00e9rgamo, como un homenaje al emperador romano del que toma su nombre, Domiciano. Sin embargo, colaps\\u00f3 tras un terremoto durante el reinado de Tiberio en el a\\u00f1o 17 d.C.. Posteriormente, fue reconstruida y experiment\\u00f3 transformaciones significativas en los siglos II y III, convirti\\u00e9ndose en un importante centro de descanso veraniego para los nobles del Imperio Romano, atra\\u00eddos por sus aguas termales.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Ubicaci\\u00f3n y Dise\\u00f1o: La Puerta est\\u00e1 situada junto a la V\\u00eda Columnada en Hier\\u00e1polis, esta impresionante estructura est\\u00e1 compuesta por tres arcos, flanqueados por dos torreones cil\\u00edndricos. La Puerta de Dominiciano serv\\u00eda como la gran entrada a la ciudad a lo largo de la ruta que ven\\u00eda desde Sardes.\\r\\nEsta puerta fue construida por Julio Frontino, proc\\u00f3nsul de Asia Menor, durante el imperio de Domiciano en los a\\u00f1os 82-83 d.C.. Las puertas del norte y del sur est\\u00e1n unidas por un camino rodeado de columnas, a cuyos costados se hallan importantes construcciones. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Actualidad: Hoy en d\\u00eda por su importancia arqueol\\u00f3gica se encuentra en ruinas, pero fue declarada Patrimonio de la Humanidad por la UNESCO. \\r\\n\\r\\n\\u00a1La Puerta de Domiciano es un testimonio fascinante de la historia y la arquitectura de la antigua Hier\\u00e1polis, no te pierdas la oportunidad de admirar esta antigua puerta y sumergirte en la historia que la rodea!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1eC9vt7xCsSou--mVBYCoc8fAuHiYlQ6B\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1e3Qo5XqrsqV2BO6wva2dgjEb1uAmJiK0\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/12K7w_ZdeOD04gJKI5JTfN6rnXazj1nSr\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CtjDdHzVFtIgSGNb3y2-HFl1uoIERvUI\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1NLcRbhGJhxnAC2qmQFKCYwRDJ6_FyJHY\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1b-CFuUmbdB1g-_jCTr_wsUWkMimW9Bbo\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 11:59:34', '2024-04-01 10:53:50'),
(187, 200, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/qS0ivML3ut8?si=MlJ06pXDjYCvBf2R\",\"esdescription\":\"Sardis, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Sardes, fue una antigua ciudad con una rica historia. Fue la capital del Imperio lidio y destac\\u00f3 por su riqueza, cultura y situaci\\u00f3n estrat\\u00e9gica en la ruta comercial central desde el mar Egeo hasta el interior de Anatolia, en lo que hoy es Turqu\\u00eda.  \\r\\nSu historia abarca miles de a\\u00f1os y su importancia se debe a varios factores, he aqu\\u00ed algunos puntos clave sobre Sardis:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Fundaci\\u00f3n y Reino de Lidia: Sardis fue fundada por el rey lidio Giges alrededor del 680 a.C. y se convirti\\u00f3 en la capital del antiguo reino de Lidia. Este reino era conocido por su riqueza y prosperidad.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Geograf\\u00eda: Sardis estaba situada estrat\\u00e9gicamente ubicada en el valle del r\\u00edo Hermo, a unos 4 kil\\u00f3metros al sur cerca del r\\u00edo Pactolo. Esto la convert\\u00eda en un punto central que conectaba el interior de Anatolia con la costa del mar Egeo. Su ciudadela estaba encaramada en el escarpado y elevado monte Tmolus, mientras que la ciudad baja se extend\\u00eda hacia el arroyo Pactolus.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Importancia hist\\u00f3rica: Sardis fue testigo de m\\u00e1s de 3.500 a\\u00f1os de ocupaci\\u00f3n, fluctuando entre una pr\\u00f3spera ciudad internacional y una colecci\\u00f3n de modestas aldeas.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Cultura Helen\\u00edstica y Bizantina: Despu\\u00e9s de la ca\\u00edda del Imperio lidio, Sardis se convirti\\u00f3 en la capital de la satrap\\u00eda persa de Lidia y, m\\u00e1s tarde, en un importante centro de la cultura helen\\u00edstica y bizantina.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Moneda: Los lidios acu\\u00f1aron las primeras monedas del mundo en Sardis, alrededor del a\\u00f1o 560 a.C.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Menciones en la Literatura Antigua: Sardis aparece en textos antiguos, como en la obra \\u201cLos persas\\u201d de Esquilo y en la Il\\u00edada de Homero. Tambi\\u00e9n se cree que la famosa ciudad de Hid\\u00e9 mencionada por Homero podr\\u00eda ser Sardis.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Descubrimientos arqueol\\u00f3gicos: Aunque hasta hace poco no se hab\\u00eda encontrado arquitectura monumental antigua, las pruebas sugieren que Sardis pudo estar ocupada incluso durante el Neol\\u00edtico.  \\r\\nEl yacimiento arqueol\\u00f3gico de Sardis se encuentra cerca del actual pueblo de Sart, en la provincia de Manisa. Aunque se han excavado partes extramuros, a\\u00fan se desconoce la arquitectura monumental antigua. En la actualidad, el yacimiento corresponde a la moderna localidad de Sart, cerca de Salihli, en la provincia turca de Manisa, a unos 75 kil\\u00f3metros de Esmirna.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, Sardis fue una ciudad con una rica historia, desde su fundaci\\u00f3n en el antiguo reino de Lidia hasta su papel en la cultura helen\\u00edstica y bizantina. Hoy en d\\u00eda, sus ruinas siguen siendo un testimonio de su pasado glorioso.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16Whsvur0mtqR8WCxuv7SyqF_QfmAnuFT\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FasAZHb3s38PL5Xkw0F-wBrHVOc-_Y6Y\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Aw-fqazHvw7mYolZKSEmEhXHS4pQSgB0\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1bhluVHlqz1CPJg648aH93oZV-eMMBwnh\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1s7ypIwzpJnUZCgY_z7PnBEVoF3fBenES\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1fTj5Sren4-294bh0GoeV-PN4N0dmEAsV\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 12:09:03', '2024-04-01 11:03:11'),
(188, 201, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1twsbWLoiLe6drPC7FCX3lc3h36k4ISHv\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"Sardis, also known as Sardes, was an ancient city with a rich history. It was the capital of the Lydian Empire and was noted for its wealth, culture and strategic location on the central trade route from the Aegean Sea to the interior of Anatolia, in what is now Turkey.  \\r\\nIts history spans thousands of years and its importance is due to several factors, here are some key points about Sardis:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Foundation and Kingdom of Lydia: Sardis was founded by the Lydian king Giges around 680 BC and became the capital of the ancient kingdom of Lydia. This kingdom was known for its wealth and prosperity.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Geography: Sardis was strategically located in the valley of the Hermo River, about 4 kilometers to the south near the Pactolus River. This made it a central point connecting the Anatolian hinterland with the Aegean coast. Its citadel was perched on the steep and lofty Mount Tmolus, while the lower city extended towards the Pactolus stream.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Historical significance: Sardis witnessed more than 3,500 years of occupation, fluctuating between a thriving international city and a collection of modest villages.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Hellenistic and Byzantine Culture: After the fall of the Lydian Empire, Sardis became the capital of the Persian satrapy of Lydia and, later, an important center of Hellenistic and Byzantine culture.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Currency: The Lydians minted the world\'s first coins in Sardis, around 560 BC.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Mentions in Ancient Literature: Sardis appears in ancient texts, such as Aeschylus\' \\\"The Persians\\\" and Homer\'s Iliad. It is also believed that the famous city of Hid\\u00e9 mentioned by Homer could be Sardis.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Archaeological discoveries: Although no ancient monumental architecture has been found until recently, evidence suggests that Sardis may have been occupied even during the Neolithic period.  \\r\\nThe archaeological site of Sardis is located near the present-day village of Sart, in the province of Manisa. Although parts outside the walls have been excavated, the ancient monumental architecture is still unknown. Today, the site corresponds to the modern town of Sart, near Salihli, in the Turkish province of Manisa, about 75 kilometers from Izmir.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, Sardis was a city with a rich history, from its foundation in the ancient kingdom of Lydia to its role in Hellenistic and Byzantine culture. Today, its ruins remain a testament to its glorious past.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16Whsvur0mtqR8WCxuv7SyqF_QfmAnuFT\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FasAZHb3s38PL5Xkw0F-wBrHVOc-_Y6Y\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Aw-fqazHvw7mYolZKSEmEhXHS4pQSgB0\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1bhluVHlqz1CPJg648aH93oZV-eMMBwnh\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1s7ypIwzpJnUZCgY_z7PnBEVoF3fBenES\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1fTj5Sren4-294bh0GoeV-PN4N0dmEAsV\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 12:09:22', '2024-03-27 12:09:22'),
(189, 291, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/16Gx4MYWkWlxJmcoLWhxDewMuqcQ5BU11\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Bosphorus is a mighty river that runs through the bowels of Istanbul, the most important city of the entire Turkish nation (encompassed during the Ottoman Empire in the European province of Rumelia) of the Asian (Anatolian) part. On the banks of this river, there are many quite interesting works of architecture, and the identity of the town is largely shaped by the Bosphorus.\\r\\nSome interesting facts about the Bosphorus:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Depth: It varies between 36 and 124 meters.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Strait: The Bosphorus Strait is with a length of 30 kilometers and a maximum width of 3,700 meters, connects the Sea of Marmara with the Black Sea, and a minimum width of 750 meters between the Ottoman castles of Anadolu Hisar\\u0131 and Rumeli Hisar\\u0131. It divides the city of Istanbul in two and is a crucial waterway in the history and geography of the region.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Population: The banks of the strait are densely populated, as the city of Istanbul (with a population of at least 14 million), \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Bridges: There are three bridges crossing the Bosphorus: the Bo\\u011fazi\\u00e7i Bridge, the Fatih Sultan Mehmed Bridge and the Yavuz Sultan Selim Bridge. The latter, completed in 2016, is the second highest bridge in the world and the widest suspension bridge in the world at 58.5 meters wide.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Architectural works: In addition to the bridges, you will find many architectural works along the Bosphorus, such as the Topkapi Palace, the Beylerbeyi Palace and the Anadolu Hisari Fortress. You can also visit the Istanbul Museum of Modern Art.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Beautiful scenery: From the banks of the Bosphorus, you can enjoy the sunset while the locals cast their fishing rod. The buildings on both banks are densely populated, and this strait is part of the daily life of millions of people who move from one continent to another to work, walk or simply cross from one bank to the other. \\r\\n\\r\\n7. Bosphorus Cruise: A cruise is the ideal way to contemplate all the majesty of the Bosphorus and get incredible views of the city from a different perspective. It can be crossed by two main bridges or by ferry. The cruise will also take you close to beautiful landscapes and great monuments, such as Dolmabahce Palace, Rumeli Fortress, Topkapi Palace and more.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, the Bosphorus is an iconic place that joins and separates continents, and its beauty and historical relevance make it a fascinating destination to visit in Istanbul.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1K3pw28J24p2qcuq9omrMFMqiDDv-m65i\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1jjOJH2CR6xPP3-WC62jq4p5m72yj5_oW\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Zd4cjK-Dhqx6-b5fLaLM54FijlDIDs0q\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13Nd2T9YVuiTdtiYftn9lgAPlxMxKer3B\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Picv_N6VVHB7Ugq63mrYF-mVs1RmZt6T\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1PMSPjBzaUs4IpOFqVSgf0iwLhF770r3x\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 12:17:27', '2024-03-27 12:17:27'),
(190, 290, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/VLezydgHjNk?si=iGb8I9fptceZgsCc\",\"esdescription\":\"El B\\u00f3sforo es un r\\u00edo poderoso que atraviesa las entra\\u00f1as de Estambul, la ciudad m\\u00e1s importante de toda la naci\\u00f3n turca (englobada durante el Imperio otomano en la provincia europea de Rumelia) de la parte asi\\u00e1tica (Anatolia). A orillas de este r\\u00edo, se encuentran muchas obras de arquitectura bastante interesantes, y la identidad de la localidad se conforma en gran medida por el B\\u00f3sforo.\\r\\nAlgunos datos interesantes sobre el B\\u00f3sforo:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Profundidad: Var\\u00eda entre 36 y 124 metros.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Estrecho: El estrecho del B\\u00f3sforo es con una longitud de 30 kil\\u00f3metros y una anchura m\\u00e1xima de 3,700 metros, conecta el mar de M\\u00e1rmara con el mar Negro, y una anchura m\\u00ednima de 750 metros entre los castillos otomanos de Anadolu Hisar\\u0131 y Rumeli Hisar\\u0131. Divide la ciudad de Estambul en dos y es una v\\u00eda fluvial crucial en la historia y geograf\\u00eda de la regi\\u00f3n.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Poblaci\\u00f3n: Las orillas del estrecho est\\u00e1n densamente pobladas, ya que la ciudad de Estambul (con una poblaci\\u00f3n de al menos 14 millones de habitantes), \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Puentes: Hay tres puentes que cruzan el B\\u00f3sforo: el puente Bo\\u011fazi\\u00e7i, el puente Fatih Sultan Mehmed y el puente Yavuz Sultan Selim. Este \\u00faltimo, finalizado en 2016, es el segundo puente m\\u00e1s alto del mundo y el puente colgante m\\u00e1s amplio del mundo con 58.5 metros de ancho.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Obras arquitect\\u00f3nicas: Adem\\u00e1s de los puentes, encontrar\\u00e1s muchas obras arquitect\\u00f3nicas a lo largo del B\\u00f3sforo, como el Palacio Topkapi, el Palacio Beylerbeyi y la Fortaleza Anadolu Hisari. Tambi\\u00e9n puedes visitar el Museo de Arte Moderno de Estambul.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Hermosos paisajes: Desde las orillas del B\\u00f3sforo, podr\\u00e1s disfrutar de la puesta de sol mientras los vecinos echan la ca\\u00f1a de pescar. Los edificios en ambas orillas est\\u00e1n densamente poblados, y este estrecho es parte de la cotidianidad de millones de personas que se trasladan de un continente a otro para trabajar, pasear o simplemente cruzar desde una orilla a la otra. \\r\\n\\r\\n7. Crucero por el B\\u00f3sforo: Un crucero es la forma ideal de contemplar toda la majestuosidad del B\\u00f3sforo y obtener vistas incre\\u00edbles de la ciudad desde una perspectiva diferente. Se puede cruzar por dos puentes principales o en ferry. El crucero tambi\\u00e9n te llevar\\u00e1 cerca de hermosos paisajes y grandes monumentos, como el Palacio de Dolmabahce, la Fortaleza Rumeli, el Palacio de Topkapi y m\\u00e1s.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, el B\\u00f3sforo es un lugar emblem\\u00e1tico que une y separa continentes, y su belleza y relevancia hist\\u00f3rica lo convierten en un destino fascinante para visitar en Estambul.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1K3pw28J24p2qcuq9omrMFMqiDDv-m65i\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1jjOJH2CR6xPP3-WC62jq4p5m72yj5_oW\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Zd4cjK-Dhqx6-b5fLaLM54FijlDIDs0q\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/13Nd2T9YVuiTdtiYftn9lgAPlxMxKer3B\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Picv_N6VVHB7Ugq63mrYF-mVs1RmZt6T\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1PMSPjBzaUs4IpOFqVSgf0iwLhF770r3x\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 12:17:40', '2024-04-02 11:17:00'),
(191, 157, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/B1CGXvL-_2c?si=aQdsS4-PUiLF4bjF\",\"esdescription\":\"Estambul es una ciudad fascinante donde las culturas se mezclan. A lo largo de los siglos, muchas culturas le dejaron su marca a Estambul. Hoy en d\\u00eda, puedes disfrutar de esas influencias de primera mano explorando sus mahalles (colonias). Desde los sitios sagrados de Sultanahmet y la elegancia europea del siglo XIX de Beyo\\u011flu hasta la alta moda de Ni\\u015fanta\\u015f\\u0131, la animada Caf\\u00e9 Society de Kad\\u0131k\\u00f6y y las calles amantes del f\\u00fatbol de Be\\u015fikta\\u015f, es f\\u00e1cil ver por qu\\u00e9 los viajeros dicen que Estambul no es solo una ciudad, sino muchas ciudades en una.\\r\\n\\r\\nAlgunos puntos esenciales para visitar en Estambul incluyen:\\r\\n\\r\\nMuseo \\/ Iglesia de Santa Sof\\u00eda (Ayasofya): Un edificio con valor arquitect\\u00f3nico y un lugar religioso.\\r\\nPalacio de Dolmabah\\u00e7e: Otro edificio con valor arquitect\\u00f3nico.\\r\\n\\u00c1reas hist\\u00f3ricas de Estambul: Lugares hist\\u00f3ricos que definen la ciudad.\\r\\nPalacio Topkapi: Un museo de historia.\\r\\nFortaleza de Rumeli: Un lugar hist\\u00f3rico y un museo.\\r\\nCisterna Bas\\u00edlica: Ruinas antiguas y un edificio con valor arquitect\\u00f3nico.\\r\\nDistrito de Sultanahmet: Un \\u00e1rea hist\\u00f3rica peatonal y un barrio.\\r\\nKucuk Ayasofya Camii (Iglesia de los Santos Sergio y Baco): Otro edificio con valor arquitect\\u00f3nico y un lugar religioso.\\r\\nBeyoglu: Un barrio interesante.\\r\\nGran Bazar: Un mercado de las pulgas y al aire libre para comprar ropa y accesorios.\\r\\n\\r\\nEstambul, antiguamente conocida como Bizancio y Constantinopla, tiene una historia rica y fascinante que abarca miles de a\\u00f1os. Aqu\\u00ed est\\u00e1n algunos de los per\\u00edodos clave que marcaron su evoluci\\u00f3n:\\r\\n\\r\\nOr\\u00edgenes en Bizancio: Fundada en el a\\u00f1o 667 a.C. por colonos griegos de Megara, Bizancio se ubic\\u00f3 a lo largo del Cuerno de Oro, un golfo profundo y resguardado. A lo largo de los siglos, fue ocupada, reconstruida y pas\\u00f3 por manos de diferentes imperios.\\r\\nImperio Romano: En el a\\u00f1o 191 a.C., Roma reconoci\\u00f3 a Bizancio como ciudad libre. M\\u00e1s tarde, en el 330 d.C., el emperador Constantino I la transform\\u00f3 en Constantinopla, la capital del Imperio Romano de Oriente (tambi\\u00e9n conocido como Imperio Bizantino). Durante este per\\u00edodo, se construyeron monumentos ic\\u00f3nicos como la Catedral de Santa Sof\\u00eda.\\r\\nImperio Otomano: En 1453, Constantinopla cay\\u00f3 ante los otomanos y se convirti\\u00f3 en la capital del Imperio Otomano. Aunque se transform\\u00f3 en una ciudad musulmana, su influencia cristiana persisti\\u00f3. La Mezquita de Ortak\\u00f6y y la Torre de G\\u00e1lata son ejemplos de su rica arquitectura.\\r\\nRep\\u00fablica de Turqu\\u00eda: Tras la Primera Guerra Mundial, el territorio europeo otomano se redujo, y Ankara se convirti\\u00f3 en la capital pol\\u00edtica. Sin embargo, Estambul sigue siendo un centro vital para la industria, el comercio y la cultura de Turqu\\u00eda.\\r\\nHoy en d\\u00eda, Estambul es una ciudad vibrante que combina su legado hist\\u00f3rico con una modernidad cautivadora. \\u00a1Explora sus calles, mezquitas y palacios para descubrir su fascinante historia!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1z-WYjzGHfryJGcGEmFR2fVKShAgyJOHv\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1K551DYJeCr_v6KDQfRiDK0fMwChTJghi\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1LztLuN4fIDQU_vwsdeV7x575_-7cB7jq\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-7XDCyetQg1zPNG-D7Dokqfa5ngXq0G9\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1fpepMttPyEC3sAsG9rOZ9g-AeTjk5f6i\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1tFmu9OdzVAAiG_DYDNcccLpd_KXjjmTE\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 12:32:35', '2024-04-02 11:04:42');
INSERT INTO `sight_media` (`id`, `sight_id`, `media_link`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(192, 359, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/15rG1mWJJFvpQL5N_XkylC4hIIP1jzOPH\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"Istanbul is a fascinating city where cultures mix. Over the centuries, many cultures have left their mark on Istanbul. Today, you can enjoy those influences firsthand by exploring its mahalles (colonies). From the sacred sites of Sultanahmet and the 19th-century European elegance of Beyo\\u011flu to the high fashion of Ni\\u015fanta\\u015f\\u0131, the lively Caf\\u00e9 Society of Kad\\u0131k\\u00f6y and the football-loving streets of Be\\u015fikta\\u015f, it\'s easy to see why travelers say Istanbul isn\'t just one city, but many cities in one.\\r\\n\\r\\nSome essential points to visit in Istanbul include:\\r\\n\\r\\nSaint Sophia Museum\\/Church (Ayasofya): A building with architectural value and a religious place.\\r\\nDolmabah\\u00e7e Palace: Another building with architectural value.\\r\\nHistorical areas of Istanbul: Historical places that define the city.\\r\\nTopkapi Palace: A history museum.\\r\\nRumeli Fortress: A historical place and a museum.\\r\\nBasilica Cistern: Ancient ruins and a building with architectural value.\\r\\nSultanahmet District: A historic pedestrian area and neighborhood.\\r\\nKucuk Ayasofya Camii (Church of Saints Sergius and Bacchus): Another building with architectural value and a religious place.\\r\\nBeyoglu: An interesting neighborhood.\\r\\nGrand Bazaar: A flea and outdoor market to buy clothes and accessories.\\r\\n\\r\\nIstanbul, formerly known as Byzantium and Constantinople, has a rich and fascinating history spanning thousands of years. Here are some of the key periods that marked its evolution:\\r\\n\\r\\nOrigins in Byzantium: Founded in 667 BC. By Greek settlers from Megara, Byzantium was located along the Golden Horn, a deep, sheltered gulf. Over the centuries, it was occupied, rebuilt and passed through the hands of different empires.\\r\\nRoman Empire: In 191 BC, Rome recognized Byzantium as a free city. Later, in 330 AD, Emperor Constantine I transformed it into Constantinople, the capital of the Eastern Roman Empire (also known as the Byzantine Empire). During this period, iconic monuments such as the Saint Sophia Cathedral were built.\\r\\nOttoman Empire: In 1453, Constantinople fell to the Ottomans and became the capital of the Ottoman Empire. Although it was transformed into a Muslim city, its Christian influence persisted. The Ortak\\u00f6y Mosque and the Galata Tower are examples of its rich architecture.\\r\\nRepublic of Turkey: After World War I, Ottoman European territory shrank, and Ankara became the political capital. However, Istanbul remains a vital center for Turkey\'s industry, commerce and culture.\\r\\nToday, Istanbul is a vibrant city that combines its historical legacy with captivating modernity. Explore its streets, mosques and palaces to discover its fascinating history!\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1z-WYjzGHfryJGcGEmFR2fVKShAgyJOHv\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1K551DYJeCr_v6KDQfRiDK0fMwChTJghi\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1LztLuN4fIDQU_vwsdeV7x575_-7cB7jq\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1-7XDCyetQg1zPNG-D7Dokqfa5ngXq0G9\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1fpepMttPyEC3sAsG9rOZ9g-AeTjk5f6i\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1tFmu9OdzVAAiG_DYDNcccLpd_KXjjmTE\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 12:32:49', '2024-03-27 12:32:49'),
(193, 382, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/fR63iRhvDYc?si=N1JjKxJor8cUOTDI\",\"esdescription\":\"La Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo, fue una ciudad de la antigua Grecia ubicada en la regi\\u00f3n de Misia, al noroeste de Asia Menor (actual Turqu\\u00eda). Sus ruinas rodean la actual ciudad de Bergama, construida sobre los cimientos de lo que fue la parte baja de P\\u00e9rgamo. Aqu\\u00ed te presento algunos detalles interesantes sobre este sitio hist\\u00f3rico:\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n geogr\\u00e1fica: P\\u00e9rgamo se encuentra a 30 km de la costa del mar Egeo, frente a la isla de Lesbos, en la regi\\u00f3n llamada E\\u00f3lida. \\r\\n\\r\\n2. Historia: Se cree que P\\u00e9rgamo fue fundada por P\\u00e9rgamos, hijo de Neopt\\u00f3lemo y Andr\\u00f3maca, personajes de la guerra de Troya. Fue sede de una importante biblioteca, rivalizando con la famosa Biblioteca de Alejandr\\u00eda. Adem\\u00e1s, aqu\\u00ed se perfeccion\\u00f3 la t\\u00e9cnica de elaboraci\\u00f3n del pergamino. La Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo alberga los restos de varios edificios, como templos, teatros, cisternas y murallas. Es un testimonio fascinante de la antigua civilizaci\\u00f3n griega y su influencia perdura hasta hoy. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Dise\\u00f1o: La Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo, una maravilla de la arquitectura helen\\u00edstica, se alza majestuosamente sobre las pendientes del terreno que dieron lugar a una ciudad escalonada, pero para optimizar las vistas y el espacio de los edificios, se construyeron terrazas artificiales.\\r\\nLa ciudad estaba escalonada en tres niveles, esta obra de ingenier\\u00eda revolucion\\u00f3 la arquitectura de la \\u00e9poca al integrar la ciudad con su paisaje. \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Altura: La Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo se encuentra en la parte m\\u00e1s alta de la ciudad sobre el valle del r\\u00edo Selinus y tiene una altitud de 335 metros sobre el nivel del mar.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Patrimonio de la Humanidad: En 2014, la UNESCO design\\u00f3 a P\\u00e9rgamo como Patrimonio de la Humanidad debido a su relevancia hist\\u00f3rica y cultural.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Actualidad: Hoy en d\\u00eda aunque las ruinas est\\u00e1n en estado de deterioro, la Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo sigue siendo un lugar de asombro y admiraci\\u00f3n para los amantes de la historia y la arqueolog\\u00eda, solo contiene los restos de varios edificios.  El paseo por la Acr\\u00f3polis permite explorar varios espacios, como la fuente sagrada, las letrinas, el t\\u00fanel de la psicoterapia y los templos de Zeus, Atenea, Dem\\u00e9ter, Trajano y Esculapio. Tambi\\u00e9n ver\\u00e1s los restos de una arteria donde sol\\u00edan descansar numerosos comercios.\\r\\n\\r\\nEn resumen, la Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo es un testimonio de la grandeza griega y un recordatorio de la importancia de la sabidur\\u00eda, la curaci\\u00f3n y la preservaci\\u00f3n del conocimiento en la antig\\u00fcedad.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IDQGZ8MfVK-tuzqpP_jN_I2YU9K9PsHE\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/10LnQ0yv9RHLsQyYB8IRQT3x5VdyVZ4GW\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xd2Ktb2NM_eoPk_xRfjKWZ9zl4nYSyt6\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/18mjsoYkb1-DKPTXK7vpDygodOd7dKgcc\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i-WKbnJS-vinkVA54LA697fMsUHS9rMY\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YvPiIHVgfpgP_PlExJyQJlMSqrXXQs4x\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 14:37:48', '2024-04-01 15:31:52'),
(194, 383, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/15Ho2LQE7qQmEJR5WRFLFGareSdcDfbap\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Acropolis of Pergamon was a city of ancient Greece located in the region of Misia, northwest of Asia Minor (present-day Turkey). Its ruins surround the present city of Bergama, built on the foundations of what was the lower part of Pergamon. Here are some interesting details about this historical site:\\r\\n1. Geographical location: Pergamon is located 30 km off the coast of the Aegean Sea, opposite the island of Lesbos, in the region called Aeolian. \\r\\n\\r\\n2. History: It is believed that Pergamon was founded by Pergamos, son of Neoptolemus and Andromache, characters of the Trojan War. It was home to an important library, rivaling the famous Library of Alexandria. In addition, the technique of making parchment was perfected here. The Acropolis of Pergamon houses the remains of several buildings, such as temples, theaters, cisterns and walls. It is a fascinating testimony to ancient Greek civilization and its influence endures to this day. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Design: The Acropolis of Pergamon, a marvel of Hellenistic architecture, rises majestically on the slopes of the land that resulted in a stepped city, but to optimize views and building space, artificial terraces were constructed.\\r\\nThe city was staggered on three levels, this engineering work revolutionized the architecture of the time by integrating the city with its landscape. \\r\\n\\r\\n4. Height: The Acropolis of Pergamon is located in the highest part of the city on the valley of the Selinus River and has an altitude of 335 meters above sea level.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. World Heritage Site: In 2014, UNESCO designated Pergamon as a World Heritage Site due to its historical and cultural significance.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Present day: Today although the ruins are in a state of disrepair, the Acropolis of Pergamon remains a place of awe and admiration for lovers of history and archaeology, it contains only the remains of several buildings.  A walk through the Acropolis allows you to explore various spaces, such as the sacred fountain, the latrines, the psychotherapy tunnel and the temples of Zeus, Athena, Demeter, Trajan and Aesculapius. You will also see the remains of an artery where numerous stores used to rest.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn short, the Acropolis of Pergamon is a testament to Greek greatness and a reminder of the importance of wisdom, healing and the preservation of knowledge in antiquity.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1IDQGZ8MfVK-tuzqpP_jN_I2YU9K9PsHE\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/10LnQ0yv9RHLsQyYB8IRQT3x5VdyVZ4GW\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1xd2Ktb2NM_eoPk_xRfjKWZ9zl4nYSyt6\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/18mjsoYkb1-DKPTXK7vpDygodOd7dKgcc\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i-WKbnJS-vinkVA54LA697fMsUHS9rMY\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YvPiIHVgfpgP_PlExJyQJlMSqrXXQs4x\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 14:38:03', '2024-03-27 14:38:03'),
(195, 384, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/v9JX6c_pYnQ?si=YKK-a42ioZ2-mbrw\",\"esdescription\":\"El Teatro de P\\u00e9rgamo, tambi\\u00e9n conocido como el teatro m\\u00e1s empinado de la historia antigua, se encuentra en la ladera sur de la Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo. Este magn\\u00edfico teatro, con capacidad para 10,000 asientos y es considerado uno de los logros arquitect\\u00f3nicos m\\u00e1s impresionantes de la antigua Asia Menor que ha perdurado a lo largo de los siglos.\\r\\nAlgunos detalles interesantes sobre el Teatro de P\\u00e9rgamo son:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Historia y uso: El Teatro de P\\u00e9rgamo fue un lugar importante para eventos culturales, representaciones teatrales y competencias musicales. En su mejor \\u00e9poca, la ciudad de P\\u00e9rgamo lleg\\u00f3 a albergar hasta 60,000 habitantes. Adem\\u00e1s del teatro, P\\u00e9rgamo tambi\\u00e9n contaba con una biblioteca y un altar de Zeus.\\r\\nAntes de este teatro, se cree que hubo otro construido en el mismo lugar. Algunos restos de la muralla del antiguo teatro a\\u00fan son visibles hoy.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Construcci\\u00f3n y Dise\\u00f1o: Se cree que fue construido en el siglo III a.C., durante el reinado del rey Eumenes II, aunque se realizaron algunas adiciones durante la \\u00e9poca romana.\\r\\nSu dise\\u00f1o original data de la \\u00e9poca helen\\u00edstica. El teatro est\\u00e1 dividido en trece partes: siete en la parte inferior y seis en la parte superior. En la fila inferior, frente a la orquesta, se encuentra una logia real de m\\u00e1rmol. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Helenismo y Escenario: Durante la \\u00e9poca helen\\u00edstica, hab\\u00eda un escenario con una plataforma de madera desmontable y un edificio esc\\u00e9nico f\\u00e1cilmente desmontable.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Vistas impresionantes: La vista desde las filas superiores del teatro, construidas con piedra andesita, es simplemente impresionante. Desde all\\u00ed, los espectadores pod\\u00edan contemplar el paisaje circundante y disfrutar de las actuaciones en el escenario.\\r\\nEl teatro se encuentra a una altura de 36 metros y consta de 80 escalones de 74 cm de ancho desde la cima hasta el escenario, cuidadosamente construidos para facilitar la entrada y salida de los asientos\\r\\n\\r\\nEste teatro es un testimonio de la habilidad y creatividad de los antiguos arquitectos y sigue siendo un hito hist\\u00f3rico fascinante en la actualidad\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1XR--8NHZJKOkRiOnCgg1_HecC2fkMxoC\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i86vFxi80Rte-lsaXz74zyUm0HIVx_jH\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1KWzv4JDdZROP7BUpHeeZ3uafhMRftHIt\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/17S5yoHk0NNKMtYzmGO9uVblUgHqzI3i5\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1a3eafDslGPwm1gArBJ01a8_n538a1uE4\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qNjOM7bNu3yRHEoHFcewb7FdUTD1_m2u\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 15:05:34', '2024-04-02 11:53:42'),
(196, 385, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1RaZV4Jk6ZUvzCaG1wgik_bS7pq6FH5Nw\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Pergamon Theater, also known as the steepest theater in ancient history, is located on the southern slope of the Pergamon Acropolis. This magnificent theater seats 10,000 and is considered one of the most impressive architectural achievements of ancient Asia Minor that has endured through the centuries.\\r\\nSome interesting details about the Pergamon Theater are:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. history and use: the Pergamon Theater was an important venue for cultural events, theatrical performances and musical competitions. At its peak, the city of Pergamon housed up to 60,000 inhabitants. In addition to the theater, Pergamon also had a library and an altar of Zeus.\\r\\nBefore this theater, it is believed that there was another one built on the same site. Some remains of the wall of the ancient theater are still visible today.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Construction and Design: It is believed to have been built in the 3rd century B.C., during the reign of King Eumenes II, although some additions were made during Roman times.\\r\\nIts original design dates from the Hellenistic period. The theater is divided into thirteen parts: seven in the lower part and six in the upper part. In the lower row, in front of the orchestra, is a royal marble loggia. \\r\\n\\r\\n3. Hellenism and Stage: During the Hellenistic period, there was a stage with a removable wooden platform and an easily removable stage building.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Breathtaking Views: The view from the upper rows of the theater, built with andesite stone, is simply breathtaking. From there, spectators could contemplate the surrounding landscape and enjoy the performances on stage.\\r\\nThe theater stands at a height of 36 meters and consists of 80 steps of 74 cm wide from the top to the stage, carefully constructed to facilitate the entrance and exit of the seats.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis theater is a testament to the skill and creativity of ancient architects and remains a fascinating historical landmark today.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1XR--8NHZJKOkRiOnCgg1_HecC2fkMxoC\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1i86vFxi80Rte-lsaXz74zyUm0HIVx_jH\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1KWzv4JDdZROP7BUpHeeZ3uafhMRftHIt\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/17S5yoHk0NNKMtYzmGO9uVblUgHqzI3i5\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1a3eafDslGPwm1gArBJ01a8_n538a1uE4\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1qNjOM7bNu3yRHEoHFcewb7FdUTD1_m2u\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 15:08:22', '2024-03-27 15:08:22'),
(197, 386, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/lhsVDdIAJvs?si=vds_66EQe7av0eyp\",\"esdescription\":\"Las Termas Romanas de P\\u00e9rgamo son un testimonio fascinante de la vida cotidiana y la cultura en la antigua ciudad de P\\u00e9rgamo, ubicada en la regi\\u00f3n de Asia Menor (hoy en d\\u00eda parte de Turqu\\u00eda). Perm\\u00edteme compartir algunos detalles sobre estas termas:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Historia y Significado:\\r\\n- Las termas romanas eran espacios p\\u00fablicos esenciales en las ciudades romanas, utilizados para ba\\u00f1os, relajaci\\u00f3n y actividades sociales.\\r\\n- Las termas de P\\u00e9rgamo datan del siglo I d.C. y estuvieron en funcionamiento hasta bien entrado el siglo V d.C..\\r\\n- Estas termas eran un lugar donde los ciudadanos se reun\\u00edan, socializaban y se sumerg\\u00edan en el agua caliente para relajarse y limpiarse.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Caracter\\u00edsticas: Las termas romanas de P\\u00e9rgamo inclu\\u00edan \\u00e1reas como las siguientes: \\r\\n- Frigidarium: La piscina de agua fr\\u00eda.\\r\\n- Tepidarium: La sala de temperatura moderada.\\r\\n- Caldarium: La sala de agua caliente.\\r\\n- Apodyterium: Vestuarios y \\u00e1reas de cambio.\\r\\nLas paredes de las termas estaban decoradas con mosaicos, frescos y esculturas.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Descubrimientos Arqueol\\u00f3gicos:\\r\\n- Las excavaciones en el yacimiento han revelado edificios de piedra, abundantes restos de cer\\u00e1mica y objetos met\\u00e1licos, como f\\u00edbulas y fragmentos de armas.\\r\\n- Se cree que las legiones romanas podr\\u00edan haber utilizado este lugar, lo que sugiere su importancia militar.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Patrimonio Cultural: Las termas romanas de P\\u00e9rgamo son parte del paisaje cultural de estratos m\\u00faltiples que rodea la actual ciudad de Bergama. En 2014, la UNESCO design\\u00f3 a P\\u00e9rgamo como Patrimonio de la Humanidad, reconociendo su rica historia y su contribuci\\u00f3n a la cultura romana y helen\\u00edstica, incluyendo las termas romanas. \\r\\n\\r\\nEstas termas son una ventana al pasado, donde podemos imaginar a los antiguos romanos disfrutando de momentos de relajaci\\u00f3n y comunidad en medio de la majestuosidad de P\\u00e9rgamo\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WVDU7rgP2ndbx2aytaxeenkGg5katqys\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FiX1NGKLouoakxQKgh7FAPVG-xJdF3ap\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1XF5uUnv44uMjxqivEfCQ87oq9vCn3bJR\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1NjCCrJ_lYR2_q2l878-F7lrX9TH-Q_yX\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aJFTvIjpGHQxnQBLO58EKjzw66gsskqy\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1e4DhsQ-REx7WynAEXf-9MN6ZRGJUb8aV\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 15:23:20', '2024-04-02 11:29:18'),
(198, 387, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1_TuadfiLTjKq25P-tecnW3aMCr_0PxpO\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"The Roman Baths of Pergamon are a fascinating testimony of daily life and culture in the ancient city of Pergamon, located in the region of Asia Minor (now part of Turkey). Let me share some details about these baths:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. History and Significance:\\r\\n- Roman baths were essential public spaces in Roman cities, used for bathing, relaxation and social activities.\\r\\n- The Pergamon baths date back to the 1st century AD and were in operation well into the 5th century AD.\\r\\n- These baths were a place where citizens gathered, socialized and immersed themselves in the hot water to relax and cleanse themselves.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Characteristics: The Roman baths of Pergamon included areas such as the following: \\r\\n- Frigidarium: The cold water pool.\\r\\n- Tepidarium: The moderate temperature room.\\r\\n- Caldarium: The hot water room.\\r\\n- Apodyterium: Changing rooms and changing areas.\\r\\nThe walls of the baths were decorated with mosaics, frescoes and sculptures.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Archaeological Discoveries:\\r\\n- Excavations at the site have revealed stone buildings, abundant pottery remains and metal objects, such as fibulae and fragments of weapons.\\r\\n- It is believed that the Roman legions may have used this site, suggesting its military importance.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Cultural Heritage: The Roman baths of Pergamon are part of the multi-layered cultural landscape surrounding the present-day town of Bergama. In 2014, UNESCO designated Pergamon as a World Heritage Site, recognizing its rich history and contribution to Roman and Hellenistic culture, including the Roman baths. \\r\\n\\r\\nThese baths are a window to the past, where we can imagine the ancient Romans enjoying moments of relaxation and community amidst the majesty of Pergamon.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1WVDU7rgP2ndbx2aytaxeenkGg5katqys\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1FiX1NGKLouoakxQKgh7FAPVG-xJdF3ap\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1XF5uUnv44uMjxqivEfCQ87oq9vCn3bJR\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1NjCCrJ_lYR2_q2l878-F7lrX9TH-Q_yX\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1aJFTvIjpGHQxnQBLO58EKjzw66gsskqy\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1e4DhsQ-REx7WynAEXf-9MN6ZRGJUb8aV\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 15:31:55', '2024-03-27 15:31:55'),
(199, 202, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/kqN4xxeI3is?si=E4Kn2MxXjRTJi_Fj\",\"esdescription\":\"P\\u00e9rgamo, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Pergamon, fue una antigua ciudad Sirvi\\u00f3 como la capital del Imperio Lidio y destac\\u00f3 por su riqueza, cultura y ubicaci\\u00f3n estrat\\u00e9gica a lo largo de la ruta comercial central desde el mar Egeo hacia el interior de Anatolia.\\r\\nEsta majestuosa urbe fue famosa por varios logros notables:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Ubicaci\\u00f3n: Fue una antigua ciudad de la Grecia en la regi\\u00f3n de Misia, con una rica historia. Ubicada al noroeste de Asia Menor (actual Turqu\\u00eda). Se encuentra a unos 30 km de la costa del mar Egeo, frente a la isla de Lesbos. Las ruinas de P\\u00e9rgamo rodean la actual ciudad de Bergama, construida sobre los cimientos de lo que fue la parte baja de la antigua ciudad.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Historia y Fundaci\\u00f3n: La leyenda cuenta que P\\u00e9rgamo fue fundada por P\\u00e9rgamos, hijo de Neopt\\u00f3lemo y Andr\\u00f3maca, personajes de la guerra de Troya. A lo largo de su historia, P\\u00e9rgamo estuvo bajo el dominio de diferentes gobernantes, incluidos Creso, rey de Lidia, y Ciro II de Persia. M\\u00e1s tarde, Alejandro Magno la conquist\\u00f3 y la ciudad prosper\\u00f3 bajo el liderazgo de la dinast\\u00eda At\\u00e1lida. Sin embargo, el reino de P\\u00e9rgamo desapareci\\u00f3 cuando \\u00c1talo III leg\\u00f3 todo el territorio a la Rep\\u00fablica romana.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Importancia Hist\\u00f3rica: P\\u00e9rgamo presenci\\u00f3 m\\u00e1s de 3.500 a\\u00f1os de ocupaci\\u00f3n, alternando entre una pr\\u00f3spera ciudad internacional y una colecci\\u00f3n de modestas aldeas.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Acu\\u00f1aci\\u00f3n de Monedas: Los lidios acu\\u00f1aron las primeras monedas del mundo en P\\u00e9rgamo, a partir del a\\u00f1o 560 a.C..\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Descubrimientos Arqueol\\u00f3gicos: Aunque la arquitectura monumental antigua no se hab\\u00eda encontrado hasta hace poco, hay evidencia de que P\\u00e9rgamo pudo haber estado ocupada incluso durante el per\\u00edodo neol\\u00edtico.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Entre sus datos de inter\\u00e9s se encuentran: La Biblioteca de P\\u00e9rgamo, La T\\u00e9cnica del Pergamino (Aqu\\u00ed se perfeccion\\u00f3 la t\\u00e9cnica de elaboraci\\u00f3n del pergamino, un material utilizado para escribir y preservar textos), Acr\\u00f3polis de P\\u00e9rgamo (La ciudadela situada en la parte m\\u00e1s alta albergaba tesoros y monumentos significativos).\\r\\n\\r\\n7. Actualidad: Hoy en d\\u00eda, las ruinas de P\\u00e9rgamo siguen siendo un testimonio impresionante de su pasado glorioso, y su legado perdura como parte de la rica historia de la antigua Grecia y Anatolia. Por su importancia su importancia hist\\u00f3rica y cultural la UNESCO design\\u00f3 a P\\u00e9rgamo como Patrimonio de la Humanidad en 2014.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ibeOXDbJGqnHlkig1mvaVlp9Ya20aUzQ\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1829EjQ8AVJfbtKOUij9CZgX-QSa9cdHa\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1GI_1L0bAonVi47lRPy3yVJEGpH2-m4RN\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1AWpZaiuZQp442IN43NPS-1hvGeqeG9qk\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1bz11g4AFG5GncjBYLfYB4Jcv9CX7Pob7\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YvPiIHVgfpgP_PlExJyQJlMSqrXXQs4x\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 15:47:23', '2024-04-01 11:32:19'),
(200, 203, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/GD0g2nZEPMQ?si=KpF1exB0rw6KVkM4\",\"esdescription\":\"Pergamon, also known as Pergamon, was an ancient city that served as the capital of the Lydian Empire and was noted for its wealth, culture and strategic location along the central trade route from the Aegean Sea into the Anatolian hinterland.\\r\\nThis majestic city was famous for several notable achievements:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. location: it was an ancient Greek city in the region of Mysia, with a rich history. It is located in the northwest of Asia Minor (present-day Turkey). It is located about 30 km off the coast of the Aegean Sea, opposite the island of Lesbos. The ruins of Pergamon surround the present city of Bergama, built on the foundations of what was the lower part of the ancient city.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. History and Foundation: Legend has it that Pergamon was founded by Pergamos, son of Neoptolemus and Andromache, characters of the Trojan War. Throughout its history, Pergamon was under the rule of different rulers, including Croesus, king of Lydia, and Cyrus II of Persia. Later, Alexander the Great conquered it and the city prospered under the leadership of the Attalid dynasty. However, the kingdom of Pergamum disappeared when Attalus III bequeathed the entire territory to the Roman Republic.\\r\\n\\r\\n3. Historical Significance: Pergamon witnessed over 3,500 years of occupation, alternating between a thriving international city and a collection of modest villages.\\r\\n\\r\\n4. Coinage: The Lydians minted the world\'s first coins in Pergamon, beginning in 560 BC.\\r\\n\\r\\n5. Archaeological Discoveries: Although ancient monumental architecture had not been found until recently, there is evidence that Pergamon may have been occupied even during the Neolithic period.\\r\\n\\r\\n6. Among its interesting facts are: The Library of Pergamon, The Parchment Technique (Here the technique of making parchment, a material used for writing and preserving texts, was perfected), Acropolis of Pergamon (The citadel located at the highest part housed treasures and significant monuments).\\r\\n\\r\\n7. Present day: Today, the ruins of Pergamon remain an impressive testimony to its glorious past, and its legacy endures as part of the rich history of ancient Greece and Anatolia. Because of its historical and cultural importance, UNESCO designated Pergamon as a World Heritage Site in 2014.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1ibeOXDbJGqnHlkig1mvaVlp9Ya20aUzQ\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1829EjQ8AVJfbtKOUij9CZgX-QSa9cdHa\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1GI_1L0bAonVi47lRPy3yVJEGpH2-m4RN\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1AWpZaiuZQp442IN43NPS-1hvGeqeG9qk\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1bz11g4AFG5GncjBYLfYB4Jcv9CX7Pob7\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1YvPiIHVgfpgP_PlExJyQJlMSqrXXQs4x\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 15:48:04', '2024-04-01 10:36:18'),
(201, 242, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/-vUew32QCk8?si=dMYK6YIvqymD4is8\",\"esdescription\":\"Scala Nuova, tambi\\u00e9n conocida como Ku\\u015fadas\\u0131, es una ciudad portuaria en la costa del mar Egeo. Su historia se remonta a la antigua ciudad de \\u00c9feso, que sol\\u00eda tener un puerto muy activo en la Antig\\u00fcedad. \\u00c9feso era uno de los principales centros comerciales del mundo romano, y su puerto se consideraba \\u201cla puerta por la que Occidente visitaba Oriente\\u201d debido a su papel crucial en el comercio entre Italia, Grecia y Asia Menor.\\r\\n\\r\\nSin embargo, con el tiempo, los dep\\u00f3sitos del antiguo r\\u00edo Caystros (K\\u00fc\\u00e7\\u00fck Menderes) llenaron gradualmente el puerto de \\u00c9feso, y la ciudad cay\\u00f3 en declive. En lugar de establecerse nuevamente en \\u00c9feso, los habitantes encontraron una nueva ciudad portuaria y la llamaron Scala Nuova, que significa \\u201cnuevo puerto\\u201d en italiano. Hoy en d\\u00eda, Scala Nuova (Ku\\u015fadas\\u0131) sigue siendo uno de los puertos mediterr\\u00e1neos m\\u00e1s populares y es un lugar vibrante con una rica cultura, gastronom\\u00eda y un continuo flujo de mercanc\\u00edas.\\r\\n\\r\\nLas ciudades puerto, como Scala Nuova, no solo son espacios de intercambio econ\\u00f3mico, sino tambi\\u00e9n cultural y social. Su ubicaci\\u00f3n junto al agua las convierte en lugares estrat\\u00e9gicos con gran potencial econ\\u00f3mico. Sin embargo, es importante planificar y construir estas ciudades de manera integral para lograr un equilibrio positivo entre el puerto y la ciudad, asegurando un crecimiento econ\\u00f3mico sostenible y beneficios para todos los ciudadanos\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1JyWTZru8Km-n2u6N4_HKHpXrw_OYtGDW\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1QqjoMGOC8RunMN7JlM3EX8RimOfpe5OK\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Q6Xc_m48Glb8pe-5-iuIh-p82SzrAetf\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1JSdrgmLpERTq5ejZ4dgRxRYgwGja5-41\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CnWpolICAl4QfQr1woAh5bKB7UF262_1\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/17G8_HsQeFX--EqMgfU1CszJR6CNfuJOV\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 16:08:32', '2024-04-02 12:01:43'),
(202, 243, '{\"esvideo\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1I6emN8y6LVuT_WPGmjBN9EOaHj4dnJXa\\/preview\",\"esdescription\":\"Scala Nuova, also known as Ku\\u015fadas\\u0131, is a port city on the coast of the Aegean Sea. Its history dates back to the ancient city of Ephesus, which used to have a busy port in ancient times. Ephesus was one of the main commercial centers of the Roman world, and its port was considered \\u201cthe gateway through which the West visited the East\\u201d due to its crucial role in trade between Italy, Greece and Asia Minor.\\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, over time, deposits from the ancient Caystros River (K\\u00fc\\u00e7\\u00fck Menderes) gradually filled the port of Ephesus, and the city fell into decline. Instead of settling back in Ephesus, the inhabitants found a new port city and named it Scala Nuova, which means \\u201cnew port\\u201d in Italian. Today, Scala Nuova (Ku\\u015fadas\\u0131) remains one of the most popular Mediterranean ports and is a vibrant place with rich culture, gastronomy and a continuous flow of goods.\\r\\n\\r\\nPort cities, like Scala Nuova, are not only spaces of economic exchange, but also cultural and social. Their location next to the water makes them strategic places with great economic potential. However, it is important to plan and build these cities comprehensively to achieve a positive balance between the port and the city, ensuring sustainable economic growth and benefits for all citizens.\",\"img1\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1JyWTZru8Km-n2u6N4_HKHpXrw_OYtGDW\\/preview\",\"img2\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1QqjoMGOC8RunMN7JlM3EX8RimOfpe5OK\\/preview\",\"img3\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1Q6Xc_m48Glb8pe-5-iuIh-p82SzrAetf\\/preview\",\"img4\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1JSdrgmLpERTq5ejZ4dgRxRYgwGja5-41\\/preview\",\"img5\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/1CnWpolICAl4QfQr1woAh5bKB7UF262_1\\/preview\",\"img6\":\"https:\\/\\/drive.google.com\\/file\\/d\\/17G8_HsQeFX--EqMgfU1CszJR6CNfuJOV\\/preview\"}', '2024-03-27 16:08:55', '2024-03-27 16:08:55'),
